ion': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/72b3ac1b-1293-5ce4-9b94-23549f992aca', 'word_count': 892, 'uri': 'nyt://article/72b3ac1b-1293-5ce4-9b94-23549f992aca'}, {'abstract': 'Bernard Tomic appears to hold the ball on his strings for an extra instant, furthering the perception that he is seeing and sensing the action earlier than other players.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/06/30/breaking-down-tomics-game/', 'snippet': 'Bernard Tomic appears to hold the ball on his strings for an extra instant, furthering the perception that he is seeing and sensing the action earlier than other players.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Watching Bernard Tomic’s masterful 7-6 (7), 5-7, 7-5, 7-6 (5) victory over Richard Gasquet in Wimbledon’s third round, I was struck by how much time the young Australian seems to have. Although he is 6-foot-5 and powerfully built, Tomic never appears hurried as he moves with a grace and efficiency reminiscent of Miloslav Mecir, who was nicknamed the Big Cat back in the late 1980s. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Breaking Down Tomic’s Game', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wimbledon Tennis Tournament', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Anderson, Kevin', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Berdych, Tomas', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gasquet, Richard', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tomic, Bernard', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-01T03:50:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Geoff Macdonald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Geoff', 'middlename': None, '