ous positions she had taken in cases where her record was already well known. But she did offer one tidbit worth repeating.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Weekend Gun Report: June 28-30, 2013', 'kicker': 'Joe Nocera', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-01T15:41:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joe Nocera', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nocera', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/435761a3-7bf0-5c9d-82f6-80c774a9d2ca', 'word_count': 2824, 'uri': 'nyt://article/435761a3-7bf0-5c9d-82f6-80c774a9d2ca'}, {'abstract': 'The press’s one-sided abortion coverage, yet again.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/01/the-media-and-wendy-davis/', 'snippet': 'The press’s one-sided abortion coverage, yet again.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With a tip of the hat to the Weekly Standard’s John McCormack, who tweeted about the interview over the weekend, here are the six questions asked of Texas State Senator Wendy Davis on ABC’s This Week\xa0after her (temporarily) successful filibuster of a bill that restricted abortion after 20 weeks of pregnancy and increased regulations on Texan abortion clinics:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Media and Wendy Davis', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ABORTION', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Filibusters and Debate Curbs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gun Control', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Newtown, Conn, Shooting (2012)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Davis, Wendy', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gosnell, Kermit', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-01T15:57:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/49e84263-c92b-5c19-b80a-1cf73b713c3c', 'word_count': 542, 'uri': 'nyt://article/49e84263-c92b-5c19-b80a-1cf73b713c3c'}, {'abstract': 'The laws surrounding pregnancy and family leave vary, so until the government finances parental leave, women need to know their rights so they can make informed decisions about their jobs.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/01/when-youre-the-first-pregnant-woman-at-your-company/', 'snippet': 'The laws surrounding pregnancy and family leave vary, so until the government finances parental leave, women need to know their rights so they can make informed decisions about their jobs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I never felt like a “woman in the workplace” until I became pregnant. My gender was not an issue in terms of my pay or general treatment. At a boozy after-work hangout, I discovered I made more than a male co-worker with the same title, because I had asked for and received raises where he had not. If you had asked me what being pregnant at work might be like before I conceived, I probably would have said something glib along the lines of: “I’m not the first working woman to ever have a kid. Haven’t they figured it out by now?”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When You’re the First Pregnant Woman at Your Company', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Careers and Professions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Family Leaves', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pregnancy and Childbirth', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-01T15:57:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jessica Grose', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jessica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grose', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a910bd6f-010e-5eff-a62c-c157aab8cd3d', 'word_count': 906, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a910bd6f-010e-5eff-a62c-c157aab8cd3d'}, {'abstract': 'Can you crack this camel challenge from the 2012 Dutch Mathematical Olympiad?', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/01/amsterdam/', 'snippet': 'Can you crack this camel challenge from the 2012 Dutch Mathematical Olympiad?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last week’s Odd Dice was one of the easier puzzles we’ve featured on Numberplay. It was meant to be that way — the challenge was offered to introduce the Dutch Mathematical Olympiad, an annual competition open to all high school students in the Netherlands. Last week’s puzzle was from Round One of the 2009 event; this week we move onto the Tweede Ronde of the competition with a camel distribution problem and a verbal arithmetic bonus from 2012.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Camels in Amsterdam', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mathematics', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Puzzles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AMSTERDAM (NETHERLANDS)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-01T16:00:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gary Antonick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Antonick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8d90ae94-6a33-5cb7-b8d2-ad9dea8a6451', 'word_count': 534, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8d90ae94-6a33-5cb7-b8d2-ad9dea8a6451'}, {'abstract': 'Chad Ackley joined Doner, Southfield, Mich., part of MDC Partners, as executive vice president and executive creative director.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/01/business/accounts-and-people-of-note-in-the-ad-industry.html', 'snippet': 'Chad Ackley joined Doner, Southfield, Mich., part of MDC Partners, as executive vice president and executive creative director.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Chad Ackley joined Doner, Southfield, Mich., part of MDC Partners, as executive vice president and executive creative director. He succeeds Murray White, who left, the agency said, to return to his hometown of Melbourne, Australia. Mr. Ackley had most recently been group creative director at the San Francisco office of DDB West, part of the DDB Worldwide division of the Omnicom Group. Also at Doner, Karen Cathel and Sam Sefton, creative directors, were promoted to senior vice presidents and group creative directors.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Accounts and People