ualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6702681d-a6be-5587-8ec3-e20a03decd50', 'word_count': 848, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6702681d-a6be-5587-8ec3-e20a03decd50'}, {'abstract': 'An exhibition at the Santa Barbara Museum of Art highlights handmade techniques in contemporary art.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/01/on-view-where-arts-and-crafts-are-one/', 'snippet': 'An exhibition at the Santa Barbara Museum of Art highlights handmade techniques in contemporary art.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The latest example of the current preoccupation with the authentic and the handmade is “Labour and Wait,” an ambitious exhibition that opens tomorrow at the Santa Barbara Museum of Art that presents work by 16 artists who use traditional craft techniques. “Craft” has often been shunned in the realm of the fine arts — perceived as being too homespun and perhaps too useful — but, as the exhibition shows, younger artists have begun embracing its materials, tools and techniques. “The exhibition’s title comes from ‘A Psalm of Life,’ a poem by Henry Wadsworth Longfellow,” explained the show’s curator, Julie Joyce. “It alludes to self-reliance and shows how these artists generally make their work themselves rather than having it manufactured.”', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Where Arts and Crafts Are One', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'On View: Lifting a Finger', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Santa Barbara Museum', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-01T21:56:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooke Hodge', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooke', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hodge', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dd56a0aa-870e-5e37-b661-a0da1c51b3e8', 'word_count': 365, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dd56a0aa-870e-5e37-b661-a0da1c51b3e8'}, {'abstract': 'All of the documentaries in ESPN’s “Nine for IX” are devoted to women in the sports world, starting with the tennis star Venus Williams.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/02/arts/television/espns-nine-for-ix-focuses-on-women-in-sports.html', 'snippet': 'All of the documentaries in ESPN’s “Nine for IX” are devoted to women in the sports world, starting with the tennis star Venus Williams.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Title IX, the 1972 legislation banning sex discrimination in federally financed education, may have revolutionized women’s sports in America, but sports journalism hasn’t exactly kept up. Hence the existence of the new documentary series “Nine for IX,” beginning on Tuesday on ESPN.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/02/arts/nine1/nine1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 378, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/02/arts/nine1/nine1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 378}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/02/arts/nine1/nine1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 644, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/02/arts/nine1/nine1-superJumbo.jpg