Uighurs it said had trained in Syria.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/03/world/asia/china-increases-security-in-restive-region.html', 'snippet': 'The government has issued arrest warrants after clashes that left dozens dead in the Xinjiang region. A newspaper linked the rise in violence to Uighurs it said had trained in Syria.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — The Chinese authorities tightened their grip on Tuesday on the far western region of Xinjiang, where two clashes left dozens dead last week, by confiscating knives and offering rewards for information about possible separatist attacks, according to the state news media. The police also issued arrest warrants for 11 people said to be wanted for murder, bombings and other acts of violence.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Beijing Increases Security in Xinjiang', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Beijing Increases Security In Xinjiang', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Uighurs (Chinese Ethnic Group)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jacobs, Andrew', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Global Times', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'East Turkestan Islamic Movement', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-02T15:20:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Jacobs and Chris Buckley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jacobs', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Chris', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Buckley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f70731dd-c531-53cd-8ad3-821047cafed2', 'word_count': 660, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f70731dd-c531-53cd-8ad3-821047cafed2'}, {'abstract': 'Ms. Midler on her performance as the Hollywood agent Sue Mengers. “Let’s not talk about the fricking wigs.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/02/bette-midler-hangs-up-the-phone-on-eat-you-last/', 'snippet': 'Ms. Midler on her performance as the Hollywood agent Sue Mengers. “Let’s not talk about the fricking wigs.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bette Midler wrapped up her Broadway run on Sunday in “I’ll Eat You Last,” her hit play about the Hollywood agent Sue Mengers, and by Monday she was starting to decompress. Her voice was strained after three months of chain-smoking herbal cigarettes as the nicotine-and-pot-loving Mengers, but otherwise she seemed hardy during a telephone interview about her first role on Broadway in some 40 years. There was little bitterness, for instance, at the two-dozen theater insiders who declined to nominate her for a Tony Award for best actress for “I’ll Eat You Last” – a decision that was one of the biggest surprises of the theater season.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/02/arts/02artsbeat-bette/02artsbeat-bette-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 433, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/02/arts/02artsbeat-bette/02artsbeat-bette-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 433}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/02/arts/02artsbeat-bette/02artsbeat-bette-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 738, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {