oduct in 1986 to nearly 17 percent in 2009.', 'lead_paragraph': ' As I write in a magazine column this week, mental illness costs the country a lot of money, primarily in indirect costs like lost worker productivity and increased use of the social safety net. That is one argument for why it might be cost-effective to increase national spending on the direct costs of treatment (like therapy, drugs, etc.).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Most U.S. Health Spending Is Exploding — but Not for Mental Health', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mental Health and Disorders', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Health Affairs (Journal)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Institute of Mental Health', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-02T18:38:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/823cb8f1-1928-5763-952f-94a88467e9a4', 'word_count': 505, 'uri': 'nyt://article/823cb8f1-1928-5763-952f-94a88467e9a4'}, {'abstract': 'The private equity-backed education company has filed for Chapter 11 bankruptcy protection as part of an effort to shrink its $5.8 billion debt load.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/07/02/cengage-learning-files-for-b