 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-03T01:02:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Hicks', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hicks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/99dcecd8-39d5-5ef3-8003-aafb0c40e9a7', 'word_count': 914, 'uri': 'nyt://article/99dcecd8-39d5-5ef3-8003-aafb0c40e9a7'}, {'abstract': 'Egypt has a dilemma: its politics are dominated by democrats who are not liberals and liberals who are not democrats.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/03/opinion/in-egypt-democrats-vs-liberals.html', 'snippet': 'Egypt has a dilemma: its politics are dominated by democrats who are not liberals and liberals who are not democrats.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — MILLIONS of Egyptians have spoken: President Mohamed Morsi, elected a year ago, has failed. With vast throngs calling for his ouster and demanding new elections, the army has given him an ultimatum: satisfy the crowds by Wednesday evening or face military intervention. How could this happen, two years after the military helped demonstrators rid Egypt of the autocrat Hosni Mubarak? ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Egypt, Democrats vs. Liberals', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Egypt, Democrats Vs. Liberals', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morsi, Mohamed', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Muslim Brotherhood (Egypt)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-03T01:03:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Samer S. Shehata', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Samer', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Shehata', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/59861561-6daf-5eb6-862c-ef98657766f5', 'word_count': 741, 'uri': 'nyt://article/59861561-6daf-5eb6-862c-ef98657766f5'}, {'abstract': 'The Islanders finally gave up on goalie Rick DiPietro, who was sidelined by numerous injuries, placing him on waivers and ending a close relationship with the team owner Charles Wang.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/03/sports/hockey/for-dipietro-and-islanders-owner-a-bond-is-broken.html', 'snippet': 'The Islanders finally gave up on goalie Rick DiPietro, who was sidelined by numerous injuries, placing him on waivers and ending a close relationship with the team owner Charles Wang.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The 15-year, $67.5 million contract that goalie Rick DiPietro signed with the Islanders in 2006 was called a lifetime contract, but in the years that followed, as DiPietro was sidelined by injury after injury, it was derided as an albatross. Still, the Islanders stuck by DiPietro, refusing to cut him loose for a combination of reasons, not least among them the close relationship between DiPietro and the team owner, Charles Wang.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For DiPietro and Islanders’ Owner, a Bond Is Broken', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'For DiPietro and Islanders’ Owner, a Bond Is Broken', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Islanders', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dipietro, Rick', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wang, Charles B', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hockey, Ice', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Free Agents (Sports)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-03T01:03:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Hockey', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Z. Klein and Allan Kreda', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': 'Z.', 'lastname': 'Klein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kreda', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b40df906-f5db-56b4-a4f6-da81e3bb3f37', 'word_count': 964, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b40df906-f5db-56b4-a4f6-da81e3bb3f37'}, {'abstract': 'Preventing terror doesn’t require violating privacy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/03/opinion/data-mining-without-big-brother.html', 'snippet': 'Preventing terror doesn’t require violating privacy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN the wake of revelations about the National Security Agency’s surveillance programs, President Obama has acknowledged the imperative to balance privacy and security. But so far, his administration’s defense of the programs has failed to assure the public that this balance has been achieved — or that basic privacy rights and civil liberties are being protected.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Data Mining, Without Big Brother', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributors', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Data Mining, Without Big Brother', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'or