t': 'The ballots will be available at 60 polling sites in Queens, nearly two years after the federal government ordered the city to provide assistance to South Asian minorities under a provision of the 1965 Voting Rights Act.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/03/nyregion/south-asians-in-queens-to-get-ballots-in-bengali.html', 'snippet': 'The ballots will be available at 60 polling sites in Queens, nearly two years after the federal government ordered the city to provide assistance to South Asian minorities under a provision of the 1965 Voting Rights Act.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When New York voters go to the polls for the primary on Sept. 10, South Asians in Queens will for the first time find ballots translated into Bengali, the first new language to be introduced at city polling booths in more than a decade, election officials said. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'South Asians in Queens to Get Ballots in Bengali', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'South Asians In Queens To Get Ballots In Bengali', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hu, Winnie', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Voting and Voters', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Board of Elections (NYS)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Queens (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Asian-Americans', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Voting Rights Act (1965)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Korean-Americans', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Primaries and Caucuses', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Language and Languages', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-03T02:26:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Winnie Hu', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Winnie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hu', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bf8ca6de-7f01-5123-98bb-151adc96d35b', 'word_count': 648, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bf8ca6de-7f01-5123-98bb-151adc96d35b'}, {'abstract': 'A St. Louis County judge reinstated Adam Paul as mayor of Ellisville on Monday, nearly three months after the City Council removed him in an acrimonious and personal dispute.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/03/us/missouri-judge-reinstates-mayor.html', 'snippet': 'A St. Louis County judge reinstated Adam Paul as mayor of Ellisville on Monday, nearly three months after the City Council removed him in an acrimonious and personal dispute.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A St. Louis County judge reinstated Adam Paul as mayor of Ellisville on Monday, nearly three months after the City Council removed him in an acrimonious and personal dispute. Judge David Lee Vincent III nullified the Council’s impeachment proceeding, saying it violated Mr. Paul’s due process rights by not giving him enough time to prepare a defense to charges that he had failed to respond to a subpoena. “The irony is that the old Council accused Adam Paul of violating the City Charter and they used that as the basis to remove him from the office of mayor,” said Chet Pleban, Mr. Paul’s lawyer. “Ironically, he was restored to the office of mayor because the Council violated the City Charter.” Mr. Paul was a political novice who came to power on the back of a campaign against providing tax incentives for a developer to build a Walmart in Ellisville. Mr. Paul claimed that the Council had it in for him because of his opposition to the Walmart, while council members accused him of being brash, disrespectful and violating rules. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Missouri: Judge Reinstates Mayor', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Midwest', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Missouri: Judge Reinstates Mayor', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Missouri', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ellisville (Mo)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'City Councils', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Impeachment', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paul, Adam', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-03T02:32:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Eligon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eligon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/a52335b5-aea3-534d-8aad-2617a4a80085', 'word_count': 179, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a52335b5-aea3-534d-8aad-2617a4a80085'}, {'abstract': 'Shares in Suntory Beverage and Food gained modestly in their debut in Tokyo on Wednesday despite the recent shaky response to new offerings in markets around the world.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/07/02/suntorys-4-billion-i-p-o-debuts-in-tokyo/', 'snippet': 'Shares in Suntory Beverage and Food gained modestly in their debut in Tokyo on Wednesday despite the recent shaky response to new offerings in markets around the world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HONG KONG — Shares in Suntory Beverage and Food rose on Wednesday in the closely watched trading debut of the biggest new share offering in Asia this year. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Suntory Unit’s $4 Billion I.P.O. Rises in Tokyo Trading Debut', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Initial Public Offerings', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ABE, SHINZO', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Suntory Ltd', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-03T02:34:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Gough', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gough', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0ba68020-7bb2-5375-bb72-a74a50c6d9af', 'word_count': 315, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0ba68020-7bb2-5375-bb72-a74a50c6d9af'}, {'abstract': 'Twenty-one people were charged Tuesday after the Georgia Bureau of Investigation said it found widespread abuse of patients at a specialized care facility northeast of Atlanta.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/03/us/georgia-21-charged-after-inquiry-finds-alzheimers-patients-were-abused.html', 'snippet': 'Twenty-one people were charged Tuesday after the Georgia Bureau of Investigation said it found widespread abuse of patients at a specialized care facility northeast of Atlanta.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Twenty-one people were charged Tuesday after the Georgia Bureau of Investigation said it found widespread abuse of patients at a specialized care facility northeast of Atlanta. The bureau said its review had found that employees at Alzheimer’s Care of Commerce had struck patients, delayed changing diapers and used bedsheets as restraining devices. The inquiry also uncovered that people with felony convictions were involved in patient care, a violation of state regulations. The authorities filed more than 70 charges against current and former facility employees. The center’s owner, Donna Wright, was among those charged. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Georgia: 21 Charged After Inquiry Finds Alzheimer’s Patients Were Abused', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | South', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Georgia: 21 Charged After Inquiry Finds Alzheimer’s Patients Were Abused', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Georgia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Alzheimer's Disease", 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Atlanta (Ga)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elderly', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elder Abuse', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Abuse of the Disabled', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-03T02:36:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alan Blinder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Blinder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/7727ea88-e6d2-59ba-9db6-8ea35ee1096a', 'word_count': 93, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7727ea88-e6d2-59ba-9db6-8ea35ee1096a'}, {'abstract': 'Although the White House is delaying a provision requiring employers to offer health coverage or pay a penalty, the key aspects of the Affordable Care Act should be little affected, an economist writes.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/02/putting-off-the-employer-mandate/', 'snippet': 'Although the White House is delaying a provision requiring employers to offer health coverage or pay a penalty, the key aspects of the Affordable Care Act should be little affected, an economist writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jared Bernstein is a senior fellow at the Center on Budget and Policy Priorities in Washington and a former chief economist to Vice President Joseph R. Biden Jr.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Putting Off the Employer Mandate', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act (2010)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reform and Reorganization', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-03T02:39:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jared Bernstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jared', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bernstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c36a3c62-4381-5d75-8f7e-45ac5b75c5ef', 'word_count': 717, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c36a3c62-4381-5d75-8f7e-45ac5b75c5ef'}, {'abstract': 'Alex Rodriguez began his minor league rehabilitation assignment Tuesday night in Charleston, S.C., but his future remains shrouded in question and controversy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/03/sports/baseball/clock-starts-for-rodriguez-a-new-riverdog.html', 'snippet': 'Alex Rodriguez began his minor league rehabilitation assignment Tuesday night in Charleston, S.C., but his future remains shrouded in question and controversy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CHARLESTON, S.C. — Alex Rodriguez came to this damp, breezy city to begin his latest comeback, hoping 