ndergan Grimes?” Answers include: “Left wing mime” and “Not ready for prime time.” It is catchy only in the sense that it is grating and looks like an ad your grandfather, trying far too hard to seem hip, might make for your High School student council race.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Is This the Worst Ever Political Ad?', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-07-03T18:04:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Juliet Lapidos', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Juliet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lapidos', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/64033313-dba8-51b7-95e0-15a4505bd40f', 'word_count': 89, 'uri': 'nyt://article/64033313-dba8-51b7-95e0-15a4505bd40f'}, {'abstract': 'People living in the Ganges Delta have adapted genetically to the ancient scourge through variations in some 300 genes, researchers say.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/04/health/gene-sleuths-find-how-some-naturally-resist-cholera.html', 'snippet': 'People living in the Ganges Delta have adapted genetically to the ancient scourge through variations in some 300 genes, researchers say.', 'lead_paragraph': 'People living in the Ganges Delta, where cholera is an ancient, endemic and often lethal disease, have adapted genetically to the scourge through variations in about 300 genes, say researchers who have scanned their genomes for the fingerprints of evolution.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/04/science/04cholera/04cholera-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarg