o document their long, medicalized ends.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Death has been a great literary theme for so long you might think there’d be little left to say on the subject, but in recent decades the literature of death has taken an interesting and novel turn. Writers are recording their own deaths as they happen.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/07/books/review/0707-bks-ORourke/0707-bks-ORourke-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 293, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/07/books/review/0707-bks-ORourke/0707-bks-ORourke-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 293}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/07/books/review/0707-bks-ORourke/0707-bks-ORourke-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 501, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/07/books/review/0707-bks-ORourke/0707-bks-ORourke-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1002, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/07/books/review/0707-bks-ORourke/0707-bks-ORourke-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/07/books/review/0707-bks-ORourke/0707-bks-ORourke-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/07/books/review/0707-bks-ORourke/0707-bks-ORourke-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Deadlines', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Deadlines', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Death and Dying', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-03T18:53:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': "By Meghan O'Rourke", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Meghan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "O'Rourke", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9ec2c75a-db9e-53c0-ba15-684067bda349', 'word_count': 2790, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9ec2c75a-db9e-53c0-ba15-684067bda349'}, {'abstract': 'The stories of troubled young women who engaged in Internet prostitution and turned up dead on Long Island.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/07/books/review/lost-girls-by-robert-kolker.html', 'snippet': 'The stories of troubled young women who engaged in Internet prostitution and turned up dead on Long Island.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In mid-December 2010, the Suffolk County police discovered the bodies of four women, each wrapped in burlap, on a desolate, bramble-covered stretch of sand called Gilgo Beach. The corpses were later identified as those of four prostitutes, Melissa Barthelemy, Megan Waterman, Maureen Brainard-Barnes and Amber Lynn Overstreet Costello. The work was believed to be that of a serial killer, and for several months to come, the media circus that accompanied the official investigation — centering on the search for another prostitute, Shannan Gilbert, who had disappeared after a nearby assignation the previous May — kept much of the New York area and the nation entranced. It was a gothic whodunit for the Internet age, replete with prostitutes, drugs, family dysfunction, investigative incompetence, not to mention a strange, insular beach community and, of course, the Web sites of Craigslist and Backpage, where the women had advertised for customers. Robert Kolker, who wrote about the murders for New York magazine in 2011, has produced in “Lost Girls” a compelling, nearly unputdownable narrative of the case and its attendant issues; a horrific, cautionary tale that makes for a very different type of beach read.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/07/books/review/0707-bks-SWARTZ/0707-bks-SWARTZ-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/07/books/review/0707-bks-SWARTZ/0707-bks-SWARTZ-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/07/books/review/0707-bks-SWARTZ/0707-bks-SWARTZ-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/07/books/review/0707-bks-SWARTZ/0707-bks-SWARTZ-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1409, 'width': 1409, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/07/books/review/0707-bks-SWARTZ/0707-bks-SWARTZ-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/07/books/review/0707-bks-SWARTZ/0707-bks-SWARTZ-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/07/books/review/0707-bks-SWARTZ/0707-bks-SWARTZ-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Gone Girls', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Gone Girls', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Swartz, Mimi', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kolker, Robert', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prostitution', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-03T18:53:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mimi Swartz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mimi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Swartz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/67165346-95a0-5789-955a-d01831242112', 'word_count': 1292, 'uri': 'nyt://article/67165346-95a0-5789-955a-d01831242112'}, {'abstract': 'How a single Union regiment saved the day at the Battle of Gettysburg, but at an enormous cost.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/03/the-sacrifice-of-the-second-wisconsin/', 'snippet': 'How a single Union regiment saved the day at the Battle of Gettysburg, but at an enormous cost.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On April 22, 1861, Philo Wright, a 19-year-old schoolteacher living in the far southwest corner of Wisconsin, answered President Lincoln’s call for volunteers and joined a budding local unit called the Grant County Grays. His life suddenly began to change rapidly: a month later the unit became Company C of the Second Wisconsin Volunteer Regiment, and a month after that the Second Wisconsin became the first three-year regiment to reach Washington. By July, three months after leaving his small-town classroom behind, Wright was fighting at the First Battle of Bull Run in a brigade commanded by William Tecumseh Sherman.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Sacrifice of the Second Wisconsin', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Army of the Potomac', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Gettysburg (Pa)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Wisconsin', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-03T18:56:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Kirschner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kirschner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e70f72fb-90b9-5c07-8638-e46fd3aa7fd5', 'word_count': 1822, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e70f72fb-90b9-5c07-8638-e46fd3aa7fd5'}, {'abstract': 'Dr. Engelbart’s 1968 demonstration of interactive computing astounded scientists and the technology was later refined for Apple and Microsoft.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/04/technology/douglas-c-engelbart-inventor-of-the-computer-mouse-dies-at-88.html', 'snippet': 'Dr. Engelbart’s 1968 demonstration of interactive computing astounded scientists and the technology was later refined for Apple and Microsoft.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Douglas C. Engelbart was 25, just engaged to be married and thi