: 'organizations', 'value': 'Mead Johnson Nutrition Company', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-03T21:33:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Wong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0699c1fd-8a57-5efb-8134-419a94ec6f29', 'word_count': 580, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0699c1fd-8a57-5efb-8134-419a94ec6f29'}, {'abstract': 'Find the Lincoln Highway in Times Square and you can drive all the way to San Francisco. Go for a long ride as the highway celebrates its 100th birthday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/07/travel/cross-country-by-a-road-less-traveled.html', 'snippet': 'Find the Lincoln Highway in Times Square and you can drive all the way to San Francisco. Go for a long ride as the highway celebrates its 100th birthday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hidden amid the flash of 42nd Street in Manhattan, like a tarnished penny on the floor of a nightclub, is a small brown sign bearing the words “Lincoln Highway.” To those who notice it at all, it’s a random curiosity, a beacon without context. It betrays no indication that it points the way to a road that once stretched across the continent, one that traced the trails forged by early pioneers and augured the future of car travel in this country. ', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/07/travel/07LINCOLN_SPAN-sf/07LINCOLN_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 494, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/07/travel/07LINCOLN_SPAN-sf/07LINCOLN_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlarge