rson': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Werdigier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3ba5d24b-9a55-521a-944f-e93d5a7c0da1', 'word_count': 207, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3ba5d24b-9a55-521a-944f-e93d5a7c0da1'}, {'abstract': 'Mario Draghi, the bank’s president, said on Thursday that interest rates will stay low “for an extended period of time.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/05/business/global/european-central-bank-leaves-interest-rates-at-record-low.html', 'snippet': 'Mario Draghi, the bank’s president, said on Thursday that interest rates will stay low “for an extended period of time.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'FRANKFURT — The European Central Bank said Thursday it would keep interest rates low “for an extended period of time,” an unprecedented commitment for an institution that had steadfastly refused to offer guidance on its future policy.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'European Central Bank Commits to Low Rate', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'European Central Bank to Keep Rates Low for ‘Extended Period’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interest Rates', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Euro (Currency)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Central Bank', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Draghi, Mario', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-04T11:52:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Ewing', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ewing', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab6594e3-683b-5a23-b396-64e72711a416', 'word_count': 672, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab6594e3-683b-5a23-b396-64e72711a416'}, {'abstract': 'Scientists have long wondered how much life can be stripped down and still remain alive. The answer seems to be that the true essence of life is not some handful of genes, but coexistence.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/04/science/how-simple-can-life-get-its-complicated.html', 'snippet': 'Scientists have long wondered how much life can be stripped down and still remain alive. The answer seems to be that the true essence of life is not some handful of genes, but coexistence.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the pageant of life, we are genetically bloated. The human genome contains around 20,000 protein-coding genes. Many other species get by with a lot less. The gut microbe Escherichia coli, for example, has just 4,100 genes.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/04/science/04zimmer/04zimmer-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/04/science/04zimmer/04zimmer-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/04/science/04zimmer/04zimmer-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 614, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/04/science/04zimmer/04zimmer-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 767, 'width': 1280, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/04/science/04zimmer/04zimmer-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/04/science/04zimmer/04zimmer-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/04/science/04zimmer/04zimmer-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Simple Can Life Get? It’s Complicated', 'kicker': 'Matter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Simple Life, With Companions', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E Coli (Bacteria)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Genetics and Heredity', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'DNA (Deoxyribonucleic Acid)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bacteria', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cell (Journal)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Microbiology', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-04T12:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carl Zimmer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zimmer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1222c84b-cdd1-5481-9ab0-809083de1f19', 'word_count': 884, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1222c84b-cdd1-5481-9ab0-809083de1f19'}, {'abstract': 'In “The Way, Way Back,” two surrogate fathers dominate a 14-year-old boy’s days and fight, implicitly, for his young soul.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/05/movies/the-way-way-back-from-nat-faxon-and-jim-rash.html', 'snippet': 'In “The Way, Way Back,” two surrogate fathers dominate a 14-year-old boy’s days and fight, implicitly, for his young soul.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Though it takes place in what looks like the present — the familiar now of ear buds and smartphones — “The Way, Way Back,” a summertime coming-of-age tale written and directed by Nat Faxon and Jim Rash, is infused with nostalgia. The movie’s title seems to refer to the rear-facing third seat of the massive old station wagon where we first encounter its hero, a glum 14-year-old boy named Duncan (Liam James). But it also describes a hazy, bittersweet mood of recollection that hovers around the action like July humidity.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/05/arts/05WAY_SPAN/05WAY_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 367, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/05/arts/05WAY_SPAN/05WAY_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 367}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/05/arts/05WAY_SPAN/05WAY_SPAN-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 626, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/05/arts/05WAY_SPAN/05WAY_SPAN-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1252, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/05/arts/05WAY_SPAN/05WAY_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/05/arts/05WAY_SPAN/05WAY_SPAN-thumbSta