s.com/2013/07/04/another-chance/', 'snippet': 'Never mind that Morsi’s removal is a coup. Egyptians feel that the freedoms they’ve hoped for since 2011 are within reach again.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CAIRO — Planning your day around a coup d’état can be tricky, but Cairenes are flexible. Shops and businesses in my central neighborhood opened for business as usual Wednesday morning, while protesters camping out in Tahrir Square and near the presidential palace finally got some sleep.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Another Chance', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': "Coups D'Etat and Attempted Coups D'Etat", 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morsi, Mohamed', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Muslim Brotherhood (Egypt)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-04T13:37:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maria Golia', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maria', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Golia', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f313b0db-78dc-575f-856c-9350cca68922', 'word_count': 652, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f313b0db-78dc-575f-856c-9350cca68922'}, {'abstract': 'The candidates’ daily campaign schedule at a glance.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/04/july-4-where-the-candidates-are-today/', 'snippet': 'The candidates’ daily campaign schedule at a glance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Planned events for the mayoral candidates, according to the campaigns and organizations they are affiliated with. Times are listed as scheduled but frequently change.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'July 4: Where the Candidates Are Today', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections, Mayors', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-04T13:55:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alison Leigh Cowan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alison', 'middlename': 'Leigh', 'lastname': 'Cowan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/869ce3f2-cbae-5f60-a139-6989006a3d53', 'word_count': 1162, 'uri': 'nyt://article/869ce3f2-cbae-5f60-a139-6989006a3d53'}, {'abstract': 'No enjoyment allowed.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/04/the-farbissen-faction/', 'snippet': 'No enjoyment allowed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My late father burst out laughing when I told him that the Lotte Lenya character in the Austin Powers movies is named Frau Farbissina; I’m sufficiently cut off from my cultural heritage that I had to ask him why. It turns out that it comes from farbissen, which — as is typical in Yiddish — has connotations that go beyond its usual translation as “bitter”. A farbissineh (think clenched teeth) is someone who not only isn’t enjoying herself, but is determined to make sure that nobody else has any enjoyment either.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Farbissen Faction', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-07-04T14:11:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1f93e00a-80cd-50fe-89c6-1bf623230f55', 'word_count': 467, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1f93e00a-80cd-50fe-89c6-1bf623230f55'}, {'abstract': 'Is this the rollback of political Islam?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/04/opinion/friedman-egypts-revolution-part-ii.html', 'snippet': 'Is this the rollback of political Islam?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Watching the toppling of the Muslim Brotherhood-led government in Egypt, the most interesting question for me is this: Will we one day look back at this moment as the beginning of the rollback of political Islam?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/thomas-l-friedman/thomas-l-friedman-articleLarge.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/thomas-l-friedman/thomas-l-friedman-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/thomas-l-friedman/thomas-l-friedman-jumbo.png', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/thomas-l-friedman/thomas-l-friedman-superJumbo.png', 'height': 1000, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/thomas-l-friedman/thomas-l-friedman-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 