 has denied a report that former President Nelson Mandela was in a “permanent vegetative state” in a Pretoria hospital, saying Mr. Mandela, the anti-apartheid leader, remains in “critical but stable” condition.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'South African Government Denies Mandela Was in ‘Vegetative State’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mandela, Nelson', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Africa', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-05T13:32:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rick Lyman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lyman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2c4fe243-547d-5238-81c3-527d5eb640a4', 'word_count': 153, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2c4fe243-547d-5238-81c3-527d5eb640a4'}, {'abstract': 'Europe never needed an overarching government, let alone monetary integration, to establish peace.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/06/opinion/global/the-euro-is-no-peacemaker.html', 'snippet': 'Europe never needed an overarching government, let alone monetary integration, to establish peace.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In “Europe’s Truths” (Globalist, July 2), Roger Cohen contended that the euro must continue to maintain European peace. But Europe never needed an overarching government, let alone monetary integration, to establish peace. Switzerland and Norway have neither and remain peaceful. In forcing austerity upon the Mediterranean states and mandating that European taxpayers finance their bailouts, the E.U. has divided Europe, prompting a surge of anti-Europeanism and xenophobia. Continuing down this path will only amplify Europe’s social and economic crisis.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Euro is No Peacemaker', 'kicker': 'Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Union', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Monetary Union', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-05T13:40:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Global Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/d4baee98-e95d-50a2-bc77-c2bf51d6568c', 'word_count': 82, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d4baee98-e95d-50a2-bc77-c2bf51d6568c'}, {'abstract': 'Personality development and self-grooming programs are becoming a booming business, as the common idea is that everyone in the country needs some kind of help with their personalities.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/05/developing-indias-personality/', 'snippet': 'Personality development and self-grooming programs are becoming a booming business, as the common idea is that everyone in the country needs some kind of help with their personalities.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The personalities of Tina Nagpal, a 26-year-old banker with ICICI, and Akshay Agarwal, 18-year-old graduate of Delhi Public School seemed to have nothing in common — except, apparently, the need for urgent intervention.And that’s how, on a balmy June morning, the well-turned-out woman and the goofy teenager ended up trying to make party conversation without breaking into a sweat as a roomful of peers and a patient, yet firm, instructor watched, some of them with notebook and pen. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype':