ard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/07/fashion/weddings/07VOWS1_SPAN/07VOWS1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Life Graced With Love Letters and Notes', 'kicker': 'Christina Daigneault and Simon Van Booy', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Life Graced With Love Letters and Notes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Laskey, Margaux', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weddings and Engagements', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dating and Courtship', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Van Booy, Simon', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vows (Times Column)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-05T19:08:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Society', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'subsection_name': 'Weddings', 'byline': {'original': 'By Margaux Laskey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Margaux', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Laskey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6e58dd87-404e-53ea-8366-51be573f3dfb', 'word_count': 1447, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6e58dd87-404e-53ea-8366-51be573f3dfb'}, {'abstract': 'Bookselling was once a local business. Now Amazon has a larger market share of new and used printed books than any company has ever. That gives it extraordinary power.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/05/the-price-of-amazon/', 'snippet': 'Bookselling was once a local business. Now Amazon has a larger market share of new and used printed books than any company has ever. That gives it extraordinary power.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Amazon.com story is remarkable. Within living memory, bookselling was a local activity. A major city would have two or three large independent stores selling new books and other large, scruffier stores selling secondhand books. Paperbacks would receive wide if uneven circulation on bus station and drugstore racks. It was not a perfect system, but it had the advantage of being diffuse and thus hard to control. The hippie, black and women’s movements of the 1960s would not have been so successful in challenging authority without the bookstores, which made their ideas widely available and sympathetic in a way that television, for instance, did not.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/06/business/05bits-amazon/05bits-amazon-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 408, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/06/business/05bits-amazon/05bits-amazon-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 408}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/06/business/05bits-amazon/05bits-amazon-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 697, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/06/business/05bits-amazon/05bits-amazon-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1393, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/06/business/05bits-amazon/05bits-amazon-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/06/business/05bits-amazon/05bits-amazon-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0