esk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Felicity Barringer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Felicity', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barringer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d993428e-5b35-550f-b286-c56a0d29271d', 'word_count': 1187, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d993428e-5b35-550f-b286-c56a0d29271d'}, {'abstract': 'A new Senate bill would serve the interests of reckless banks and careless corporations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/06/opinion/reining-in-the-regulators.html', 'snippet': 'A new Senate bill would serve the interests of reckless banks and careless corporations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For all its rabid partisanship, Congress has shown time and again that it is willing to come together to deregulate corporate America. The latest example is a new bill in the Senate that would effectively end the independence of independent regulatory agencies, including the Securities and Exchange Commission, the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau, the Consumer Product Safety Commission and the National Labor Relations Board. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reining In the Regulators', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Reining In the Regulators', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dodd-Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer Protection Act (2010)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lobbying and Lobbyists', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Securities and Exchange Commission', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Labor Relations Board', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Consumer Financial Protection Bureau', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Consumer Product Safety Commission', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Portman, Rob', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Warner, Mark R', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Collins, Susan M', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-06T01:10:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/e6ad026e-f2a0-5c86-992b-9de1fc753a0c', 'word_count': 450, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e6ad026e-f2a0-5c86-992b-9de1fc753a0c'}, {'abstract': 'The case of a pregnant 11-year old girl who was raped has set off a national debate about abortion in one of the most socially conservative countries in Latin America.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/06/world/americas/chile-rape-ignites-abortion-debate.html', 'snippet': 'The case of a pregnant 11-year old girl who was raped has set off a national debate about abortion in one of the most socially conservative countries in Latin America.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The case of a pregnant 11-year-old girl who was raped has set off a national debate about abortion in one of the most socially conservative countries in Latin America. State television reported that the girl, known only as “Belen,” is 14 weeks pregnant. Police arrested her mother’s partner, who confessed to abusing the fifth grader. Doctors say her life and that of the fetus are at high risk. But ending the pregnancy is not an option. Chile allowed abortions for medical reasons until they were outlawed in 1973 by the Pinochet dictatorship. The current government of President Sebastián Piñera has opposed any loosening of the prohibition. Many Chileans were venting outrage about the case on social media on Friday. (AP)', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Chile: Rape Ignites Abortion Debate', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Americas', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Chile: Rape Ignites Abortion Debate', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ABORTION', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pinera, Sebastian', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chile', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-06T01:15:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/6d4bb1f9-5ffe-5b6d-bb57-7d16cbef59c8', 'word_count': 120, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6d4bb1f9-5ffe-5b6d-bb57-7d16cbef59c8'}, {'abstract': 'Dwight Howard committed to the Rockets after mulling offers from five teams and became the first star in modern N.B.A. history to walk away from the Lakers while still in his prime.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/06/sports/basketball/lakers-dwight-howard-and-rockets.html', 'snippet': 'Dwight Howard committed to the Rockets after mulling offers from five teams and became the first star in modern N.B.A. history to walk away from the Lakers while still in his prime.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dwight Howard soared into Los Angeles last summer as a flawed but celebrated savior, a smiling, muscle-bound, mischievous superstar who cou