ew York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonah Engel Bromwich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonah', 'middlename': 'Engel', 'lastname': 'Bromwich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/82474edc-29fc-5131-8a7d-6bab604ad686', 'word_count': 595, 'uri': 'nyt://article/82474edc-29fc-5131-8a7d-6bab604ad686'}, {'abstract': 'Thee Wimbledon veterans take a look at the unlikely final between Sabine Lisicki and Marion Bartoli.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/05/assessing-the-womens-final/', 'snippet': 'Thee Wimbledon veterans take a look at the unlikely final between Sabine Lisicki and Marion Bartoli.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WIMBLEDON, England — Wimbledon 2013 was supposed to be the Serena Williams show. Two weeks ago, all the talk was about the supposedly near-impossible task of stopping the indomitable American from claiming her sixth title at the All England Club.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Assessing the Women’s Final', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wimbledon Tennis Tournament', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Austin, Tracy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bartoli, Marion', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lisicki, Sabine', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tauziat, Nathalie', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-06T03:30:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Cox', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cox', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a0dea845-28cb-5d16-b8e4-e84937c3ef93', 'word_count': 782, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a0dea845-28cb-5d16-b8e4-e84937c3ef93'}, {'abstract': 'A court in China convicted and sentenced a man to 10 years in prison for battering the owner of a Japanese car during anti-Japanese protests that swept the country in September.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/06/world/asia/china-anti-japan-violence-punished.html', 'snippet': 'A court in China convicted and sentenced a man to 10 years in prison for battering the owner of a Japanese car during anti-Japanese protests that swept the country in September.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A court in China convicted and sentenced a man to 10 years in prison for battering the owner of a Japanesecar with a motorcycle lock during anti-Japanese protests that swept the country in September, official news media said Friday. It was an assault that prompted debate about violent undercurrents of popular nationalism in China. The attacker, Cai Yang, was among 12 men sentenced by courts in Xi’an, a city that experienced violent protests after a territorial dispute with Japan flared, Xinhua, the state-run news agency reported. The dispute has brought an upwelling of public anger that also draws on bitterness over the Japanese invasion and occupation of China in the 20th century. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'China: Anti-Japan Violence Punished', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Asia', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'China: Anti-Japan Violence Punished', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Buckley, Christopher', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sentences (Criminal)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Assaults', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-06T03:40:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chris Buckley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chris', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Buckley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/89380a0f-b869-5fe7-b0a0-b5ffca6390d5', 'word_count': 112, 'uri': 'nyt://article/89380a0f-b869-5fe7-b0a0-b5ffca6390d5'}, {'abstract': 'Steve Sterner is one of the last remaining silent-film accompanists, playing at the Brooklyn Academy of Music and at the Film Forum in Manhattan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/07/nyregion/piano-man-of-a-silent-screen.html', 'snippet': 'Steve Sterner is one of the last remaining silent-film accompanists, playing at the Brooklyn Academy of Music and at the Film Forum in Manhattan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Wednesday evening, Steve Sterner, 62, walked into the Brooklyn Academy of Music’s Harvey Theater and sat at an upright piano with a sliver-view of the screen.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/07/nyregion/07CHARACTER_SPAN/07CHARACTER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/07/nyregion/07CHARACTER_SPAN/07CHARACTER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/07/nyregion/07CHARACTER_SPAN/07CHARACTER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'super