talking about race.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/07/sunday-review/has-caucasian-lost-its-meaning.html', 'snippet': 'An obsolete term survives, often as a last resort for people who are uncomfortable talking about race.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AS a racial classification, the term Caucasian has many flaws, dating as it does from a time when the study of race was based on skull measurements and travel diaries. It has long been entirely unmoored from its geographical reference point, the Caucasus region. Its equivalents from that era are obsolete — nobody refers to Asians as “Mongolian” or blacks as “Negroid.”', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/07/sunday-review/07WHITE/07WHITE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 763, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/07/sunday-review/07WHITE/07WHITE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 763}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/07/sunday-review/07WHITE/07WHITE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 806, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/07/sunday-review/07WHITE/07WHITE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1538, 'width': 1210, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/07/sunday-review/07WHITE/07WHITE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/07/sunday-review/07WHITE/07WHITE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/07/sunday-review/07WHITE/07WHITE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Has ‘Caucasian’ Lost Its Meaning?', 'kicker': 'News Analysis', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Has ‘Caucasian’ Lost Its Meaning?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dewan, Shaila K', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'English Language', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-06T18:30:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shaila Dewan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shaila', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dewan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News Analysis', '_id': 'nyt://article/a7c9e13c-a7ce-5337-9958-756d1e0bdb3a', 'word_count': 1004, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a7c9e13c-a7ce-5337-9958-756d1e0bdb3a'}, {'abstract': 'I’m in pain. The doctor’s about to stick a needle in my eye. Here’s why that’s a good thing.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/06/eyeball-of-providence/', 'snippet': 'I’m in pain. The doctor’s about to stick a needle in my eye. Here’s why that’s a good thing.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Oh, that’s very nice,” I say, as the mild sedative takes hold just before Dr. Nagel performs cataract surgery on my left eye in the ambulatory-surgery facility at Mount Sinai Hospital. “I can see why people become addicts,” I add, beginning the Sedative Gabble, which I will approximate here. “Do you know where the word ‘cataract’ comes come — from?” I mumble. “Probably from the Latin cataracta, but not what thu yink. You think. Not for like a waterfowl — fall. From” — and here the sedative makes me pause for at least 30 seconds and then take a deep breath — “from a meaning of Latin of gate meaning ‘portcullis’ like a castle thing that you can see through a little but barrier.”', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Eyeball of Providence', 'kicker': 'Anxiety', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Eyeball of Providence', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cataracts', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Eyes and Eyesight', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Surgery and Surgeons', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-06T18:32:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel Menaker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Menaker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b6ba6d8e-08cd-56cc-878a-2d2ff79653d6', 'word_count': 1062, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b6ba6d8e-08cd-56cc-878a-2d2ff79653d6'}, {'abstract': 'At 80, I often feel life is about to begin, only to realize it is almost over.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/07/opinion/sunday/the-joy-of-old-age-no-kidding.html', 'snippet': 'At 80, I often feel life is about to begin, only to realize it is almost over.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LAST night I dreamed about mercury — huge, shining globules of quicksilver rising and falling. Mercury is element number 80, and my dream is a reminder that on Tuesday, I will be 80 myself. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Joy of Old Age. (No Kidding.)', 'kicker': 'Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Joy of Old Age. (No Kidding.)', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elderly', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sacks, Oliver', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Death and Dying', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Age, Chronological', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-06T18:34:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Oliver Sacks', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Oliver', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sacks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/0225a5cd-5bbb-593d-a14d-70867ff4bf2d', 'word_count': 1051, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0225a5cd-5bbb-593d-a14d-70867ff4bf2d'}, {'abstract': 'The longest semifinal match in Wimbledon history was a gift to fans from two top players.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/06/to-tennis-fans-from-djokovic-and-del-potro/', 'snippet': 'The longest semifinal match in Wimbledon history was a gift to fans from two top players.', 'lead_paragraph': 'That’s as good as it gets.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'To Tennis Fans, From Djokovic and Del Potro', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wimbledon Tennis Tournament', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'del Potro, Juan Martin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Djokovic, Novak', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-06T18:42:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': "By Craig O'Shannessy", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Craig', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "O'Shannessy", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d5c075e9-cf1d-5a1e-a8e9-f4e63771cc05', 'word_count': 954, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d5c075e9-cf1d-5a1e-a8e9-f4e63771cc05'}, {'abstract': 'Transplanting the stool from one person into the digestive tract of another seems, well, repulsive, but sometimes it makes a lot of sense.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/06/why-i-donated-my-stool/', 'snippet': 'Transplanting the stool from one person into the digestive tract of another seems, well, repulsive, but sometimes it makes a lot of sense.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This spring I saved a friend from a terrible illness, maybe even death. No, I didn’t donate a kidney or a piece of my lung. I did it with my stool.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why I Donated My Stool', 'kicker': 'Private Lives', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Why I Donated My Stool', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Clostridium Difficile (Bacterium)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colitis', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Feces', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Intestinal Tract', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Transplants', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-06T18:54:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marie Myung-Ok Lee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marie', 'middlename': 'Myung-ok', 'lastname': 'Lee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0fa956d7-637c-5aac-b92d-edbf95939340', 'word_count': 1800, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0fa956d7-637c-5aac-b92d-edbf95939340'}, {'abstract': 'An Islamist coup grabbed headlines in Mali, which is the first stop on this year’s win-a-trip. But the real threat to Malian children is extreme malnutrition.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/07/opinion/sunday/kristof-qaeda-rebels-are-gone-death-isnt.html', 'snippet': 'An Islamist coup grabbed headlines in Mali, which is the first stop on this year’s win-a-trip. But the real threat to Malian children is extreme malnutrition.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TIMBUKTU, Mali — IT’S time for my annual win-a-trip journey, in which I take a university student with me on a reporting trip to Africa. So I’m here in Timbuktu, the ancient crossroads of the Sahara, with Erin Luhmann, a journalism student from the University of Wisconsin, navigating roadblocks and jittery soldiers in a city that Islamist militants ruled until early this year.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/nicholas-kristof/nicholas-kristof-articleLarge-v2.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/nicholas-kristof/nicholas-kristof-articleLarge-v2.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/nicholas-kristof/nicholas-kristof-jumbo-v2.png', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/nicholas-kristof/nicholas-kristof-superJumbo-v2.png', 'height': 1000, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': N