eight': 746, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/08/arts/boule/boule-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 746}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/08/arts/boule/boule-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 825, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/08/arts/boule/boule-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1649, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/08/arts/boule/boule-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/08/arts/boule/boule-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/08/arts/boule/boule-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Avidly Lighting the Way as a Partner Saws Along', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Avidly Lighting the Way As a Partner Saws Along', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boule, Michelle', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lee, Okkyung', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-07T21:51:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Seibert', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Seibert', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/95703bfb-4b44-51ad-af14-27641577f9dc', 'word_count': 431, 'uri': 'nyt://article/95703bfb-4b44-51ad-af14-27641577f9dc'}, {'abstract': 'Inside the battered jetliner, passengers helped other passengers to scramble out to safety.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/08/us/one-passengers-story-we-had-to-help-each-other-out.html', 'snippet': 'Inside the battered jetliner, passengers helped other passengers to scramble out to safety.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Inside the battered hull of Asiana Airlines Flight 214, the screaming had ceased.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'One Passenger’s Story: ‘We Had to Help Each Other Out’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Aviation Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Asiana Airlines', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'San Francisco (Calif)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-07T21:51:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Maslin Nir', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': 'Maslin', 'lastname': 'Nir', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a8033df8-d4d7-5f79-b48f-9abf12d08bff', 'word_count': 538, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a8033df8-d4d7-5f79-b48f-9abf12d08bff'}, {'abstract': '“This Town,” by Mark Leibovich, the national political correspondent for The New York Times Magazine, is a blistering look at the egos and machinations of Washington’s insider culture.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/08/books/this-town-by-mark-leibovich-skewers-washingtons-insiders.html', 'snippet': '“This Town,” by Mark Leibovich, the national political correspondent for The New York Times Magazine, is a blistering look at the egos and machinations of Washington’s insider culture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Of all the irritating things about Washington — the phoniness, the showy cars, the utter inability of a metropolitan area of 6.9 million people to produce a single decent slice of pizza or a passable submarine sandwich with oil and not mayonnaise — none is more infuriating than the local insider habit of referring to the place as “this town,” as in “He’s the most important power broker in this town” or, more likely (and worse), “The way to get ahead in this town is to seem not to be trying to get ahead.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/08/arts/jpbook/jpbook-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 621, 'width': 532, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/08/arts/jpbook/jpbook-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 621, 'width': 532, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/08/arts/jpbook/jpbook-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/08/arts/jpbook/jpbook-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/08/arts/jpbook/jpbook-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hypocrisy, Thy Name Is (Duh!) Washington', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hypocrisy, Thy Name Is (Duh!) Washington', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Leibovich, Mark', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-07T21:54:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By David M. Shribman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/228f816e-5e78-512f-9e7b-9c5ee9fe4923', 'word_count': 1131, 'uri': 'nyt://article/228f816e-5e78-512f-9e7b-9c5ee9fe4923'}, {'abstract': 'Jody Oberfelder Dance Projects performed the installation “4Chambers” at Officers’ House No. 15 on Governors Island.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/08/arts/dance/4chambers-offers-an-artsy-embrace-on-governors-island.html', 'snippet': 'Jody Oberfelder Dance Projects performed the installation “4Chambers” at Officers’ House No. 15 on Governors Island.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On a blisteringly hot afternoon, it’s hard to suppress that moment of horror when a choreographer announces that the participatory work you’re about to see involves physical touch. But really, pity the dancers. They are the ones who must manipulate and interact with overheated bodies. They are those who, in the case of Jody Oberfelder’s performance-installation “4Chambers,” have the responsibility of turning a science project into a dance. It’s a thankless task. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'There Are Pillows and a Lot of Touching, but, Really, It’s a Dance Work', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'There Are Pillows and a Lot of Touching, but, Really, It’s a Dance Work', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Oberfelder, Jody', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-07T21:55:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gia Kourlas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kourlas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/18f6066f-54f6-5c69-b7d7-23c9a636b0c0', 'word_count': 460, 'uri': 'nyt://article/18f6066f-54f6-5c69-b7d7-23c9a636b0c0'}, {'abstract': 'The Boston Symphony Orchestra opened its summer festival at Tanglewood with an all-Tchaikovsky program conducted by Rafael Frühbeck de Burgos.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/08/arts/music/boston-symphony-orchestra-opens-the-tanglewood-festival.html', 'snippet': 'The Boston Symphony Orchestra opened its summer festival at Tanglewood with an all-Tchaikovsky program conducted by Rafael Frühbeck de Burgos.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LENOX, Mass. — Meteorologists, astronomers and schoolchildren may disagree. But for a substantial crowd of music lovers, summer officially began on Friday when the Boston Symphony Orchestra opened its summer festival at Tanglewood with an all-Tchaikovsky concert under the baton of Rafael Frühbeck de Burgos. The concert took place in the Koussevitzky Music Shed, which celebrates its 75th year this season, and, like that structure, aimed for a happy medium between solidly grounded and breezy.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/08/arts/subtangle/subtangle-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 377, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/08/arts/subtangle/subtangle-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 377}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/08/arts/subtangle/subtangle-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 643, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/08/arts/subtangle/subtangle-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1194, 'width': 1902, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/08/arts/subtangle/subtangle-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/08/arts/subtangle/subtangle-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/08/arts/subtangle/subtangle-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Teasing and Gradual, Then a Dash to the Finish', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Teasing and Gradual, Then a Dash to the Finish', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'da Fonseca-Wollheim, Corinna', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tanglewood Music Festival (Lenox, Mass)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Boston Symphony Orchestra', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fruhbeck de Burgos, Rafael', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bell, Joshua', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-07T21:57:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Corinna da Fonseca-Wollheim', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/be731624-f036-5cde-bc85-277a9c60ee03', 'word_count': 525, 'uri': 'nyt://article/be731624-f036-5cde-bc85-277a9c60ee03'}, {'abstract': 'A Kickstarter campaign to raise money for a sequel to Maurice Sendak’s “Where the Wild Things Are” has been suspended over a copyright dispute.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/08/books/publisher-objects-to-sequel-to-sendaks-wild-things.html', 'snippet': 'A Kickstarter campaign to raise money for a sequel to Maurice Sendak’s “Where the Wild Things Are” has been suspended over a copyright dispute.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A Kickstarter campaign to raise money for a sequel to Maurice Sendak’s “Where the Wild Things Are” has been suspended over a copyright dispute. The project, a poem written by Geoffrey O. Todd with illustrations by Rich Berner called “Back to the Wild,” was to chronicle a new adventure in which Max travels with his daughter, Sophie, to see the Wild Things for the first time in 30 years. In the original Kickstarter post, published in May, the authors said they hoped to raise £25,000 (about $37,270) to publish the work and had “been very careful not to impinge on Mr. Sendak’s copyright and have taken the necessary legal advice around this whole project.” HarperCollins Publishers, which published the original book, felt differently. In a legal notice sent to Kickstarter on July 3, the company said, “Any such unauthorized ‘sequel’ would clearly violate the estate’s right to create derivative works.” Before Sendak died last year, at 83, he spoke out against writing a sequel to his 1963 classic in a 2011 video interview with the Web site for the Tate museums in Britain: “Go to hell,” he said. “I’m not a whore. I don’t do those things.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Publisher Objects to Sequel to Sendak’s ‘Wild Things’', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Publisher Objects to Sequel to Sendak’s ‘Wild Things’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sendak, Maurice', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'HarperCollins Publishers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kickstarter', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-07T22:00:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/bb3208c6-1462-56b1-9495-b0ff34aa636a', 'word_count': 197, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bb3208c6-1462-56b1-9495-b0ff34aa636a'}, {'abstract': 'For everything there is a season, and this is the season of the return of Randy Sowell.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/07/seemingly-mad/', 'snippet': 'For everything there is a season, and this is the season of the return of Randy Sowell.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MONDAY’S PUZZLE — We haven’t seen Randy Sowell since 2010, but he returns today with a puzzle that might have you feeling a bit dizzy after you’re done solving.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Seemingly Mad', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-07T22:00:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/03ea3d7a-edd9-5274-b8a4-736d52671b56', 'word_count': 156, 'uri': 'nyt://article/03ea3d7a-edd9-5274-b8a4-736d52671b56'}, {'abstract': 'The original art for the cover of Issue No. 2 of “The Dark Knight Returns,” right, will be sold next month by Heritage Auctions for an estimated price of more than $500,000.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/08/arts/dark-knight-returns-cover-art-goes-for-sale.html', 'snippet': 'The original art for the cover of Issue No. 2 of “The Dark Knight Returns,” right, will be sold next month by Heritage Auctions for an estimated price of more than $500,000.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The original art for the cover of Issue No. 2 of “The Dark Knight Returns,” right, will be sold next month by Heritage Auctions for an estimated price of more than $500,000. “The Dark Knight Returns,” written and illustrated by Frank Miller, was published in 1986, and it, along with “Watchmen,” by Alan Moore and Dave Gibbons, ushered in a new sophistication in comic books. “The Dark Knight Returns” imagines a future Gotham City, where crime and corruption are rampant and Batman comes out of retirement to save it. He also recruits the first female Robin, Carrie Kelley. “The Dark Knight Returns” prompted a Batman revival that led to a series of movies in the 1980s and ’90s and more recently to Christopher Nolan’s three blockbusters about the character. “For fans of modern comics, this drawing is where everything really begins,” Todd Hignite, the vice president of Heritage Auctions, said in a statement. He noted that the cover of “The Dark Knight Returns” No. 3, with Batman and the new Robin, sold for $448,125 in 2011. The market for original comic book art, as well as for Golden Age comic books, continues to boom. In 2011 a copy of Action Comics No. 1 sold for $2.16 million.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Dark Knight Returns’ Cover Art Goes for Sale', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Dark Knight Returns’ Cover Art Goes for Sale', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comic Books and Strips', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gustines, George Gene', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Miller, Frank', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Batman (Fictional Character)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Heritage Auctions', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-07T22:01:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By George Gene Gustines', 'person': [{'firstname': 'George', 'middlename': 'Gene', 'lastname': 'Gustines', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/5da2ee94-45c8-5a5d-b938-2eba3e185c0b', 'word_count': 207, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5da2ee94-45c8-5a5d-b938-2eba3e185c0b'}, {'abstract': 'More than 60,000 country music fans gathered on Saturday at the University of Oklahoma in Norman to watch a concert and help raise money for tornado relief.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/08/arts/music/a-country-concert-for-tornado-victims.html', 'snippet': 'More than 60,000 country music fans gathered on Saturday at the University of Oklahoma in Norman to watch a concert and help raise money for tornado relief.', 'lead_paragraph': 'More than 60,000 country music fans gathered on Saturday at the University of Oklahoma in Norman to watch a concert and help raise money for tornado relief, The Associated Press reported. Toby Keith headlined a lineup that also included, among others, Garth Brooks and his wife, Trisha Yearwood (both at right); Willie Nelson; Sammy Hagar; and Carrie Underwood, in a satellite performance. Proceeds from the sold-out seven-hour show went to a fund established by the United Way of Central Oklahoma designed to help the victims of the tornadoes that struck the state in May. The concert was organized by Mr. Keith, who grew up in Moore, Okla., one of the towns most affected by the storms. It was the first live performance for Mr. Brooks in Oklahoma since 1997, and he did not hesitate to play his hits. But according to The Tulsa World, he also hinted that “new music feels like it’s on the horizon.” Mr. Brooks has not released a studio album of new material since “Scarecrow” in 2001.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Country Concert for Tornado Victims', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Country Concert For Tornado Victims', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Country Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Keith, Toby', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Oklahoma', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tornadoes', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-07T22:03:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'C