on': [{'firstname': 'Sebnem', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Arsu', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9155ef43-f3ed-55df-be49-e46a936518d9', 'word_count': 1081, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9155ef43-f3ed-55df-be49-e46a936518d9'}, {'abstract': 'Along with the Union and Confederate armies, hundreds of nurses descended on Gettysburg.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/07/nursing-the-wounded-at-gettysburg/', 'snippet': 'Along with the Union and Confederate armies, hundreds of nurses descended on Gettysburg.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Miss Cornelia Hancock had embarked on a mission of mercy, and wasn’t about to let her age and appearance prevent her from carrying it through. The day before, July 5, 1863, she had left her home in southern New Jersey to accompany her brother-in-law, Dr. Henry Child, to the Gettysburg battlefield. They had made their way as far as the Baltimore train station, where Ms. Hancock and other female volunteers had encountered Dorothea Dix, the superintendent of Union Army nurses. Mrs. Dix had established strict standards for the Army’s female nurses, insisting that they be “mature in years (at least 30), plain almost to homeliness in dress, and by no means liberally endowed with personal attractions.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nursing the Wounded at Gettysburg', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nursing and Nurses', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Gettysburg (Pa)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-08T00:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pat Leonard', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pat', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leonard', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/db60b1a4-6432-5997-bee2-bd9736ad4200', 'word_count': 2542, 'uri': 'nyt://article/db60b1a4-6432-5997-bee2-bd9736ad4200'}, {'abstract': 'Eliot Spitzer, who resigned as governor of New York in 2008 amid a prostitution scandal, has until Thursday to obtain the necessary signatures to get on the ballot.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/08/nyregion/spitzer-seeks-ballot-for-city-comptrollers-race.html', 'snippet': 'Eliot Spitzer, who resigned as governor of New York in 2008 amid a prostitution scandal, has until Thursday to obtain the necessary signatures to get on the ballot.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Eliot Spitzer, who resigned as governor of New York five years ago amid a prostitution scandal, is re-entering political life, with a run for the citywide office of comptroller and a wager that voters are ready to look past his previous misconduct. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/08/nyregion/SPITZER/SPITZER-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 930, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/08/nyregion/SPITZER/SPITZER-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 930}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/08/nyregion/SPITZER/SPITZER-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 661, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/08/nyregion/SPITZER/SPITZER-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1321, 'subType': 'superJum