 [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schwartz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Martin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/567c830a-26e2-5d6d-a322-d52a3783aaa3', 'word_count': 1164, 'uri': 'nyt://article/567c830a-26e2-5d6d-a322-d52a3783aaa3'}, {'abstract': 'Fewer parents today say they want more responsibilities in their jobs than did so in 1992. Perhaps changing gender roles are at play.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/08/office-advancement-vs-home-duties/', 'snippet': 'Fewer parents today say they want more responsibilities in their jobs than did so in 1992. Perhaps changing gender roles are at play.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In an article on Monday, I wrote about the choices faced by middle-class working mothers, who typically don’t have the resources to hire nannies and other support to help them “lean in” at the office. But one of the striking things I came across in my reporting was that a majority of working parents, men as well as women, don’t actually want more responsibilities at the office. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Office Advancement vs. Home Duties', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Careers and Professions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Child Care', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Men and Boys', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-08T19:47:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4feaf0db-a818-5456-b9ce-2de40872f839', 'word_count': 546, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4feaf0db-a818-5456-b9ce-2de40872f839'}, {'abstract': 'When something goes wrong in medicine, it can be hard to know where blame lies — and in the long run, it may not matter.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/08/playing-and-losing-as-a-medical-team/', 'snippet': 'When something goes wrong in medicine, it can be hard to know where blame lies — and in the long run, it may not matter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of my patients died last month, a man I was particularly fond of. I had known him for almost 20 years. I had come to know his wife in person, the children in his frequently exasperated anecdotes and his grandchildren in blurry cellphone photos. His wife left a message with the unexpected news on a Monday morning.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/09/science/09ZUGE/09ZUGE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 566, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/09/science/09ZUGE/09ZUGE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 566}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/09/science/09ZUGE/09ZUGE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 965, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/09/science/09ZUGE/09ZUGE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1872, 'width': 1986, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/09/science/09ZUGE/09ZUGE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/09/science/09ZUGE/09ZUGE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/09/science/09ZUGE/09ZUGE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Playing, and Losing, as a Medical Team', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Playing, and Losing, as a Team', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doctors', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-08T19:49:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Abigail Zuger, M.d', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Abigail', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zuger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fa4f5624-2d72-5d49-8b43-93d7af5c8d3f', 'word_count': 777, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fa4f5624-2d72-5d49-8b43-93d7af5c8d3f'}, {'abstract': 'Atto, a new ready-to-wear label in Paris, quietly made its debut during the couture shows.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/08/new-from-paris-a-label-with-surprises/', 'snippet': 'Atto, a new ready-to-wear label in Paris, quietly made its debut during the couture shows.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Atto, a new ready-to-wear label in Paris, quietly made its debut during the couture shows. Designed by Julien Dossena, formerly at Balenciaga, with Lion Blau, Atto seems born out a twin respect for French ’60s fashion (Cardin and so forth) and Japanese minimalism. Not that the clothes look the least bit retro, but the designers don’t shy away from fabrics that lend structure to their tailoring, like neoprene and jersey.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New From Paris, a Label With Surprises', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Couture Fashion Week', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dossena, Julien', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ATTO', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Paris (France)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-08T19:52:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cathy Horyn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cathy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Horyn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f320b6ac-a72f-553b-9a79-940597bb3a2b', 'word_count': 201, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f320b6ac-a72f-553b-9a79-940597bb3a2b'}, {'abstract': 'The number of seconds in a minute — and minutes in an hour — comes from the base-60 numeral system of ancient Mesopotamia.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/09/science/60-behind-every-second-millenniums-of-history.html', 'snippet': 'The number of seconds in a minute — and minutes in an hour — comes from the base-60 numeral system of ancient Mesopotamia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The number of seconds in a minute — and minutes in an hour — comes from the base-60 numeral system of ancient Mesopotamia. Developed around 3100 B.C., the sexagesimal system, as it is known, has fallen out of favor but is still used (with slight adjustments) to measure time and angles.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '60: Behind Every Second, Millenniums of History', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Take a Number', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Time', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mathematics', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-08T19:57:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roni Jacobson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roni', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jacobson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/6c9905d9-9f9c-5458-99bb-18a694aaee3c', 'word_count': 340, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6c9905d9-9f9c-5458-99bb-18a694aaee3c'}, {'abstract': 'We’re testing a new idea: a weekly news quiz. Let us know what you think!', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/08/weekly-news-quiz-july-2-july-8-2013/', 'snippet': 'We’re testing a new idea: a weekly news quiz. Let us know what you think!', 'lead_paragraph': 'Answer the 10 questions below to see what you know about the people and events that made news this week.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/04/world/africa/20130704EGYPT-slide-AJE7-LN/20130704EGYPT-slide-AJE7-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/04/world/africa/20130704EGYPT-slide-AJE7-LN/20130704EGYPT-slide-AJE7-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/04/world/africa/20130704EGYPT-slide-AJE7-LN/20130704EGYPT-slide-AJE7-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/04/world/africa/20130704EGYPT-slide-AJE7-LN/20130704EGYPT-slide-AJE7-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/04/world/africa/20130704EGYPT-slide-AJE7-LN/20130704EGYPT-slide-AJE7-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/04/world/africa/20130704EGYPT-slide-AJE7-LN/20130704EGYPT-slide-AJE7-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/04/world/africa/20130704EGYPT-slide-AJE7-LN/20130704EGYPT-slide-AJE7-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Weekly News Quiz | July 2 – July 8, 2013', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-07-08T20:00:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Gonchar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonchar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a2578807-a04e-52ff-a09f-4844c88bff87', 'word_count': 89, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a2578807-a04e-52ff-a09f-4844c88bff87'}, {'abstract': '“Good student” discounts can lessen the financial hit from adding a teenage driver to a car insurance policy.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/08/the-impact-of-adding-a-teenager-to-your-auto-policy/', 'snippet': '“Good student” discounts can lessen the financial hit from adding a teenage driver to a car insurance policy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Most parents watch their children learn to drive with trepidation. But the stress of watching them get behind the wheel is compounded by the financial shock of adding a teenager to an automobile insurance policy.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Impact of Adding a Teenager to Your Auto Policy', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Insurance and Liability', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hawaii', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-08T20:02:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/680d97be-8d49-5a7b-b60b-07c303e243a7', 'word_count': 583, 'uri': 'nyt://article/680d97be-8d49-5a7b-b60b-07c303e243a7'}, {'abstract': 'The longest-serving governor in Texas history did more than say “oops” at a presidential debate.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/08/rick-perry-wont-run-for-governor/', 'snippet': 'The longest-serving governor in Texas history did more than say “oops” at a presidential debate.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s not shocking that Gov. Rick Perry of Texas has decided against seeking a fourth term in office. Even three — as Dorothy Parker said about martinis — can be too many. Your constituents get pretty tired of hearing from you (think Mayor Michael Bloomberg and former Gov. Mario Cuomo of New York).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rick Perry Won’t Run', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perry, Rick', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-08T20:08:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Rosenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b5df2b79-0545-5d4d-8aab-3c570ce028c8', 'word_count': 395, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b5df2b79-0545-5d4d-8aab-3c570ce028c8'}, {'abstract': 'We are paying for extended life spans with a high rate of late-life disability, a new study finds.', 'web_url': 'https://newoldage.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/08/high-disability-rates-persist-in-old-age/', 'snippet': 'We are paying for extended life spans with a high rate of late-life disability, a new study finds.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Weird berries. Capsules of unpronounceable supplements. Yoga or tai chi. Crossword puzzles. Such amulets, we’re told, may ward off disability — which is the real fear that accompanies aging, isn’t it? Not the sheer number of years that will have passed, but the things we’ll no longer be able to do.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'High Disability Rates Persist in Old Age', 'kicker': 'The New Old Age', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Disabilities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elderly', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-08T20:20:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paula Span', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paula', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Span', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6851c07d-aa62-5398-afc1-aefe947298af', 'word_count': 892, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6851c07d-aa62-5398-afc1-aefe947298af'}, {'abstract': 'Parisians have been fascinated with Japan since the 19th century, and a tour of the city today reveals that the eastern influence is still strong.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/08/letter-from-paris-tokyo-on-the-seine/', 'snippet': 'Parisians have been fascinated with Japan since the 19th century, and a tour of the city today reveals that the eastern influence is still strong.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Van Gogh collected and painted imitations of Japanese woodblock prints. Baudelaire gave them to his friends. Monet painted his wife in a kimono. A popular cafe and concert hall called Le Divan Japonais — the Japanese Divan — was decorated in a Japanese style and immortalized in a poster by Toulouse-Lautrec. France’s fixation with things Japanese became so intense in the 19th century that the French gave it a name: “Japonisme.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tokyo on the Seine', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Paris (France)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-08T20:22:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elaine Sciolino', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elaine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sciolino', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/68996473-10e6-5d12-8cf5-b305f7ef2783', 'word_count': 1114, 'uri': 'nyt://article/68996473-10e6-5d12-8cf5-b305f7ef2783'}, {'abstract': 'If you’re a fan of the cookbook “Jerusalem,” by Yotam Ottolenghi and Sami Tamimi, tell us which recipe you’d like to see featured in this month’s Recipe Lab.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/08/jerusalem-fans-whats-your-favorite-recipe-from-the-book/', 'snippet': 'If you’re a fan of the cookbook “Jerusalem,” by Yotam Ottolenghi and Sami Tamimi, tell us which recipe you’d like to see featured in this month’s Recipe Lab.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A dozen new cookbooks flood my cubicle every day, and most of them go right back out, never to be heard from again. But one cookbook has kept making noise this year: “Jerusalem,” by the chefs Yotam Ottolenghi and Sami Tamimi, has gone viral among food lovers since it was published in the United States last fall. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Jerusalem’ Fans: What’s Your Favorite Recipe From the Book?', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-07-08T20:29:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Moskin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moskin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c6c2aef8-de1f-52a9-9d8f-4c60ed5b03d8', 'word_count': 228, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c6c2aef8-de1f-52a9-9d8f-4c60ed5b03d8'}, {'abstract': 'The federal judge overseeing the case said she would not rule on the N.F.L.’s motion to dismiss until Sept. 3 to give the mediator time to bring the sides closer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/09/sports/football/judge-orders-nfl-concussion-case-to-mediation.html', 'snippet': 'The federal judge overseeing the case said she would not rule on the N.F.L.’s motion to dismiss until Sept. 3 to give the mediator time to bring the sides closer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The federal judge overseeing the case brought by thousands of former N.F.L. players who have accused the league of hiding the dangers of concussions ordered both sides to mediation Monday.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Judge Orders N.F.L. Concussion Case to Mediation', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Judge Delays Ruling in N.F.L. Concussion Case and Brings In Mediator', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Concussions', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brody, Anita B', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-08T20:30:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Belson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5c4cad54-3511-535e-951e-2712e924ae94', 'word_count': 752, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5c4cad54-3511-535e-951e-2712e924ae94'}, {'abstract': 'Fabrice Tourre was the only individual at Goldman Sachs and across Wall Street sued by the Securities and Exchange Commission for helping to sell a mortgage-securities investment. The case against him was bolstered by colorful e-mails he wrote.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2013/07/09/business/dealbook/20130709-tourre-documents.html', 'snippet': 'Fabrice Tourre was the only individual at Goldman Sachs and across Wall Street sued by the Securities and Exchange Commission for helping to sell a mortgage-securities investment. The case against him was bolstered by colorful e-mails he wrote.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Fabrice Tourre was the only individual at Goldman Sachs and across Wall Street sued by the Securities and Exchange Commission for helping to sell a mortgage-securities investment. The case against him was bolstered by colorful e-mails he wrote.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/15/business/16tourre-document/16tourre-document-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 126, 'width': 190, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/15/business/16tourre-document/16tourre-document-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 126, 'width': 190, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/15/business/16tourre-document/16tourre-document-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/15/business/16tourre-document/16tourre-document-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Case Against Fabrice Tourre', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Goldman Sachs Group Inc', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Securities and Exchange Commission', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Subprime Mortgage Crisis', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tourre, Fabrice P', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mortgage-Backed Securities', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-08T20:33:30+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Business Day', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'DealBook', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/7cdaff00-6911-57ae-8f97-a41c141ce824', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/7cdaff00-6911-57ae-8f97-a41c141ce824'}, {'abstract': 'A selection of events for this week.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/08/dining-calendar-25/', 'snippet': 'A selection of events for this week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Various food and drinks specials at French restaurants in the Forgeois Group (which includes Café Noir, Jules Bistro, Cercle Rouge and Le Singe Vert) across the city will be offered for a week leading up to the Bastille Day celebration on July 14 at Bar Tabac, 128 Smith Street in Cobble Hill, Brooklyn, which features Pétanque tournaments, live music and food vendors from the neighborhood. Thirty-seven restaurants will also be participating in French Restaurant Week at the same time between Monday and July 14, offering various specials for $17.89 (the year of the French Revolution). A full list is available online.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dining Calendar', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-07-08T20:38:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bao Ong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bao', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6c5ca602-f2b7-5ec0-9021-0ea0b520f401', 'word_count': 278, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6c5ca602-f2b7-5ec0-9021-0ea0b520f401'}, {'abstract': '“The Examined Life,” by Stephen Grosz, describes the process of psychoanalysis and how the ways people connect the past, present and future reflect their capacity to change.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/09/books/the-examined-life-by-stephen-grosz.html', 'snippet': '“The Examined Life,” by Stephen Grosz, describes the process of psychoanalysis and how the ways people connect the past, present and future reflect their capacity to change.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Freud’s famous case studies, like Dora, the Wolf Man, Little Hans and the Rat Man, are psychoanalytic readings, suspenseful detective stories and elliptical narratives that have all the drama and contradictions of modernist fiction. Not only is Freud a powerful writer, but his methodology and insights also have a lot in common with literary criticism and novelistic architecture. His patient portraits showcase his skills both as a critic, intent on deconstructing his subjects’ lives, and as a masterly storyteller, adept at using unreliable narrators to explore the mysteries of love and sex and death. It’s no coincidence that he liked to write about characters from Shakespeare, Goethe, Ibsen and Sophocles (yes, Oedipus), or that he paid so much attention to the language and imagery employed by his patients. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/09/arts/book/book-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 928, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/09/arts/book/book-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 928}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/09/arts/book/book-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 663, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/09/arts/book/book-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1325, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/09/arts/book/book-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/09/arts/book/book-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/09/arts/book/book-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Listening for Clues to Mind’s Mysteries', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Listening for Clues To Mind’s Mysteries', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychology and Psychologists', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grosz, Stephen', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-08T20:44:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michiko Kakutani', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michiko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kakutani', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/fa214898-abbe-52b0-b89e-b59fdac31d45', 'word_count': 1095, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fa214898-abbe-52b0-b89e-b59fdac31d45'}, {'abstract': 'Summergarden, at the Museum of Modern Art’s Sculpture Garden, begins its 2013 offerings with four New York premieres from Joel Sachs and the New Juilliard Ensemble.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/09/arts/music/moma-summergarden-series-opens-with-contemporary-works.html', 'snippet': 'Summergarden, at the Museum of Modern Art’s Sculpture Garden, begins its 2013 offerings with four New York premieres from Joel Sachs and the New Juilliard Ensemble.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The annual series of free Summergarden concerts, presented in the sculpture garden of the Museum of Modern Art, opened on Sunday night with players from the New Juilliard Ensemble performing four contemporary works. The garden, as a concert site, is both inviting and exasperating. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/09/arts/09GARDEN/09GARDEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 377, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/09/arts/09GARDEN/09GARDEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 377}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/09/arts/09GARDEN/09GARDEN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 643, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/09/arts/09GARDEN/09GARDEN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1286, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/09/arts/09GARDEN/09GARDEN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75,