 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9084cf5c-49ad-58c2-81d7-8ec6dd8c5a79', 'word_count': 769, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9084cf5c-49ad-58c2-81d7-8ec6dd8c5a79'}, {'abstract': 'The military is fueling divisions in the country by using violence against the Muslim Brotherhood.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/09/opinion/bloodshed-in-egypt.html', 'snippet': 'The military is fueling divisions in the country by using violence against the Muslim Brotherhood.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It has been all downhill for Egypt since the military ousted President Mohamed Morsi last week. On Monday, according to reports in The Times, Egyptian soldiers fired on hundreds of Mr. Morsi’s supporters as they were praying outside the facility where he was believed to be detained. At least 51 civilians were killed and more than 300 were wounded. The military claimed its soldiers fired in response to an attack by gunmen from an unnamed “terrorist group.” But other evidence — bullet casings with the army stamp, indications that the gunfire came from the top of a nearby building — suggest a military all too willing to use excessive force.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bloodshed in Egypt', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bloodshed in Egypt', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morsi, Mohamed', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_d