', 'value': 'Mozilla Foundation', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-09T13:13:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By J.d. Biersdorfer', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d21ba16-dfbe-55fe-92c5-3578412b9044', 'word_count': 244, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d21ba16-dfbe-55fe-92c5-3578412b9044'}, {'abstract': 'Yes, they use it.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/09/whites-and-the-safety-net/', 'snippet': 'Yes, they use it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For a brief period after the 2012 election, it looked as if there might be some serious introspection among Republicans, some reconsideration of where their scorched-earth opposition to everything Obama had gotten them. But it didn’t last long. Even the notion that the GOP might need to accommodate itself a bit to an increasingly nonwhite nation has been fading fast; the big thing now is that the trouble in 2012 was missing white voters, and that the GOP just needs to redouble its efforts to identify itself as the party of white people.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Whites and the Safety Net', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-07-09T13:14:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2495676e-7470-5acd-ae45-ffb5f1b60498', 'word_count': 461, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2495676e-7470-5acd-ae45-ffb5f1b60498'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Glover, who plays the former high school football star Troy Barnes, will appear in only 5 episodes of a planned 13-episode “Community” season.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/09/donald-glover-reduces-his-course-load-on-community/', 'snippet': 'Mr. Glover, who plays the former high school football star Troy Barnes, will appear in only 5 episodes of a planned 13-episode “Community” season.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There has been another setback at Greendale Community College, the home of the NBC sitcom “Community,” and the school year hasn’t even started. Though that cult comedy series about a group of mismatched students recently regained the services of Dan Harmon, its once-deposed creator and show runner, it is losing its co-star Donald Glover for more than half of its coming fifth season, Vulture.com reported. Mr. Glover, who plays the former high school football star Troy Barnes, will appear in only 5 episodes of a planned 13-episode season. Vulture.com said that Mr. Glover, who has also appeared on “Girls” and written for “30 Rock,” plans to focus more attention on his musical career, rapping under the name Childish Gambino. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/09/arts/glover/glover-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 346, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/09/arts/glover/glover-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 346}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/09/arts/glover/glover-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 346, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/09/arts/glover/glover-s