gh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/419ab2f8-bafb-569b-a477-56a9f714f998', 'word_count': 490, 'uri': 'nyt://article/419ab2f8-bafb-569b-a477-56a9f714f998'}, {'abstract': 'New research suggests that mothers who identify as depressed are more likely to have children who are overweight or obese.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/10/depressed-mothers-more-likely-to-have-overweight-children-study-finds/', 'snippet': 'New research suggests that mothers who identify as depressed are more likely to have children who are overweight or obese.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mothers who report symptoms of depression are more likely to display obesity-promoting practices and have children who are overweight or obese than mothers who say they are not depressed, according to a new study.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Depressed Mothers More Likely to Have Overweight Children, Study Finds', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Babies and Infants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Depression (Mental)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mental Health and Disorders', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Obesity', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Children's Hospital at Montefiore", 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-10T22:25:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hope Reeves', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hope', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Reeves', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f5e80e86-1148-5c21-abe9-2abec16c1874', 'word_count': 303, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f5e80e86-1148-5c21-abe9-2abec16c1874'}, {'abstract': 'A conversation with Klaus Biesenbach, the director of MoMA PS1, about the Colony, a temporary art camp set up in its courtyard.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/11/garden/making-art-sneaking-bacon.html', 'snippet': 'A conversation with Klaus Biesenbach, the director of MoMA PS1, about the Colony, a temporary art camp set up in its courtyard.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was raining lightly on the Colony the other day. That’s the name of the temporary art camp set up in the courtyard of MoMA PS1 in Long Island City, Queens. The campers stayed dry, thanks to the silvery sunscreen curtains donated by a French company that hung atmospherically over the makeshift campsite put together by a77, an Argentine architectural firm. There were trailers and tents, a solar shower, a hydroponic garden and a kitchen presided over by Anne Apparu, a chef and food-performance artist originally from Corsica. She is a former roommate of Jenny Schlenzka, an associate curator at PS1 and one of the organizers of “Expo 1: New York,” an extended art happening exploring the planet’s environmental challenges.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/11/garden/11QNA3_SPAN/11QNA3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/11/garden/11QNA3_SPAN/11QNA3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/11/garden/11QNA3_SPAN/11QNA3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType'