'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Home & Garden', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/ce9681ab-253c-5a08-8d35-089dc6eb6229', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/ce9681ab-253c-5a08-8d35-089dc6eb6229'}, {'abstract': 'It’s part family compound, part creative commune. With spud guns.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/11/garden/they-shoot-potatoes-dont-they.html', 'snippet': 'It’s part family compound, part creative commune. With spud guns.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SANTA MONICA, Calif. — It was a typical weekend at the Chao Pound, to use the name Stephen Chao, the former television executive and National Enquirer reporter, has given to the six little 1920s Sears kit houses (plus barn) that he and his girlfriend, Rachel Mansur, a handbag impresario, call home. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/11/garden/11COMPOUND_SPAN/11JPCOMPOUND6-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/11/garden/11COMPOUND_SPAN/11JPCOMPOUND6-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/11/garden/11COMPOUND_SPAN/11JPCOMPOUND6-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/11/garden/11COMPOUND_SPAN/11JPCOMPOUND6-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/11/garden/11COMPOUND_SPAN/11JPCOMPOUND6-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/11/garden/11COMPOUND_SPAN/11JPCOMPOUND6-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/11/garden/11COMPOUND_SPAN/11JPCOMPOUND6-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'They Shoot Potatoes, Don’t They?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'They Shoot Potatoes, Don’t They?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Green, Penelope', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chao, Stephen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interior Design and Furnishings', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Santa Monica (Calif)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-10T22:29:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Penelope Green', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Penelope', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Green', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/66e8a3f8-b192-5136-be9f-6bea4b9dfee2', 'word_count': 1730, 'uri': 'nyt://article/66e8a3f8-b192-5136-be9f-6bea4b9dfee2'}, {'abstract': 'The QuaDror system of blocks is devilishly difficult to explain but easy to play with.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/11/garden/connect-the-blocks.html', 'snippet': 'The QuaDror system of blocks is devilishly difficult to explain but easy to play with.', 'lead_paragraph': 'R. Buckminster Fuller coined the term “tensegrity,” for “tensional integrity,” a construction principle that led to a popular toy. Similarly, Dror Benshetrit, the designer of the Peacock chair from Cappellini, came up with a new truss that doubles as a diversion: four pieces of any material (like wood, metal or plastic) and four connectors of any kind (like screws, pegs or glue) that fold flat but open up to create four triangles. The QuaDror system of blocks is devilishly difficult to explain but easy to use; so easy, that prototypes disappeared. Thus was born a toy. Donald Rattner, the owner of the Module R art and design shop in Brooklyn, was strolling the International Gift Fair in New York in January and found the QuaDror blocks. The set ($36) consists of 10 trusses that can be assembled into anything in a child’s (or adult’s) imagination. Module R is at 141 Atlantic Avenue, Brooklyn. Information: (877) 360-3660 or module-r.com.\xa0', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/11/garden/11GOODS/11CURR1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 657, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/11/garden/11GOODS/11CURR1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 657}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/11/garden/11GOODS/11CURR1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 676, 'width': 617, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/11/garden/11GOODS/11CURR1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 676, 'width': 617, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/11/garden/11GOODS/11CURR1-thumbSta