ury Goods', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Midtown Area (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-12T14:16:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Satow', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Satow', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/92017914-21c7-5941-9335-96907804ae2d', 'word_count': 1171, 'uri': 'nyt://article/92017914-21c7-5941-9335-96907804ae2d'}, {'abstract': 'Consumers rarely read the standardized language for online terms and conditions, and experts disagree on their significance.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/13/your-money/novel-length-contracts-online-and-what-they-say.html', 'snippet': 'Consumers rarely read the standardized language for online terms and conditions, and experts disagree on their significance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THIS past month I’ve been agreeing to contracts like crazy. I clicked “yes” on Verizon’s terms and conditions when I paid my bill; ditto on iTunes when I was installing it on my computer. I even signed a waiver agreeing not to sue a white-water rafting company if anything went wrong when I spent a day on the Hudson.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/13/business/Shortcuts/Shortcuts-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 433, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/13/business/Shortcuts/Shortcuts-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 433}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/13/business/Shortcuts/Shortcuts-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 74