nge.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'July 12: Where the Candidates Are Today', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections, Mayors', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-12T14:45:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alison Leigh Cowan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alison', 'middlename': 'Leigh', 'lastname': 'Cowan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fd5b3cba-a23b-5089-8507-e23d85dda812', 'word_count': 1243, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fd5b3cba-a23b-5089-8507-e23d85dda812'}, {'abstract': 'A day that began with optimism ended with the Yankees’ Derek Jeter undergoing an M.R.I. and the Dodgers’ first-half sensation, Yasiel Puig, left out of the All-Star Game.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/13/sports/a-humbling-day-for-jeter-and-puig.html', 'snippet': 'A day that began with optimism ended with the Yankees’ Derek Jeter undergoing an M.R.I. and the Dodgers’ first-half sensation, Yasiel Puig, left out of the All-Star Game.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Baseball is an inherently humbling game with an expanding repertory of humility delivery devices. That’s what tied two of its brightest stars together Thursday, baseball reality checks that do not discriminate. You can be a 39-year-old shortstop with devotees sketching out your Hall of Fame plaque and be firmly reminded that you are overstaying your time in a young man’s game. Or you can be a 22-year-old who defected from Cuba and stumbled into stardom in Los Angeles and be swatted back by an army of All-Star-voting fans wielding smartphones. It’s always something.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/13/sports/13leading2/13leading1-1373640633079-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 397, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/13/sports/13leading2/13leading1-1373640633079-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 397}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/13/sports/13leading2/13leading1-1373640633079-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 677, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/13/sports/13leading2/13leading1-1373640633079-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1355, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/13/sports/13leading2/13leading1-1373640633079-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/13/sports/13leading2/13leading1-1373640633079-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/13/sports/13leading2/13leading1-1373640633079-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Humbling Day for Jeter and Puig', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jeter, Derek', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Puig, Yasiel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Herren, Chris', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'World Peace, Metta', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Strawberry, Darryl', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tebow, Tim', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Howard, Dwight', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kovalchuk, Ilya', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-12T14:52:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lynn Zinser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zinser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5f770a1d-5f0c-5fbd-b177-f3736d67fbcc', 'word_count': 745, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5f770a1d-5f0c-5fbd-b177-f3736d67fbcc'}, {'abstract': 'Today cable television is the place for deep character development and intricate structure — and antiheroic protagonists.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/14/books/review/difficult-men-by-brett-martin.html', 'snippet': 'Today cable television is the place for deep character development and intricate structure — and antiheroic protagonists.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Many years ago, in an era when the phrases “boob tube,” “idiot box” and “vast wasteland” went unchallenged as television descriptors among the Discerning Classes, I sat in a pool of prospective jurors on a case involving a fancy jeweler. Aiming, apparently, to assess whether we were likely to confuse the actions of real-life rich people with those on “Dynasty,” the defense lawyer asked each of us, “Do you watch TV?” “Nah,” swore a majority of my exceptionally discriminating citizen peers. “Just a little.” “Maybe some ‘Nova’ from PBS on a black-and-white TV.” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/0714-bks-Schwarzbaum-sub/0714-bks-Schwarzbaum-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 528, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/0714-bks-Schwarzbaum-sub/0714-bks-Schwarzbaum-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 528}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/0714-bks-Schwarzbaum-sub/0714-bks-Schwarzbaum-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/0714-bks-Schwarzbaum-sub/0714-bks-Schwarzbaum-sub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1237, 'width': 1407, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/0714-bks-Schwarzbaum-sub/0714-bks-Schwarzbaum-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/0714-bks-Schwarzbaum-sub/0714-bks-Schwarzbaum-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/0714-bks-Schwarzbaum-sub/0714-bks-Schwarzbaum-sub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'No More Mr. Nice Guys', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'No More Mr. Nice Guys', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schwarzbaum, Lisa', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Martin, Brett', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Sopranos (TV Program)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Breaking Bad (TV Program)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Mad Men (TV Program)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Wire (TV Program)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-12T14:59:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Schwarzbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schwarzbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9a5b6e76-4085-54bd-b9b3-0fc47d4d3b6a', 'word_count': 1270, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9a5b6e76-4085-54bd-b9b3-0fc47d4d3b6a'}, {'abstract': 'Adelle Waldman’s comedy of manners examines the Brooklyn literary set.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/14/books/review/adelle-waldmans-love-affairs-of-nathaniel-p.html', 'snippet': 'Adelle Waldman’s comedy of manners examines the Brooklyn literary set.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The data are in. All precincts have reported. It’s official: men suck. At least when it comes to relationships, they do. Men are sex-obsessed and self-absorbed, shallow, callow, capricious and cruel. I wouldn’t date us if we were one of the last two genders on earth.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/0714-bks-Walter/0714-bks-Walter-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 430, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/0714-bks-Walter/0714-bks-Walter-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 430}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/0714-bks-Walter/0714-bks-Walter-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 734, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/0714-bks-Walter/0714-bks-Walter-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1151, 'width': 1606, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/0714-bks-Walter/0714-bks-Walter-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/0714-bks-Walter/0714-bks-Walter-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/0714-bks-Walter/0714-bks-Walter-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bright Young Things', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bright Young Things', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Waldman, Adelle', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-12T14:59:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jess Walter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jess', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Walter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b614670d-7490-5444-90ee-30d671adf592', 'word_count': 932, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b614670d-7490-5444-90ee-30d671adf592'}, {'abstract': 'A distraught woman inhabits different selves across the 20th century in Andrew Sean Greer’s novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/14/books/review/the-impossible-lives-of-greta-wells-by-andrew-sean-greer.html', 'snippet': 'A distraught woman inhabits different selves across the 20th century in Andrew Sean Greer’s novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The clock is always ticking in Andrew Sean Greer’s “Impossible Lives of Greta Wells.” Elegiac in tone, this tale of time travel, loss and compromise is as precisely engineered as a Swiss watch. The premise is deceptively simple. It is 1985, and Greta Wells, a photographer living in Greenwich Village, has just suffered two devastating losses: Her twin brother, Felix, has died of AIDS and her lover, Nathan, has left her for another woman. Thrown into a deep depression, she consults a psychiatrist, who in turn sends her to Dr. Cerletti, an advocate of electroconvulsive therapy. “Will it change me?” Greta asks, before her first session. “Not at all, Miss Wells,” he replies. “What has changed you is your depression. What we’re trying to do is bring you back.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/14leavitt-span/14leavitt-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 288, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/14leavitt-span/14leavitt-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 288}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/14leavitt-span/14leavitt-span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 406, 'width': 846, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/14leavitt-span/14leavitt-span-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 406, 'width': 846, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/14leavitt-span/14leavitt-span-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/14leavitt-span/14leavitt-span-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/14leavitt-span/14leavitt-span-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Second and Third Chances', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Second and Third Chances', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Greer, Andrew Sean', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-12T15:00:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Leavitt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leavitt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7afb9962-746b-5da7-a5f1-c3f711132c23', 'word_count': 1338, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7afb9962-746b-5da7-a5f1-c3f711132c23'}, {'abstract': 'Tim Parks recounts Italian rail journeys and his encounters with people along the way.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/14/books/review/italian-ways-by-tim-parks.html', 'snippet': 'Tim Parks recounts Italian rail journeys and his encounters with people along the way.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The British author Tim Parks has lived in Italy since 1981, commuting on his adopted country’s vast, various and ever changing networks of trains from Verona (where he first made his home) to Milan (where he now lives and still teaches). Parks was for many years married to an Italian and has three children, all born in Italy. He has written more than a dozen novels and 10 other books, including a handful of funny, appreciative and grousing memoirs about the particolari of daily life, Italian style. In the first of these, “Italian Neighbors,” he asked the reader to picture him as “a busy but inexpert fellow dashing about the narrow confines of his territory waving a net on the end of a long stick.” A “huge” net, he added. What did he hope to catch? “National character, a sense of place, the feeling people, place and weather generate.” In his new \xadmemoir-cum-travelogue, “Italian Ways,” Parks enlarges this net to fantastical dimensions in order to bag Italy’s sprawling, convoluted and (in a way) stupendous railway system and subject it to his scrutiny — on the theory, he explains to skeptics who don’t share his train mania, that any culture “manifests itself entirely in anything the people of that culture do.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/0714-bks-Schillinger/0714-bks-Schillinger-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/0714-bks-Schillinger/0714-bks-Schillinger-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/0714-bks-Schillinger/0714-bks-Schillinger-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/0714-bks-Schillinger/0714-bks-Schillinger-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1410, 'width': 1410, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/0714-bks-Schillinger/0714-bks-Schillinger-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/0714-bks-Schillinger/0714-bks-Schillinger-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/0714-bks-Schillinger/0714-bks-Schillinger-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rites of Passage', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rites of Passage', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schillinger, Liesl', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Parks, Tim', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Railroads', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-12T15:00:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Liesl Schillinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Liesl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schillinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d573dbe9-c2c1-54b1-94de-0531e0a820f0', 'word_count': 1313, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d573dbe9-c2c1-54b1-94de-0531e0a820f0'}, {'abstract': 'New books by Fay Weldon, Marjorie Celona, Elizabeth Black, Tracy Chevalier and Lynn Coady.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/14/books/review/habits-of-the-house-by-fay-weldon-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'New books by Fay Weldon, Marjorie Celona, Elizabeth Black, Tracy Chevalier and Lynn Coady.', 'lead_paragraph': ' HABITS OF THE HOUSE  By Fay Weldon  St. Martin’s, $25.99.  ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/14stuart/14stuart-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 921, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/14stuart/14stuart-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 921}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/14stuart/14stuart-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 921, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/14stuart/14stuart-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 921, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/14stuart/14stuart-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/14stuart/14stuart-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/14stuart/14stuart-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fiction Chronicle', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fiction Chronicle', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stuart, Jan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weldon, Fay', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chevalier, Tracy', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-12T15:01:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jan Stuart', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stuart', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a63e2b94-47e0-59cd-8f4a-0a2e3e178d61', 'word_count': 1027, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a63e2b94-47e0-59cd-8f4a-0a2e3e178d61'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/14/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MY FATHERS’ GHOST IS CLIMBING IN THE RAIN,  by Patricio Pron. Translated by Mara Faye \xadLethem. (Knopf, $24.) In Pron’s haunting novel about Argentina’s Dirty War and its aftermath, an exile attempts to solve the puzzle of his father’s cloudy life.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-12T15:02:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/8576ecdc-adff-51ae-bddd-5ae7e112adf2', 'word_count': 287, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8576ecdc-adff-51ae-bddd-5ae7e112adf2'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/14/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ON SAUDI ARABIA: Its People, Past, Religion, Fault Lines — and Future,  by Karen Elliott House. (Vintage, $16.95.) House, a Pulitzer Prize-winning journalist who has been reporting on Saudi Arabia for more than 30 years, unveils this inscrutable oil-rich country — where “Saudis endlessly maneuver through winding paths between high walls of religious rules, government restrictions and cultural traditions” — and compares its calcified regime to the Soviet Union in its final days.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-12T15:02:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/29c57b1d-baa2-5a30-bc87-830933f551d6', 'word_count': 492, 'uri': 'nyt://article/29c57b1d-baa2-5a30-bc87-830933f551d6'}, {'abstract': 'Ben Ratliff, Jon Caramanica and Jon Pareles look at the three big hip-hop hit albums of the summer: Kanye West’s “Yeezus,” J. Cole’s “Born Sinner” and Jay-Z’s “Magna Carta Holy Grail.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/audio/2013/07/12/arts/music/12popcast_pod.html', 'snippet': 'Ben Ratliff, Jon Caramanica and Jon Pareles look at the three big hip-hop hit albums of the summer: Kanye West’s “Yeezus,” J. Cole’s “Born Sinner” and Jay-Z’s “Magna Carta Holy Grail.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ben Ratliff, Jon Caramanica and Jon Pareles look at the three big hip-hop hit albums of the summer: Kanye West’s “Yeezus,” J. Cole’s “Born Sinner” and Jay-Z’s “Magna Carta Holy Grail.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hip-Hop’s Old Themes and New Frontiers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-07-12T15:02:44+0000', 'document_type': 'audio', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://audio/47aa98e4-1ff9-5d07-a584-3774b5062528', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/47aa98e4-1ff9-5d07-a584-3774b5062528'}, {'abstract': 'I want my son to realize that actions have repercussions, and also how to forgive and forget. It’s hard to do both at the same time.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/12/teaching-and-learning-the-meaning-of-consequences/', 'snippet': 'I want my son to realize that actions have repercussions, and also how to forgive and forget. It’s hard to do both at the same time.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two-year-old Luka, still wobbly on uncertain toddler legs, launches himself at his 5-year-old brother, whose modern Lego masterpiece (four bedrooms, rooftop terrace, towering staircase to nowhere) he’d torn down a few hours before with gleeful abandon. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Teaching (and Learning) the Meaning of Consequences', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-12T15:07:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lynn Messina', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Messina', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a37eea61-81d6-55e4-9260-dfe284b0bdf0', 'word_count': 745, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a37eea61-81d6-55e4-9260-dfe284b0bdf0'}, {'abstract': 'Lives are bruised and twisted by history in Courtney Angela Brkic’s novel of a dislocated Croatian family.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/14/books/review/courtney-angela-brkics-first-rule-of-swimming.html', 'snippet': 'Lives are bruised and twisted by history in Courtney Angela Brkic’s novel of a dislocated Croatian family.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The violent history of postwar Croatia, from 1945 until the turn of the millennium, created three generations of dislocated people. Some were dislocated from home and roots: many thousands fled their homeland during the years of Yugoslav Communism, with its informers, “eliminations” and prison camps. Others, who stayed on, were dislocated from their history as the Communist authorities rewrote the past, a process that continued during the bloody Balkan wars of the 1990s. And dislocation, as always, extended into the psychological realm: timeless ideas of value and even truth were warped by decades of lies.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Staying Afloat', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Staying Afloat', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brkic, Courtney Angela', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-12T15:07:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooke Allen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooke', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Allen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/362c7b57-6760-56ee-8969-0780591cd6f1', 'word_count': 598, 'uri': 'nyt://article/362c7b57-6760-56ee-8969-0780591cd6f1'}, {'abstract': 'An aspiring filmmaker captures her family’s strife in this novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/14/books/review/natasha-farrants-after-iris.html', 'snippet': 'An aspiring filmmaker captures her family’s strife in this novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bluebell Gadsby is the kind of heroine who brings to mind Cassandra Mortmain in “I Capture the Castle” or Harriet the Spy: awkward, lonely and always off to one side, scribbling, or — in the latest iteration of the teenage journal — filming. “Stop creeping up on people!” exclaims her elder sister, Flora. “And stop looking at everyone through that stupid camera.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/14BROCKES/14BROCKES-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/14BROCKES/14BROCKES-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/14BROCKES/14BROCKES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/14BROCKES/14BROCKES-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1725, 'width': 1725, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/14BROCKES/14BROCKES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/14BROCKES/14BROCKES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/14BROCKES/14BROCKES-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Candid Camera', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Candid Camera', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brockes, Emma', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Farrant, Natasha', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-12T15:07:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emma Brockes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emma', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brockes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8e5da980-9387-507f-ad3b-13ff7ac42efd', 'word_count': 617, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8e5da980-9387-507f-ad3b-13ff7ac42efd'}, {'abstract': 'Two raccoons must thwart plans for an adventure theme park.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/14/books/review/kathi-appelts-true-blue-scouts-of-sugar-man-swamp.html', 'snippet': 'Two raccoons must thwart plans for an adventure theme park.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Kathi Appelt can tell a story. She has published over 30 children’s books, for everyone from toddlers to teenagers, and won awards, including a Newbery Honor, along the way. In “The True Blue Scouts of Sugar Man Swamp,” aimed at middle-grade readers, Appelt returns to the folksy narrative voice of her beloved picture book, “Bubba and Beau, Best Friends.” Her mastery of pacing and tone makes for wonderful reading aloud, even to children who would happily take on a relatively long novel on their own. There is music in her prose: “For as long as raccoons had inhabited the Sugar Man Swamp, which was eons, they had been the Official Scouts, ordained by the Sugar Man himself back in the year Aught One, also known as the Beginning of Time.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Furry Courage', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Furry Courage', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Appelt, Kathi', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-12T15:07:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Von Drasek', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': 'Von', 'lastname': 'Drasek', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/14bcc670-a238-5fbf-9017-e6c7532e85d0', 'word_count': 713, 'uri': 'nyt://article/14bcc670-a238-5fbf-9017-e6c7532e85d0'}, {'abstract': 'Two teenagers spend a summer negotiating jobs, friendships and boys.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/14/books/review/the-moon-and-more-and-nantucket-blue.html', 'snippet': 'Two teenagers spend a summer negotiating jobs, friendships and boys.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Summer is here, and with it the search for the perfect beach book, a pithy, plotty page turner that can be discussed with confidence at any cookout or party since everyone seems to be reading the same thing. Best of all is stretching out on the sand with a beach book that is actually set at the shore; somehow it heightens our delight in the transient joy of summer. Sarah Dessen, a seasoned young adult author, and the bold newcomer Leila Howland present readers with two different visions of sun and surf. While each features a young woman struggling to make the right decisions about life and love, one reads as smoothly as a rowboat ride across Central Park Lake while the other tumbles you like a rolling Coney Island wave.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Making Waves', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Making Waves', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dessen, Sarah', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Howland, Leila', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-12T15:08:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Hubert Swan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': 'Hubert', 'lastname': 'Swan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/de616cc7-bdaf-5d48-a8fc-163534709acc', 'word_count': 887, 'uri': 'nyt://article/de616cc7-bdaf-5d48-a8fc-163534709acc'}, {'abstract': 'Two picture books follow Paul Erdos and Albert Einstein on lifelong journeys of curiosity and scientific discovery.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/14/books/review/the-boy-who-loved-math-and-on-a-beam-of-light.html', 'snippet': 'Two picture books follow Paul Erdos and Albert Einstein on lifelong journeys of curiosity and scientific discovery.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When I was a kid, I could multiply two-digit numbers in my head. Take 48 and 54, for example. (The answer is 2,592.) I would visualize the numbers as though I were solving the problem in longhand. And I would almost always get the answer right. It’s a feat of concentration I find almost impossible today. There are just too many adult distractions: the flight I haven’t booked, the laundry I haven’t done, the out-of-town visitors I haven’t arranged for. Multiplication — isn’t there an app for that?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/0714-bks-Silver/0714-bks-Silver-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 532, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/0714-bks-Silver/0714-bks-Silver-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 532}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/books/review/071