ailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/12/fashion/20130712-BRIDESMAIDS-slide-G6AQ/20130712-BRIDESMAIDS-slide-G6AQ-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Your Outside-the-Box Bridesmaid Dresses', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weddings and Engagements', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dresses', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-12T16:18:15+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Fashion & Style', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'subsection_name': 'Weddings', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/ef5af13b-a45f-5883-a947-619ee9c6f0af', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/ef5af13b-a45f-5883-a947-619ee9c6f0af'}, {'abstract': 'In his new book, Jaron Lanier argues that many more middle-class jobs will disappear to intelligent-seeming machines.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/12/considering-the-horror-of-zuckerbergian-dystopias/', 'snippet': 'In his new book, Jaron Lanier argues that many more middle-class jobs will disappear to intelligent-seeming machines.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I wasted a substantial portion of a recent Friday night engaged in an argument with a “data scientist” I met at a strange party in Brooklyn. He was, naturally, working on something that struck me as being of questionable social and economic value, which was already being done by someone else: an algorithm that recommends Y.A. novels to young adults, or whomever, based on Y.A. novels they have already read. Eventually, somehow, the conversation turned to a discussion of whether technology is hollowing out the middle class and would continue to displace higher-skilled workers without generating enough economic growth to make up for the “disruption.” He found my concerns to be amusing. People can get work creating SEO-optimized niche blogs, he said. Or they can learn to code. And anyway, it won’t matter if software puts everyone out of work because poor people already don’t have to pay for anything — one of many fascinating, Randian canards he introduced to our conversation, which by this point had become less than civil.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/11/magazine/11-lanier/11-lanier-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 818, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/11/magazine/11-lanier/11-lanier-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 818}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/11/magazine/11-lanier/11-lanier-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 752, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/11/magazine/11-lanier/11-lanier-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1503, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/11/magazine/11-lanier/11-lanier-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/11/magazine/11-lanier/11-lanier-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/11/magazine/11-lanier/11-lanier-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Considering the Horror of Zuckerbergian Dystopias', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lanier, Jaron', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-12T16:19:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By WILLY STALEY', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Willy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'STALEY', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/20585177-c5a4-5a83-a18d-674532ba3389', 'word_count': 807, 'uri': 'nyt://article/20585177-c5a4-5a83-a18d-674532ba3389'}, {'abstract': 'Ben Brantley reviews “Children of the Sun” and “Dusa, Fish, Stash and Vi.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/12/london-theater-journal-change-in-the-air/', 'snippet': 'Ben Brantley reviews “Children of the Sun” and “Dusa, Fish, Stash and Vi.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nobody ever said the revolution was going to be tidy. This week I spent time in two worlds, separated by more than half a century and thousands of miles, in which the tremors of cataclysmic change were causing folks to make thorough messes of their lives. In the case of Maxim Gorky’s “Children of the Sun” (1905), at the National Theater, the setting is a roomy estate at the edge of a village in imperial Russia; in Pam Gems’s “Dusa, Fish, Stash and Vi” (1976), at the Finborough Theater, it’s a cramped London apartment in which four women consider lives with and without men. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'London Theater Journal: Change in the Air', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub'