name': 'Fashion & Style', 'subsection_name': 'Weddings', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lois Smith Brady', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lois', 'middlename': 'Smith', 'lastname': 'Brady', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f5bac2ba-2d4f-5b31-8914-64b8ebbcad81', 'word_count': 1391, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f5bac2ba-2d4f-5b31-8914-64b8ebbcad81'}, {'abstract': 'The Epipactis helleborine sprouts happily from patios, rock walls, driveways, tree pits, lawns, privet hedges and hosta beds across all five boroughs and beyond.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/12/an-orchid-disguised-as-a-weed/', 'snippet': 'The Epipactis helleborine sprouts happily from patios, rock walls, driveways, tree pits, lawns, privet hedges and hosta beds across all five boroughs and beyond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For those of us old enough to remember giving or receiving an orchid corsage, the concept of a “weed orchid” seems odd. First discovered in 1879 near Syracuse, the helleborine (Epipactis helleborine) was first thought to be a new species of North American orchid. This caused quite a stir among 19th-century botanists and orchid enthusiasts, but the plant was later identified as a Eurasian native with a history dating to mid-16th-century herbal lore as a cure for gout.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/nyregion/14NATURE1_SPAN/14NATURE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 263, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/14/nyregion/14NATURE1_SPAN/14NATURE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 263}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/nyregion/14NATURE1_SPAN/14NATURE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 449, 'width':