}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Keating, Charles H Jr', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Financial Times', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-12T17:16:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Floyd Norris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Floyd', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Norris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/93d4d31d-6135-506b-8138-5b76ff238dbc', 'word_count': 378, 'uri': 'nyt://article/93d4d31d-6135-506b-8138-5b76ff238dbc'}, {'abstract': 'The company issued a statement in response to questions about the views of Orson Scott Card, the writer of the book upon which the film “Ender’s Game” is based.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/13/movies/lionsgate-entertainment-statement-about-enders-game.html', 'snippet': 'The company issued a statement in response to questions about the views of Orson Scott Card, the writer of the book upon which the film “Ender’s Game” is based.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As proud longtime supporters of the LGBT community, champions of films ranging from “Gods and Monsters” to “The Perks of Being a Wallflower” and a company that is proud to have recognized same-sex unions and domestic partnerships within its employee benefits policies for many years, we obviously do not agree with the personal views of Orson Scott Card and those of the National Organization for Marriage. However, they are completely irrelevant to a discussion of “Ender’s Game.” The simple fact is that neither the underlying book nor the film itself reflect these views in any way, shape or form. On the contrary, the film not only transports viewers to an entertaining and action-filled world, but it does so with positive and inspiring characters who ultimately deliver an ennobling and life-affirming message. Lionsgate will continue its longstanding commitment to the LGBT community by exploring new ways we can support LGBT causes and, as part of this ongoing process, will host a benefit premiere for “Ender’s Game.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lionsgate Entertainment Statement About ‘Ender’s Game’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-07-12T17:19:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Text', '_id': 'nyt://article/af346fcd-e927-5da0-891c-67b04c837f2e', 'word_count': 165, 'uri': 'nyt://article/af346fcd-e927-5da0-891c-67b04c837f2e'}, {'abstract': 'On her 16th birthday, Malala Yousafzai spoke at the United Nations and called on world leaders to make education available and compulsory for every child.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/12/video-of-malala-yousafzai-at-u-n-calling-on-world-leaders-to-provide-education-to-every-child/', 'snippet': 'On her 16th birthday, Malala Yousafzai spoke at the United Nations and called on world leaders to make education available and compulsory for every child.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last Updated, Saturday, 11:19 p.m. In a speech at the United Nations on her 16th birthday, Malala Yousafzai, who was shot in the head by the Taliban for promoting education for girls in Pakistan, called on world leaders to provide “free, compulsory education” for every child. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Malala Yousafzai, Girl Shot by Taliban, Makes Appeal at U.N.', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Girl Shot by Taliban Makes Appeal at U.N.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub':