on our minds. My wife and I had never been to Rio, and we had a rather more prosaic agenda. Hers was to soak up the warmth and beauty of Rio’s beaches. Mine was to listen to music. Others can describe the glories of the beaches. Let me tell you about the music.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/travel/14PURSUITS7/14PURSUITS7-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/14/travel/14PURSUITS7/14PURSUITS7-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/travel/14PURSUITS7/14PURSUITS7-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/travel/14PURSUITS7/14PURSUITS7-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1364, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/travel/14PURSUITS7/14PURSUITS7-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/14/travel/14PURSUITS7/14PURSUITS7-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/travel/14PURSUITS7/14PURSUITS7-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Moving to the Rhythms of Rio', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Where Bossa Nova Is Just the Start', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rio de Janeiro (Brazil)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-12T17:36:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joe Nocera', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nocera', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d796e33a-250e-51e4-a94e-2c20aef7932f', 'word_count': 1336, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d796e33a-250e-51e4-a94e-2c20aef7932f'}, {'abstract': 'How the prisoners inside Richmond’s infamous Libby Prison reacted to post-Gettysburg southern propaganda.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/12/meades-army-annihilated/', 'snippet': 'How the prisoners inside Richmond’s infamous Libby Prison reacted to post-Gettysburg southern propaganda.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Outside, a heavy summer rainstorm thrashed the streets of Richmond, Va. Inside Libby Prison that afternoon — July 8, 1863 — hundreds of captured Union officers sprawled across the bare wooden floors of the converted tobacco warehouse, picking lice out of their underwear and passing around copies of Richmond newspapers. The news was grim for the Yankees: Gen. Robert E. Lee’s Confederate army had defeated Gen. George Meade’s Union troops at a Pennsylvania town called Gettysburg. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Meade’s Army Annihilated!’', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Newspapers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisoners of War', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Meade, George G', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Gettysburg (Pa)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Richmond (Va)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-12T17:38:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Carlson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carlson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3f0e026b-3850-5551-be85-d0709880cf89', 'word_count': 1504, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3f0e026b-3850-5551-be85-d0709880cf89'}, {'abstract': 'The Living Interfaith Church in Lynnwood, Wash., tries to move beyond an “us” and “them” mentality in worship.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/13/us/a-religion-that-embraces-all-religions.html', 'snippet': 'The Living Interfaith Church in Lynnwood, Wash., tries to move beyond an “us” and “them” mentality in worship.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LYNNWOOD, Wash. — Clad in proper Pacific Northwest flannel, toting a flask of “rocket fuel” coffee typical of Starbucks’ home turf, Steven Greenebaum rolled his Prius into a middle school parking lot one Sunday morning last month. Then he set about transforming its cafeteria into a sanctuary and himself into a minister.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/13/us/RELIGION-1/RELIGION-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 398, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/13/us/RELIGION-1/RELIGION-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 398}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype':