ests, and Riots', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-12T17:51:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Hubbard', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hubbard', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5f68e49a-363a-58ea-a289-2bb505a9d856', 'word_count': 984, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5f68e49a-363a-58ea-a289-2bb505a9d856'}, {'abstract': 'A guide to cultural and recreational events in the Hudson Valley.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/14/nyregion/events-in-westchester.html', 'snippet': 'A guide to cultural and recreational events in the Hudson Valley.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A guide to cultural and recreational events in the Hudson Valley. Items for the calendar should be sent at least three weeks in advance to westweek@nytimes.com.', 'print_section': 'WE', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Events in Westchester', 'kicker': 'Calendar', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Westchester', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Culture (Arts)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Westchester County (NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-12T17:53:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/abc8110e-e0ab-5ad1-bf42-18f0635244c6', 'word_count': 3695, 'uri': 'nyt://article/abc8110e-e0ab-5ad1-bf42-18f0635244c6'}, {'abstract': '“Nobody Loves You,” a musical at Second Stage Theater, is a semi-affectionate spoof of matchmaking reality television shows.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/14/theater/to-court-your-ex-try-a-reality-show.html', 'snippet': '“Nobody Loves You,” a musical at Second Stage Theater, is a semi-affectionate spoof of matchmaking reality television shows.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lately it’s been possible — if not always pleasurable — to turn on the television and watch musical theater, after a fashion. The guilty pleasures of  “Smash” are now fading memories, but the long-running “Glee” still carries the torch. Now theater is returning the favor by squeezing a little of the tube onto the stage: “Nobody Loves You,” a musical at Second Stage Theater, is a semi-affectionate spoof of matchmaking reality television shows like “The Bachelor,” in which contestants bare their souls while eliminating suitors for their love. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'To Court Your Ex, Try a Reality Show', 'kicker': 'Theater', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Court Your Ex, Try a Reality Show', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-12T17:55:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles Isherwood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Isherwood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6105e75f-9ca5-5643-9c5a-41ac499d4ca3', 'word_count': 196, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6105e75f-9ca5-5643-9c5a-41ac499d4ca3'}, {'abstract': 'After a revelation in the Season 2 finale of “Suits,” what next?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/14/arts/television/so-mikes-a-fraud-get-over-it.html', 'snippet': 'After a revelation in the Season 2 finale of “Suits,” what next?', 'lead_paragraph': 'After Mike Ross (Patrick J. Adams) revealed to Rachel Zane (Meghan Markle) in the Season 2 finale of “Suits” that he couldn’t write her a recommendation to Harvard Law School because he hadn’t actually gone there — “I’m a fraud,” he said — the result was not one but two swift slaps to his face, followed by steamy sex among the copying-room shelves. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'So, Mike’s a Fraud. Get Over It.', 'kicker': 'Television', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'So, Mike’s a Fraud. Get Over It.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-12T17:56:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/261dce48-c61e-503c-ad66-27a310940c07', 'word_count': 212, 'uri': 'nyt://article/261dce48-c61e-503c-ad66-27a310940c07'}, {'abstract': 'An exhibition in Nyack, N.Y., uses recently studied notes to provide context to Edward Hopper’s work.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/14/nyregion/my-dear-mr-hopper-the-story-starts-here-at-the-edward-hopper-house-art-center-in-nyack.html', 'snippet': 'An exhibition in Nyack, N.Y., uses recently studied notes to provide context to Edward Hopper’s work.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A stash of letters written by a secret girlfriend and unearthed a century later is a tantalizing discovery. When the recipient of those letters was a reclusive American painter whose work explored alienation and loneliness, such a find can shed new light on an entire oeuvre. ', 'print_section': 'WE', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/nyregion/14ARTSWE1_SPAN/14ARTSWE1_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 471, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/14/nyregion/14ARTSWE1_SPAN/14ARTSWE1_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 471}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/nyregion/14ARTSWE1_SPAN/14ARTSWE1_SPAN-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 805, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/nyregion/14ARTSWE1_SPAN/14ARTSWE1_SPAN-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 917, 'width': 1167, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/nyregion/14ARTSWE1_SPAN/14ARTSWE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/14/nyregion/14ARTSWE1_SPAN/14ARTSWE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/nyregion/14ARTSWE1_SPAN/14ARTSWE1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'New Insight Into Hopper in a Girlfriend’s Letters', 'kicker': 'Arts | Westchester', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New Insight Into Hopper in a Girlfriend’s Letters', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Nyack (NY)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Letters', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hopper House Art Center', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hopper, Edward', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-12T17:56:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susan Hodara', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hodara', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ba5d893d-d830-5605-bf77-09e837278596', 'word_count': 887, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ba5d893d-d830-5605-bf77-09e837278596'}, {'abstract': 'In “Broken,” opening on Friday, a young girl’s circumstances might prove overwhelming were it not for a luminous performance.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/14/movies/no-innocence-for-the-watchful.html', 'snippet': 'In “Broken,” opening on Friday, a young girl’s circumstances might prove overwhelming were it not for a luminous performance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s not easy being Skunk. Eleven years old and living in a nondescript circle of middle-class houses in England, she faces a battery of challenges. There’s her Type 1 diabetes; the teenage boy (George Sargeant) who meets her at a junkyard for flattery and flirtation; her au pair’s on-and-off boyfriend (Cillian Murphy), who also happens to be Skunk’s new teacher; and the family on her block whose mentally impaired son (Robert Emms) has been unfairly accused of molesting a neighborhood girl. In “Broken,” opening on Friday, Skunk’s circumstances might prove overwhelming were it not for a luminous performance by Eloise Laurence, who in her film debut conveys the character’s dimensions with remarkable range and nuance. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'No Innocence for the Watchful', 'kicker': 'Film', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'No Innocence For the Watchful', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-12T17:57:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Webster', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Webster', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7ba9fc32-6822-5ed2-be62-4422b112bbb0', 'word_count': 168, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7ba9fc32-6822-5ed2-be62-4422b112bbb0'}, {'abstract': 'On Friday night in the Bronx, the hometown king Jay-Z will take over Yankee Stadium for the first of two nights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/14/arts/music/hip-hop-titans-still-going-strong.html', 'snippet': 'On Friday night in the Bronx, the hometown king Jay-Z will take over Yankee Stadium for the first of two nights.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Friday night in the Bronx, the hometown king Jay-Z will take over Yankee Stadium for the first of two nights, a tag-team event with an again-ascendant Justin Timberlake. It’s a gimme, as these things go, an event too big to fail. By comparison, what’s taking place out at the Nikon at Jones Beach Theater on Long Island the same night — the "America’s Most Wanted Festival" — might feel small, but it’s the more intriguing statement about hip-hop now. The headliner is Lil Wayne, whose failures are more intriguing than Jay-Z’s and who’s been the more challenging hip-hop star over the last decade. That he has managed to continue making successful records even as his interest in rap has been in decline is a testament to his stubborn natural talent. He’ll be joined by, among others, T.I. and 2 Chainz, representing two generations of Atlanta-based hip-hop success. (A 6 p.m., 1000 Ocean Parkway, Wantagh, N.Y.; 516-221-1000, jonesbeach.com.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hip-Hop Titans, Still Going Strong', 'kicker': 'Pop', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hip-Hop Titans, Still Going Strong', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rap and Hip-Hop', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-12T17:58:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/761661f7-f17f-5c3e-ac0c-781a495772de', 'word_count': 158, 'uri': 'nyt://article/761661f7-f17f-5c3e-ac0c-781a495772de'}, {'abstract': 'For their series TAB, Stephanie Browne, a visual artist, and Kay Ottinger, a dancer, focus on a facet of a show that some producers of performance art ignore: the audience.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/14/arts/dance/performance-art-without-longueurs.html', 'snippet': 'For their series TAB, Stephanie Browne, a visual artist, and Kay Ottinger, a dancer, focus on a facet of a show that some producers of performance art ignore: the audience.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For their series TAB, Stephanie Browne, a visual artist, and Kay Ottinger, a dancer, focus on a facet of a show that some producers of performance art ignore: the audience. “It came out of being tired of performances that were really hard to watch,” Ms. Ottinger said in an interview. “We wanted to make an enjoyable way for people to watch artists’ work that is in progress.” The latest installment, part of the Brooklyn International Performance Art Festival, takes place at Brooklyn Fire Proof, with two free shows on Saturday. Audience members are led from one performance area to the next as several artists, including Kaia Gilje, Paige Fredlundand Leeza Meksin, show static and movement-based work. The series, which started last fall, makes watching experimental art a pleasure. But for its producers, TAB is also a research project dealing in perception: How does one guide others into watching multiple art forms at once? They have a magic touch. TAB, unpretentious and refreshing, is every bit a summer tonic. (Saturday, 4 and 6 p.m., doors open at 3; 119 Ingraham Street, at Porter Avenue, Bushwick; tabartbrooklyn.com.) ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Performance Art Without Longueurs', 'kicker': 'Dance', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Performance Art Without Longueurs', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Performance Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-12T17:58:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gia Kourlas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kourlas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e5def07a-feed-5a9d-9d62-c9e989c94c90', 'word_count': 185, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e5def07a-feed-5a9d-9d62-c9e989c94c90'}, {'abstract': 'Seventy-five years ago, the Museum of Modern Art presented its first solo photography exhibition, a show of pictures by Walker Evans titled “American Photographs.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/14/arts/design/let-us-now-praise-a-pioneering-show.html', 'snippet': 'Seventy-five years ago, the Museum of Modern Art presented its first solo photography exhibition, a show of pictures by Walker Evans titled “American Photographs.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Seventy-five years ago, the Museum of Modern Art presented its first solo photography exhibition, a show of pictures by Walker Evans titled “American Photographs.” Maybe Evans was the obvious choice to inaugurate MoMA’s photography programming, or maybe not. But it’s hard to think of many others who would have stood the test of time as well. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Let Us Now Praise a Pioneering Show', 'kicker': 'Art', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Let Us Now Praise A Pioneering Show', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Evans, Walker', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-12T17:59:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f0874715-74e2-58cf-a2ed-6b0701267940', 'word_count': 195, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f0874715-74e2-58cf-a2ed-6b0701267940'}, {'abstract': 'A guide to cultural and recreational events in Connecticut.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/14/nyregion/events-in-connecticut.html', 'snippet': 'A guide to cultural and recreational events in Connecticut.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A guide to cultural and recreational events in Connecticut. Items for the calendar should be sent at least three weeks in advance to ctcal@nytimes.com.', 'print_section': 'CT', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Events in Connecticut', 'kicker': 'Calendar', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Connecticut', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Culture (Arts)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Connecticut', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-12T18:00:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/3fa020be-a599-5253-9190-bdee11a7c737', 'word_count': 4144, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3fa020be-a599-5253-9190-bdee11a7c737'}, {'abstract': 'For those who love piano music, piano recitals and everything about the piano, Mannes College the New School for Music is again ready to gratify you.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/14/arts/music/a-festival-tunes-up-for-recitals-galore.html', 'snippet': 'For those who love piano music, piano recitals and everything about the piano, Mannes College the New School for Music is again ready to gratify you.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For those who love piano music, piano recitals and everything about the piano, Mannes College the New School for Music is again ready to gratify you. Sunday night is the kickoff of the popular two-week International Keyboard Institute and Festival, marking its 15th anniversary with daily recitals as well as lectures, master classes and a mini-competition. The opening recital features the institute’s founding director, Jerome Rose, who will play three favorite Beethoven sonatas: the “Pathètique,” the “Waldstein” and the “Appassionata.” ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Festival Tunes Up for Recitals Galore', 'kicker': 'Classical', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Festival Tunes Up For Recitals Galore', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-12T18:00:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f3f4cee2-c9a8-579d-a268-a7fc6f22aab4', 'word_count': 162, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f3f4cee2-c9a8-579d-a268-a7fc6f22aab4'}, {'abstract': 'The streaming-video Web site had been seeking bids for months. The owners — 21st Century Fox, the Walt Disney Company and NBCUniversal — instead decided not to sell.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/13/business/media/owners-of-hulu-call-off-sale-and-plan-to-invest-750-million.html', 'snippet': 'The streaming-video Web site had been seeking bids for months. The owners — 21st Century Fox, the Walt Disney Company and NBCUniversal — instead decided not to sell.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The owners of Hulu, having plotted a breakup twice now, have decided again to stay together — and as in many relationships of this sort, they are putting on a happy face and promising to work harder this time. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/13/business/Hulu1/Hulu1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 960, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/13/business/Hulu1/Hulu1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 960}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/13/business/Hulu1/Hulu1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 640, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'super