], 'pub_date': '2013-07-12T18:08:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Binyamin Appelbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Binyamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Appelbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/86e98c87-eff8-58f8-b101-6095d1457f7a', 'word_count': 1208, 'uri': 'nyt://article/86e98c87-eff8-58f8-b101-6095d1457f7a'}, {'abstract': 'A new poll indicates that most people think illegal immigrants should be allowed to remain.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/12/on-immigration-reform-let-them-stay/', 'snippet': 'A new poll indicates that most people think illegal immigrants should be allowed to remain.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A few numbers jumped out of the new Quinnipiac poll today that suggested David Brooks was right when he said that Republicans in the House may be committing “political suicide” by refusing to support a compromise Senate bill on immigration reform that does virtually everything they claim to want.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On Immigration Reform: Let Them Stay', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Illegal Immigration', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brooks, David', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-12T18:10:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Rosenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d69fd163-7ec7-5b83-ae78-9cf2b5caf83f', 'word_count': 333, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d69fd163-7ec7-5b83-ae78-9cf2b5caf83f'}, {'abstract': 'Through unconventional shapes and bright colors, “Art in Focus: St. Ives Abstraction” evokes the Cornish coastal town that inspired artists for years.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/14/nyregion/a-review-of-art-in-focus-st-ives-abstraction-at-yale-center-for-british-art.html', 'snippet': 'Through unconventional shapes and bright colors, “Art in Focus: St. Ives Abstraction” evokes the Cornish coastal town that inspired artists for years.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Like many another fishing village set amid craggy hills and picturesque coves, St. Ives, on England’s Cornish coast, reels in tourists. But unlike most of the others, St. Ives has made a specialty of tourists with paintbrushes and carving tools. And in a small but telling show at the Yale Center for British Art, “Art in Focus: St. Ives Abstraction,” the museum examines nearly two dozen works made in the middle of the last century in St. Ives, during the height of its fame as an artists’ colony.', 'print_section': 'CT', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/nyregion/14ARTSCT3/14ARTSCT3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 526, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/14/nyregion/14ARTSCT3/14ARTSCT3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 526}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/nyregion/14ARTSCT3/14ARTSCT3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 897, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/14/nyregion/14ARTSCT3/14A