ue': 'Editorials', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-13T01:00:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/0df3310b-9c8a-5666-b0f7-495fc8e2938e', 'word_count': 337, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0df3310b-9c8a-5666-b0f7-495fc8e2938e'}, {'abstract': 'By brutally stripping food aid from its farm bill, the House ended a tradition of decency.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/13/opinion/missing-the-food-stamp-program.html', 'snippet': 'By brutally stripping food aid from its farm bill, the House ended a tradition of decency.', 'lead_paragraph': '“We’ll get to that later.” That was the dismissive answer of Speaker John Boehner on Thursday, when asked if the House would restore the food stamp program it had just coldly ripped out of the farm bill. “Later,” he said, Republicans will deal with the nation’s most important anti-hunger program. “Later,” maybe, they will think about the needs of 47 million people who can’t afford adequate food, probably by cutting the average daily subsidy of $4.39. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Missing: The Food Stamp Program', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Missing: The Food Stamp Program', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Farm Bill (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food Stamps', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Agriculture and Farming', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crop Controls and Subsidies', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-13T01:00:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/19cd53c9-e03b-53e5-9863-f826360c6202', 'word_count': 489, 'uri': 'nyt://article/19cd53c9-e03b-53e5-9863-f826360c6202'}, {'abstract': 'A new derivatives deal falls short of what’s needed to protect American taxpayers and the global economy from the calamitous effects of reckless bank trades.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/13/opinion/reckless-banking-inadequate-rules.html', 'snippet': 'A new derivatives deal falls short of what’s needed to protect American taxpayers and the global economy from the calamitous effects of reckless bank trades.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gary Gensler, the reform-minded chairman of the Commodity Futures Trading Commission, got the best deal he could on Friday, when the commission voted 3 to 1 to approve guidance on how new rules on derivatives will apply internationally, as required under the Dodd-Frank financial reform law. But, in the face of unified opposition to strong “cross-border” regulation — from the big banks; their government allies in both the United States and Europe; and a swing-vote Democrat on the commission, Mark Wetjen — the deal falls short of what’s needed to protect American taxpayers and the global economy from the calamitous effects of reckless bank trades. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reckless Banking, Inadequate Rules', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Reckless Banking, Inadequate Rules', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dodd-Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer Protection Act (2010)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Derivatives (Financial Instruments)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gensler, Gary S', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Commodity Futures Trading Commission', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-13T01:00:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/f7f05fe4-e059-5f9c-98eb-6cb2d51a1088', 'word_count': 417, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f7f05fe4-e059-5f9c-98eb-6cb2d51a1088'}, {'abstract': 'Decades of roaring around the country in a van full of gear, beer and sweat didn’t prepare me for sitting at a computer all day, except maybe for the sitting part.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/12/theres-a-rock-star-in-my-office/', 'snippet': 'Decades of roaring around the country in a van full of gear, beer and sweat didn’t prepare me for sitting at a computer all day, except maybe for the sitting part.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I’m a victim/of the very song I sing.”— Candi Staton', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'There’s a Rock Star in My Office', 'kicker': 'Measure for Measure', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-13T01:00:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Holsapple', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holsapple', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5f2e1491-de17-5fe5-9572-b8bb3b10867a', 'word_count': 1443, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5f2e1491-de17-5fe5-9572-b8bb3b10867a'}, {'abstract': 'Apple’s pact with publishers raised prices in the short term, but it also brought much-needed competition to the marketplace.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/13/opinion/the-e-book-price-fixing-conspiracy.html', 'snippet': 'Apple’s pact with publishers raised prices in the short term, but it also brought much-needed competition to the marketplace.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It came as no surprise that a federal judge on Wednesday ruled against Apple in an e-book price-fixing case. The easy money was always on the Justice Department, which had evidence that Apple conspired with five major book publishers to manipulate the market. Yet it remains an open question whether these legal proceedings served consumers’ best interest. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The E-Book Price-Fixing Conspiracy', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The E-Book Price-Fixing Conspiracy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E-Books and Readers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prices (Fares, Fees and Rates)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Antitrust Laws and Competition Issues', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMAZON.COM INC', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Inc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Justice Department', 'rank'