, 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-07-14T08:32:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Global Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/34c967af-5746-51e2-b5f2-c72e6cc9f138', 'word_count': 9, 'uri': 'nyt://article/34c967af-5746-51e2-b5f2-c72e6cc9f138'}, {'abstract': '“Blackfish,” directed by Gabriela Cowperthwaite, and shown at the Sundance Film Festival in January, focuses on Tilikum, a killer whale that was involved in the death of a trainer at SeaWorld’s park in Orlando, Fla., in 2010.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/14/seaworld-entertainment-challenges-a-documentary-about-captive-orcas/', 'snippet': '“Blackfish,” directed by Gabriela Cowperthwaite, and shown at the Sundance Film Festival in January, focuses on Tilikum, a killer whale that was involved in the death of a trainer at SeaWorld’s park in Orlando, Fla., in 2010.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — In a sharp exchange over the weekend, SeaWorld Entertainment and the team behind “Blackfish,” a documentary about the dangers associated with captive orcas, traded charges about the validity of the film, which is set for release on Friday by Magnolia Pictures.“Blackfish” was directed by Gabriela Cowperthwaite, and was shown at the Sundance Film Festival in January. It particularly focuses on Tilikum, a killer whale that was involved in the death of a trainer, Dawn Brancheau, at SeaWorld’s park in Orlando, Fla., in 2010.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/15/arts/arts1/arts1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 347, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy':