life of armed America.', 'web_url': 'https://nocera.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/15/weekend-gun-report-july-12-14-2013/', 'snippet': 'A weekend in the life of armed America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Yes, the George Zimmerman verdict was reached on Saturday, and there is much to say about it—about the implications of the case regarding racial profiling, Stand Your Ground legislation, and our gun laws (or lack thereof). But many others have weighed in on these issues. So instead we are going to draw your attention to a story that broke early last week, which has gotten much less publicity.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Weekend Gun Report: July 12-14, 2013', 'kicker': 'Joe Nocera', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-07-15T15:55:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joe Nocera', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nocera', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/65a83672-d8bd-5523-96bd-06051e196503', 'word_count': 2299, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65a83672-d8bd-5523-96bd-06051e196503'}, {'abstract': 'Several books illuminate the causes of the financial crisis and its aftermath, according to an economist who was involved in the Treasury’s response to the events.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/15/financial-crisis-reading-list-2/', 'snippet': 'Several books illuminate the causes of the financial crisis and its aftermath, according to an economist who was involved in the Treasury’s response to the events.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Phillip Swagel is a professor at the School of Public Policy at the University of Maryland, and was assistant secretary for economic policy at the Treasury Department from 2006 to 2009.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Financial Crisis Reading List', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Subprime Mortgage Crisis', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sorkin, Andrew Ross', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Treasury Department', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-15T15:56:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Phillip Swagel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phillip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Swagel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/81329f3b-ce12-52b9-a140-b0f866ffa0b4', 'word_count': 1182, 'uri': 'nyt://article/81329f3b-ce12-52b9-a140-b0f866ffa0b4'}, {'abstract': 'You may have heard of Magic Squares, in which every row, column and diagonal sums to the same number. How about a Magic Double Hexagon?', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/15/hex/', 'snippet': 'You may have heard of Magic Squares, in which every row, column and diagonal sums to the same number. How about a Magic Double Hexagon?', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week’s puzzle is an original creation proposed by Russian-born American mathematician Nadejda E. Dyakevich. She introduces the puzzle this way:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nadejda Dyakevich’s Magic Double Hexagon', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mathematics', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Numbers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Puzzles', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-15T16:00:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gary Antonick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Antonick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c9b8df71-4d04-59e4-ba8c-1f35895aaa6c', 'word_count': 854, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c9b8df71-4d04-59e4-ba8c-1f35895aaa6c'}, {'abstract': 'Global banks will now have the choice of trading and clearing transatlantic swaps in either Europe or the United States.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/07/15/banks-dodge-a-bullett-with-deal-on-swaps/', 'snippet': 'Global banks will now have the choice of trading and clearing transatlantic swaps in either Europe or the United States.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A deal between American and European regulators on derivatives has helped banks dodge a bullet. Before a compromise hatched on July 11, the Commodity Futures Trading Commission intended to force swaps involving United States’ counterparties to be cleared in this country. That would have frozen cross-border flows and hammered volumes by creating separate regulatory jurisdictions.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Banks Dodge a Bullet With Deal on Swaps', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dodd-Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer Protection Act (2010)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Futures and Options Trading', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'},