 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sisario', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/39a49c7a-5556-5904-bde2-536cc2765b34', 'word_count': 606, 'uri': 'nyt://article/39a49c7a-5556-5904-bde2-536cc2765b34'}, {'abstract': 'Two recent decisions by appeals courts have upheld the discretion of judges to mete out long sentences for insider trading, even if this was not the common practice in similar cases.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/07/15/insider-traders-should-be-ready-to-do-hard-time/', 'snippet': 'Two recent decisions by appeals courts have upheld the discretion of judges to mete out long sentences for insider trading, even if this was not the common practice in similar cases.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The length of the sentences being meted out for insider trading have been on an upward trajectory lately, and recent decisions by federal appeals courts in Manhattan and Philadelphia will further that trend. The message some judges are trying to send is that being caught trading on confidential information can be very costly, far beyond the gains a defendant might realize.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Insider Traders Should Be Ready to Do Hard Time', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Courts and the Judiciary', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insider Trading', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sentences (Criminal)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goffer, Zvi', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rakoff, Jed S', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ropes & Gray', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-15T16:09:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter J. Henning', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Henning', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8f0d0b27-778a-5872-b7ef-92d9be484b6a', 'word_count': 1094, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8f0d0b27-778a-5872-b7ef-92d9be484b6a'}, {'abstract': 'A mix of hot and sweet peppers and “velveted” chicken makes for a delicious dish.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/18/health/stir-fried-chicken-with-mixed-sweet-and-hot-peppers-and-cashews.html', 'snippet': 'A mix of hot and sweet peppers and “velveted” chicken makes for a delicious dish.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I used green peppers only for this stir-fry. Try to use a mix of hot and sweet peppers, and feel free to use red, yellow or orange ones if you want to introduce some color. The chicken is “velveted” before stir-frying; a good name for this technique as the texture of the chicken remains velvety and moist after stir-frying.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/16/science/18recipehealth/18recipehealth-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 398, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/16/science/18recipehealth/18recipehealth-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 398}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/16/science/18recipehealth/18recipehealth-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 680, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/16/science/18recipehealth/18recipehealth-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1360, '