None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/55857593-6df5-5c0a-bed0-8fab69db071b', 'word_count': 796, 'uri': 'nyt://article/55857593-6df5-5c0a-bed0-8fab69db071b'}, {'abstract': 'Are there issues where the base needs to lose outright so that the party can win again?', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/15/is-immigration-reform-the-g-o-p-s-welfare-reform/', 'snippet': 'Are there issues where the base needs to lose outright so that the party can win again?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Since my Sunday column considered the paths that House Republicans might take on immigration if they don’t want to just embrace the Senate’s approach, it’s worth saying something about why\xa0— purely as a political question, with the policy merits to one side\xa0— it’s difficult for the G.O.P. to simply take a dive on the issue, go along with the elite consensus (their own elite included), and accept Rubio-Schumer and move on. Here let me quote The New Republic’s Nate Cohn, offering what I think is generally sensible advice to Republicans trying to rebrand their party without splintering it:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Welfare Reform Then, Immigration Reform Now?', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dream Act (Development, Relief and Education for Minors Act)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hispanic-Americans', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Welfare (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clinton, Bill', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Re