s a scene from the Roundabout Theater Company’s production of Steven Levenson’s new drama.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Steven Levenson’s new drama “The Unavoidable Disappearance of Tom Durnin,” the title character is a disgraced former securities attorney who attempts to reconcile with his family after serving a five-year prison sentence for fraud. In this scene, Tom’s son, James, played by Christopher Denham, confronts his father about the aftermath of his crime. The show continues through Aug. 25 at the Roundabout Theater Company’s Laura Pels Theater. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Performance: Christopher Denham of ‘The Unavoidable Disappearance of Tom Durnin’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-07-16T12:00:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Erik Piepenburg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erik', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Piepenburg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3b2cfe41-76e2-5958-bd81-aa14404db484', 'word_count': 130, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3b2cfe41-76e2-5958-bd81-aa14404db484'}, {'abstract': 'The volatility in Egypt is mitigated by the millions of ordinary people who have learned to take things one day at a time.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/16/looking-into-the-eye-of-horus/', 'snippet': 'The volatility in Egypt is mitigated by the millions of ordinary people who have learned to take things one day at a time.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CAIRO — My family and friends outside Egypt are worried about me because of the alarming footage and headlines they’ve seen. Egypt is descending into chaos; it’s “at the edge”; it’s a derailed train with “its front wheels dangling” over a ravine. I tell them I’m fine, that the Egypt portrayed in the media is only part of the picture.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Looking Into the Eye of Horus', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morsi, Mohamed', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Muslim Brotherhood (Egypt)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-16T12:01:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maria Golia', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maria', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Golia', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/db239980-258a-501e-8ac8-4b44b8353826', 'word_count': 815, 'uri': 'nyt://article/db239980-258a-501e-8ac8-4b44b8353826'}, {'abstract': 'As India’s public hospitals struggle with decaying infrastructure, Andhra Pradesh debates a populist health insurance program.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/16/hyderabad-debates-health-insurance-model-as-public-hospitals-decay/', 'snippet': 'As India’s public hospitals struggle with decaying infrastructure, Andhra Pradesh debates a populist health insurance program.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On February 21, during the afternoon rush hour in a busy shopping center in downtown Hyderabad in the southern state of Andhra Pradesh, two makeshift bombs packed with bolts and nails were detonated in one of the deadliest terrorist attacks in the city’s history. Over 100 people were injured by the blasts, and many were transported for treatment to Osmania General Hospital, the largest public hospital in the city.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/16/world/asia/16-injured-blast-IndiaInk/16-injured-blast-IndiaInk-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/16/world/asia/16-injured-blast-IndiaInk/16-injured-blast-IndiaInk-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/16/world/asia/16-injured-blast-IndiaInk/16-injured-blast-IndiaInk-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/16/world/asia/16-injured-blast-IndiaInk/16-injured-blast-IndiaInk-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/16/world/asia/16-injured-blast-IndiaInk/16-injured-blast-IndiaInk-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/16/world/asia/16-injured-blast-IndiaInk/16-injured-blast-IndiaInk-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/16/world/asia/16-injured-blast-IndiaInk/16-injured-blast-IndiaInk-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hyderabad Debates Health Insurance Model As Public Hospitals Decay', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Budgets and Budgeting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hospitals', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hygiene and Cleanliness', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rural Areas', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reddy, Y S Rajasekhara', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Indian National Congress', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hyderabad (India)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-16T12:27:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Cox', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cox', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b93dfd14-12b7-5f9e-aba7-9bc606eb8a5e', 'word_count': 1218, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b93dfd14-12b7-5f9e-aba7-9bc606eb8a5e'}, {'abstract': 'Metropolitan Diary: A peeved shopper apparently didn’t know the unofficial rules of the Loehmann’s communal dressing room.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/16/shopping-advice-at-loehmanns/', 'snippet': 'Metropolitan Diary: A peeved shopper apparently didn’t know the unofficial rules of the Loehmann’s communal dressing room.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Dear Diary:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Shopping Advice at Loehmann’s', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Loehmann's Inc", 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-16T12:30:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joellen Sommer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joellen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sommer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f31ecec2-b0fa-5db6-bb38-7b681d9e4f14', 'word_count': 166, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f31ecec2-b0fa-5db6-bb38-7b681d9e4f14'}, {'abstract': 'Goldman Sachs’s second-quarter profit doubles. | The Fabrice Tourre trial puts a spotlight on a tightknit network of legal players. | A new report suggests big banks’ high-minded words about ethics may largely be lip service. | Herbert M. Allison Jr., a former president of Merrill Lynch, dies at 69.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/07/16/morning-agenda-strength-at-goldman/', 'snippet': 'Goldman Sachs’s second-quarter profit doubles. | The Fabrice Tourre trial puts a spotlight on a tightknit network of legal players. | A new report suggests big banks’ high-minded words about ethics may largely be lip service. | Herbert M. Allison ...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Goldman Sachs posted second-quarter profit on Tuesday that was twice what the bank reported in the period a year earlier. The profit of $1.93 billion, or $3.70 a share, was well ahead of analysts’ expectations of $2.83 a share, according to Thomson Reuters. Revenue rose to $8.6 billion from $6.6 billion in the year-ago period. “The firm’s performance was solid, especially in the context of mixed economic sentiment during the quarter,” Goldman’s chairman and chief executive, Lloyd C. Blankfein, said in a statement. A conference call to discuss the results is being held at 9:30 a.m.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Strength at Goldman', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-07-16T12:37:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Alden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/798c605a-6065-59ae-8d41-9218934826de', 'word_count': 1802, 'uri': 'nyt://article/798c605a-6065-59ae-8d41-9218934826de'}, {'abstract': 'All Nippon Airways and Japan Airlines said emergency checks on their Boeing 787s turned up no threats after a fire last week in a 787 parked at Heathrow Airport.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/17/business/global/japan-carriers-find-no-787-problems.html', 'snippet': 'All Nippon Airways and Japan Airlines said emergency checks on their Boeing 787s turned up no threats after a fire last week in a 787 parked at Heathrow Airport.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TOKYO — All Nippon Airways and Japan Airlines, the world’s biggest operators of Boeing’s 787 Dreamliner, said on Tuesday that they had completed emergency checks on their fleet and found no threats to safety after a fire occurred inside a parked 787 jet at Heathrow Airport last week.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/17/business/Plane/Plane-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 368, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/17/business/Plane/Plane-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 368}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/17/business/Plane/Plane-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 628, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/17/business/Plane/Plane-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1257, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/17/business/Plane/Plane-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/17/business/Plane/Plane-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/17/business/Plane/Plane-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '2 Japanese Airlines Report No Problems With 787 ', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '2 Japanese Airlines Report No Problems With 787', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Drew, Christopher', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRLINES AND AIRPLANES', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Aviation Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ALL NIPPON AIRWAYS', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Japan Airlines', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Boeing Company', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-16T12:42:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hiroko Tabuchi and Christopher Drew', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hiroko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tabuchi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Drew', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/07c5755b-fe27-5b89-9671-4604ac9f1950', 'word_count': 624, 'uri': 'nyt://article/07c5755b-fe27-5b89-9671-4604ac9f1950'}, {'abstract': 'The Supreme Court had ordered the former president, Chun Doo-hwan, to repay money it said he had illegally accumulated in the 1980s.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/17/world/asia/prosecutors-raid-home-of-former-south-korean-president.html', 'snippet': 'The Supreme Court had ordered the former president, Chun Doo-hwan, to repay money it said he had illegally accumulated in the 1980s.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SEOUL, South Korea — State prosecutors equipped with metal detectors raided the Seoul residence of former President Chun Doo-hwan on Tuesday in a search for assets. Mr. Chun, the former military dictator, owes South Korea 167.5 billion won, or $150 million, in fines but claims to be broke.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Prosecutors Raid Home of Former South Korean President', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Prosecutors Raid Home of Former South Korean President', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Korea', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-16T12:50:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Choe Sang-Hun', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Choe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sang-Hun', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/047ee260-8f2f-5acc-ae52-729c9f3c42ed', 'word_count': 572, 'uri': 'nyt://article/047ee260-8f2f-5acc-ae52-729c9f3c42ed'}, {'abstract': 'The promotional campaign for the book “Carnivore” represents the latest and most extreme iteration of a troublesome publishing phenomenon.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/16/a-problematic-genre-the-war-on-terror-kill-memoir/', 'snippet': 'The promotional campaign for the book “Carnivore” represents the latest and most extreme iteration of a troublesome publishing phenomenon.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Iraq we had a sergeant who dipped Copenhagen while gunning on his tank and who kept an exact count of how many men he believed he had killed in battle. If I recall correctly, that number was 14, give or take a few. What I remember more vividly was his idea for the tattoo that he wanted to get after returning home: an American eagle clutching a sheaf of arrows. Each arrow would stand for one of the insurgents he had laid low in the streets of Baghdad.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Problematic Genre, the ‘Kill Memoir’', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Fatalities)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Iraq War (2003-11)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Veterans', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-16T13:00:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Van Reet', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': 'Van', 'lastname': 'Reet', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e1fd0433-5fe8-5608-930a-2aba28a198a9', 'word_count': 1541, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e1fd0433-5fe8-5608-930a-2aba28a198a9'}, {'abstract': 'Two new studies out of Europe have confirmed what researchers had long suspected: rates would fall and mental acuity improve as populations grew healthier and better educated.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/17/health/study-finds-dip-in-dementia-rates.html', 'snippet': 'Two new studies out of Europe have confirmed what researchers had long suspected: rates would fall and mental acuity improve as populations grew healthier and better educated.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A new study has found that dementia rates among people 65 and older in England and Wales have plummeted by 25 percent over the past two decades, to 6.2 percent from 8.3 percent, a trend that researchers say is probably occurring across developed countries and that could have major social and economic implications for families and societies.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/17/science/17dementia/17dementia-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/17/science/17dementia/17dementia-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/17/science/17dementia/17dementia-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subt