ject', 'value': 'Birth Rates', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gender', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Work-Life Balance', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Workplace Environment', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-16T16:12:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2dbde63e-c724-5ca4-8c60-950c2208289b', 'word_count': 1203, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2dbde63e-c724-5ca4-8c60-950c2208289b'}, {'abstract': 'There’s been more attention paid to his background as a Palestinian-American than his skills on the football field.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/16/the-online-defamation-of-oday-aboushi/', 'snippet': 'There’s been more attention paid to his background as a Palestinian-American than his skills on the football field.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If Oday Aboushi were just any Jets football recruit, New Yorkers and sportscasters would be discussing what contributions the fifth round draft pick might bring as an offensive lineman to the NFL team.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Online Defamation of Oday Aboushi', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Aboushi, Oday', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-16T16:15:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carol Giacomo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carol', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Giacomo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3b4c2121-0103-50b7-9e13-af7e1df02fc7', 'word_count': 342, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3b4c2121-0103-50b7-9e13-af7e1df02fc7'}, {'abstract': 'In July 1863, New York wasn’t the only city to get hit by mob violence.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/16/the-boston-draft-riots/', 'snippet': 'In July 1863, New York wasn’t the only city to get hit by mob violence.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Maj. Stephen Cabot must have expected the worst when he assumed command of the Massachusetts militia unit stationed at the Cooper Street Armory, in Boston’s North End, on the evening of July 14, 1863. Earlier that afternoon, federal officials trudging through neighborhoods around the city had delivered the first round of conscription notices to the homes of drafted men. Cabot, like many in Boston, was probably aware that rampaging crowds of draft resisters had stormed across New York City the previous day, looting, burning and killing along the way. There was a good chance, he knew, that Bostonians might greet their nation’s call to service in the same way. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Boston Draft Riots', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests and Riots', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Draft and Recruitment (Military)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Boston (Mass)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_da