jor': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "A Midsummer Night's Dream (Play)", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shakespeare, William', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Classical Theater of Harlem', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jah, Zainab', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-16T22:00:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anita Gates', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anita', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gates', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7840b702-9217-5a9e-8b77-dbe241a9128c', 'word_count': 377, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7840b702-9217-5a9e-8b77-dbe241a9128c'}, {'abstract': 'In its bid to buy Gardner Denver, Kohlberg Kravis Roberts hired the company’s former chief, Barry Pennypacker, to provide advice, raising questions of fairness.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/07/16/when-an-executive-turns-buyout-adviser-alarm-bells-go-off/', 'snippet': 'In its bid to buy Gardner Denver, Kohlberg Kravis Roberts hired the company’s former chief, Barry Pennypacker, to provide advice, raising questions of fairness.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Here’s one way to gain an edge in deal-making: hire the former chief executive of the company you want to buy to advise you.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When an Executive Turns Buyout Adviser, Alarm Bells Go Off', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When an Executive Turns Buyout Adviser, Alarm Bells Go Off', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Boards of Directors', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Conflicts of Interest', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Private Equity', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Gardner Denver Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kohlberg Kravis Roberts & Co', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-16T22:00:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Davidoff Solomon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': 'Davidoff', 'lastname': 'Solomon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/540c7032-745e-5182-a601-8a584b8f54f9', 'word_count': 1174, 'uri': 'nyt://article/540c7032-745e-5182-a601-8a584b8f54f9'}, {'abstract': '“Computer Chess” is set in 1980 at a gathering where computer programs created by nerds face human competition.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/17/movies/computer-chess-depicts-programmers-in-1980.html', 'snippet': '“Computer Chess” is set in 1980 at a gathering where computer programs created by nerds face human competition.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What was the world like before? That is the question every period movie contemplates and seeks to answer by means of historically appropriate props, costumes and habits of speech. An accurate depiction of the past will also involve a certain amount of subtraction, since one of the differences between us and our ancestors is that they didn’t have a lot of the stuff that we take for granted. Part of the fun for viewers lies in imagining (or remembering) how anyone managed without our helpful gadgets and gizmos.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Code-Writing Pioneers, It’s Your Turn', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Code-Writing Pioneers, It’s Your Turn', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bujalski, Andrew', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-16T22:06:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1919d100-a1a6-53f4-b39b-a222061be1d4', 'word_count': 610, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1919d100-a1a6-53f4-b39b-a222061be1d4'}, {'abstract': 'The health care giant reported earnings of $3.8 billion in the second quarter, aided in part by the sale of the company’s stake in Elan of Ireland.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/17/business/johnson-johnson-profit-rises-on-strong-prescription-sales.html', 'snippet': 'The health care giant reported earnings of $3.8 billion in the second quarter, aided in part by the sale of the company’s stake in Elan of Ireland.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Johnson & Johnson reported on Tuesday that its earnings more than doubled in the second quarter of this year, buoyed by strong sales of prescription drugs and an influx of cash from the sale of its stake in the Irish drug manufacturer Elan.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Johnson & Johnson Profit Rises on Strong Prescription Sales', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Strong Sales Underpin Earnings At J.&J.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Company Reports', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thomas, Katie', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Johnson & Johnson', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drugs (Pharmaceuticals)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Elan Corporation PLC', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-16T22:22:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katie Thomas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Thomas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e7e672da-281b-59b7-806e-16577e7a65f9', 'word_count': 252, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e7e672da-281b-59b7-806e-16577e7a65f9'}, {'abstract': 'A snail who thinks he can do great things is supercharged by ambition in “Turbo,” but there is a trail of big commercial interests in the story.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/17/movies/turbo-from-dreamworks-shows-one-way-to-the-finish-line.html', 'snippet': 'A snail who thinks he can do great things is supercharged by ambition in “Turbo,” but there is a trail of big commercial interests in the story.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Turbo” is a new movie from DreamWorks Animation Studios about a little snail with a big dream. It is also the latest animated feature to use an animal’s frustration with the limitations of his species as a metaphor for human aspirations. Much as the rat Remy in “Ratatouille” did not want to eat garbage, and Mumble the penguin in “Happy Feet” wanted to dance, so does Turbo — given name Theo, voice courtesy of Ryan Reynolds — long to move fast. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/17/arts/17turbo/17turbo-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 240, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/17/arts/17turbo/17turbo-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 240}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtyp