0', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d67b28b5-193d-5d11-9228-72a9916eef4a', 'word_count': 29, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d67b28b5-193d-5d11-9228-72a9916eef4a'}, {'abstract': 'Police agencies abide by a variety of policies on how long to retain license plate reader data and with whom to share it, posing privacy dangers, according to the American Civil Liberties Union.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/17/policies-on-license-plate-readers-vary-widely-says-a-c-l-u/', 'snippet': 'Police agencies abide by a variety of policies on how long to retain license plate reader data and with whom to share it, posing privacy dangers, according to the American Civil Liberties Union.', 'lead_paragraph': 'License plate readers have proliferated across the country, from the Hudson River Valley to San Francisco Bay. But cities and states are all over the map on how long they hold information they collect and with whom they share it. That is according to a new report from the American Civil Liberties Union, which collated license plate reader policies from nearly 300 law enforcement agencies around the country.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/18/business/17bits-licenses/17bits-licenses-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 426, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/18/business/17bits-licenses/17bits-licenses-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 426}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/18/business/17bits-licenses/17bits-licenses-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 727, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_nam