cument_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Quentin Hardy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Quentin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hardy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7864f6ce-c0c0-54f1-9662-748531e74c80', 'word_count': 615, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7864f6ce-c0c0-54f1-9662-748531e74c80'}, {'abstract': 'A handbag line to keep everything sorted.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/17/your-life-bagged-and-sorted/', 'snippet': 'A handbag line to keep everything sorted.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We like our tote bags to be big enough to hold everything, but dislike them when everything falls to the bottom and we can’t find the MetroCard. This is the kind of glitch Christine Park aims to fix with her new handbag line, Time’s Arrow. Ms. Park, a jack-of-all-trades in the fashion business (she was the C.O.O. of Rogan and worked in many capacities at Steven Alan), prizes function as much as a stylish look and sumptuous leather. Her Infinite Tote (above, $595) has a pocket for a 15-inch laptop, two other inside pockets, a diagonal exterior pocket and slots for pens and a passport. The smaller Infinite Mini (below, $395), a cross-body that’s great day to night, is sized to fit an iPad. Just think: no one ever waiting around while you dig for your keys.', 'print_section': 'E', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Your Life, Bagged and Sorted', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Your Life, Bagged and Sorted', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-17T20:32:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By ERICA M. BLUMENTHAL', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1c00b9f4-b0f2-5ce3-ba89-ba77fd469f62', 'word_count': 138, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1c00b9f4-b0f2-5ce3-ba89-ba77fd469f62'}, {'abstract': 'The slow spread of the illness, which has killed 45 people, mostly in the Middle East, has not prompted travel restrictions as of yet.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/18/health/mers-virus-not-global-emergency-health-officials-say.html', 'snippet': 'The slow spread of the illness, which has killed 45 people, mostly in the Middle East, has not prompted travel restrictions as of yet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The slow spread of a new virus that has killed 45 people, mostly in the Middle East, does not amount to a global health emergency, and no travel restrictions will be issued now, the World Health Organization said Wednesday.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'MERS Virus Not Global Emergency, Health Officials Say', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'MERS Virus Is Not Being Considered Global Emergency', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'MERS (Middle East Respiratory Syndrome)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McNeil, Donald G Jr', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Middle East', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'World Health Organization', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Saudi Arabia', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Viruses', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-17T20:57:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Donald G. McNeil Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Donald', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'McNeil', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7dfb24e7-89ee-58fc-b693-0686e01a3da6', 'word_count': 243, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7dfb24e7-89ee-58fc-b693-0686e01a3da6'}, {'abstract': 'Brogues and Oxfords arrive for fall.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/17/the-first-steps-toward-autumn/', 'snippet': 'Brogues and Oxfords arrive for fall.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As twisted as it sounds in this heat, fall merchandise is trickling into stores. Fur trim and chunky sweaters could make you gasp for air, but footwear is always tempting. Luckily, the first crop to hit the shelves includes a new wave of brogues and oxfords that also work with your light summer wear. The model Ming Xi, shown here, had the right idea in Paris, pairing burgundy Miu Miu shoes with her all-black ensemble, a look you could pull off with tights all year long. Wear the shoes now with tailored shorts or floaty dresses. Come fall, they’ll be well broken in and ready to add polish to jeans and a blazer. ', 'print_section': 'E', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The First Steps Toward Autumn', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The First Steps Toward Autumn', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shoes and Boots', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Miu Miu', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-17T20:59:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By ERICA M. BLUMENTHAL', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'org