-07-17T23:07:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Lasky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lasky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3eb98667-c511-551e-a454-7987d03e75be', 'word_count': 146, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3eb98667-c511-551e-a454-7987d03e75be'}, {'abstract': 'Sergio Rodrigues oversaw a colorful reissue of his Tajá outdoor-furniture collection.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/18/garden/seating-thats-not-for-wallflowers.html', 'snippet': 'Sergio Rodrigues oversaw a colorful reissue of his Tajá outdoor-furniture collection.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1978, the Brazilian designer Sergio Rodrigues created an extroverted furniture group called Tajá. Now 86, Mr. Rodrigues has overseen a reissue of the collection, which is being distributed by the New York design showroom Espasso. The group includes an armchair, dining chair, settee and bar stool, all made of eucalyptus wood certified by the Forest Stewardship Council. (Dining, coffee and side tables will be ready by the end of the year.) Each piece is available in a natural finish, or painted yellow, red, green, orange or blue. The wood versions are suitable for outdoor use, while the painted versions are recommended for covered outdoor areas only, to avoid sun fading. “In Brazil, a lot of social life revolves around the outdoors,” said Carlos Junqueira, Espasso’s founder. “Tajá reflects this spirit.” Prices range from $1,150 for the dining chair to $2,600 for the settee. Available at Espasso, 38 North Moore Street (Hudson Street), (212) 219-0017, espasso.com. \xa0', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/18/garden/18OUTDOORS_SPAN/18CURR7-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 308, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/18/garden/18OUTDOORS_SPAN/18CURR7-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 308}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/18/garden/18OUTDOORS_SPAN/18CURR7-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 525, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/18/garden/18OUTDOORS_SPAN/18CURR7-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1050, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/18/garden/18OUTDOORS_SPAN/18CURR7-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/18/garden/18OUTDOORS_SPAN/18CURR7-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/18/garden/18OUTDOORS_SPAN/18CURR7-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Seating That’s Not for Wallflowers', 'kicker': 'Currents | Outdoors', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Seating Not Meant for Wallflowers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brazil', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rodrigues, Sergio', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Furniture', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-17T23:07:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Milioti', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Milioti', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f39fea41-ec58-5e2e-858e-9aa83faa63f2', 'word_count': 156, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f39fea41-ec58-5e2e-858e-9aa83faa63f2'}, {'abstract': 'The architect unveils a prototype house for Vitra.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/18/garden/renzo-pianos-dream-of-a-tiny-house.html', 'snippet': 'The architect unveils a prototype house for Vitra.', 'lead_paragraph': '“When I was a student, in the ’60s, I dreamt of making a house 7 feet by 7 feet, as a dream of freedom, of self-moderation,” said Renzo Piano, the Pritzker Prize-winning architect. In June, Mr. Piano, 75, surpassed those dimensions, but only slightly, with Diogene, a prototype house for the furniture company Vitra that is 8.2 feet wide by 9.8 feet long by 11.5 feet high. The aluminum-clad wood house features two rainwater tanks attached to a boiler, a composting toilet, triple-glazed windows and insulated walls that can withstand temperatures from minus 10 degrees to 105 degrees Fahrenheit. Electricity comes from three rechargeable batteries that plug into an external power source. Diogene is expected to be produced in three years and will cost about $45,000. A deluxe model with rooftop photovoltaic panels will be about $75,000. The prototype can be seen on Vitra’s campus in Weil am Rhein, Germany. Information: vitra.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/18/garden/18ROOMS2_SPAN/18CURR2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 367, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/18/garden/1