{'abstract': 'Don’t let Rio’s picture-perfection fool you: Its waters are foul.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/18/a-river-runs-over-with-it/', 'snippet': 'Don’t let Rio’s picture-perfection fool you: Its waters are foul.', 'lead_paragraph': 'RIO DE JANEIRO — One of the perks of living in this city is taking your morning jog through its postcard beauty. I usually start around the Sugar Loaf, a granite monolith that juts out of the western rim of Guanabara Bay. I pass the palm trees along Flamengo Beach, sending up clusters of green and gray parakeets, and then turn and run toward the statue of Christ the Redeemer, which rises from a sheer boulder nestled in mountains. The final stretch takes me over a walkway that straddles the Carioca, a river that runs down those peaks into the sea at Flamengo. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A River Runs Over With It', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Infrastructure (Public Works)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Population', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rain', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rivers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sewers and Sewage', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Waste Materials and Disposal', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Water', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brazil', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Portugal', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rio de Janeiro (Brazil)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-18T16:16:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Juliana Barbassa', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Juliana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barbassa', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a95b49d5-e546-5610-b27b-532934b93c10', 'word_count': 597, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a95b49d5-e546-5610-b27b-532934b93c10'}, {'abstract': 'Amanda Plummer and Brad Dourif take on “The Two-Character Play” by Tennessee Williams.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/21/theater/amanda-plummer-and-brad-dourif-onstage.html', 'snippet': 'Amanda Plummer and Brad Dourif take on “The Two-Character Play” by Tennessee Williams.', 'lead_paragraph': '“There’s no such thing as an inescapable corner with two people in it,” the actor and playwright Felice tells his sister, Clare, an actress, late in the second act of Tennessee Williams’s dense absurdist farrago “The Two-Character Play.” That pithy aphorism rings out from the play’s current Off Broadway production at New World Stages, and not only because it aptly describes the professional and spiritual dead end in which this damaged brother-and-sister team, abandoned in a decrepit theater by a touring company, conjures seemingly infinite variations of role playing and shape shifting. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/arts/21TWOCHARACTER1_SPAN/21TWOCHARACTER1_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/21/arts/21TWOCHARACTER1_SPAN/21TWOCHARACTER1_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/arts/21TWOCHARACTER1_SPAN/21TWOCHARACTER1_SPAN-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'wid