19-25', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Listings', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comedy and Humor', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-18T22:07:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/06e0a560-e85d-504c-a5b6-4d304e5242cb', 'word_count': 341, 'uri': 'nyt://article/06e0a560-e85d-504c-a5b6-4d304e5242cb'}, {'abstract': 'Is art made better by nature? The New York Times’s culture critics wade in.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/19/arts/playing-outside-the-box.html', 'snippet': 'Is art made better by nature? The New York Times’s culture critics wade in.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ART alfresco is such a staple of the city summer that sometimes it seems as if no park, parking lot, rooftop, field or front stoop is free of it. There are tubas under the trees, giant sculptures plopped onto noisy median strips, rhymed couplets recited into the wind. This might not be a good time to ask — since right about now, most of us just want to lean our damp faces into a big box blowing B.T.U.’s — but really, what is so great about the great outdoors? Is art made better by nature? Does it compete with nature? Is it hopelessly diluted by it? When the walls come down, is it more about the picnic blanket, the kids, the Big Dipper and the gamboling puppy than about the art? We asked some of The New York Times’s culture critics to wade in.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/19/arts/19OUTDOOR_ILLO/19OUTDOOR_ILLO-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 922, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/19/arts/19OUTDOOR_ILLO/1