c colleges and universities have sent a letter to the House speaker, John A. Boehner, and the minority leader, Nancy Pelosi, urging them to pass a comprehensive immigration bill that includes a path to citizenship for undocumented immigrants. Roman Catholic bishops have been lobbying hard for such a bill, but the college presidents’ organized involvement is new. They announced on Thursday that they plan to personally call their members of Congress and will hold events with local church leaders to get Catholic laypeople to pressure their representatives to pass a bill. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Catholic Educators Push Immigration Bill', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Washington', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Catholic Educators Push Immigration Bill', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Roman Catholic Church', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boehner, John A', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pelosi, Nancy', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-19T04:15:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Laurie Goodstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Laurie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goodstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/42caa3de-e6a2-5557-a207-577d0368dce3', 'word_count': 95, 'uri': 'nyt://article/42caa3de-e6a2-5557-a207-577d0368dce3'}, {'abstract': 'A Massachusetts State Police sergeant released images from the capture of the Boston Marathon bombing suspect, Dzhokhar Tsarnaev.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/20/us/graphic-photos-of-tsarnaev-capture-released.html', 'snippet': 'A Massachusetts State Police sergeant released images from the capture of the Boston Marathon bombing suspect, Dzhokhar Tsarnaev.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A Massachusetts State Police sergeant was relieved of duty Thursday after supplying Boston Magazine with graphic images from the capture of the Boston Marathon bombing suspect, Dzhokhar Tsarnaev, the editor of the magazine said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Graphic Photos of Tsarnaev Capture Released', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Graphic Photos of Tsarnaev Capture Released', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Boston Marathon Bombings (2013)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tsarnaev, Dzhokhar A', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Rolling Stone', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Boston Magazine', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tsarnaev, Tamerlan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Massachusetts', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murphy, Sean', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-19T04:22:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ravi Somaiya', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ravi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Somaiya', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4b7c2535-6176-5d1f-9a0b-e079b8bfc54b', 'word_count': 288, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4b7c2535-6176-5d1f-9a0b-e079b8bfc54b'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/19/arts/television/whats-on-friday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '8 P.M. (TCM) FRIDAY NIGHT SPOTLIGHT: THE FILMS OF FRANÇOIS TRUFFAUT A monthlong tribute to this film critic turned director, and mainstay of the French New Wave, continues with “The Soft Skin” (1964). The movie shows Alfred Hitchcock’s influence on Truffaut, and his own preoccupation with the romantic triangle, through the story of a literary scholar (Jean Desailly), his wife (Nelly Benedetti) and the flight attendant (Françoise Dorléac) who comes between them. The lineup continues at 10 with “Jules and Jim” (1962), adapted from Henri-Pierre Roché’s 1953 semi-autobiographical novel and starring Jeanne Moreau as a tempestuous beauty who drives a wedge between college friends (Henri Serre, above center, and Oskar Werner, with Ms. Moreau). In “Two English Girls” (1972), at midnight, a young writer (Jean-Pierre Léaud, who served as Truffaut’s alter ego as a young man) carries on love affairs with two sisters (Kika Markham and Stacey Tendeter). Truffaut shares the directing credit with Jean-Luc Godard in “A Story of Water” (1961), a short film inspired by a severe flood in Paris, at 2:15 a.m. And in “The Woman Next Door” (1981), at 2:30, former lovers (Gérard Depardieu, whom Truffaut chose as his stand-in during his later years, and Fanny Ardant, Truffaut’s real-life love at the time) find themselves unexpectedly reunited after seven years as neighbors in a French village. The marathon wraps at 4:30 with “The Man Who Loved Women” (1977), with Charles Denner as an inveterate womanizer (the beauties are portrayed by Brigitte Fossey, Nelly Borgeaud, Geneviève Fontanel and Leslie Caron, among others) who tries to make sense of his problems by writing his memoirs.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On Friday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Friday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-07-19T05:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/c1d0e71d-0574-5869-983d-b09306d939fc', 'word_count': 725, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c1d0e71d-0574-5869-983d-b09306d939fc'}, {'abstract': 'Saratoga Race Course has been the site of victories by celebrated thoroughbreds like Rachel Alexandra as well as the scene of Man o’ War’s only defeat.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2013/07/19/sports/19saratogatimeline.html', 'snippet': 'Saratoga Race Course has been the site of victories by celebrated thoroughbreds like Rachel Alexandra as well as the scene of Man o’ War’s only defeat.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Saratoga Race Course has been the site of victories by celebrated thoroughbreds like Rachel Alexandra as well as the scene of Man o’ War’s only defeat.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/19/sports/19saratogatimeline7-img/19saratogatimeline7-img-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/19/sports/19saratogatimeline7-img/19saratogatimeline7-img-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/19/sports/19saratogatimeline7-img/19saratogatimeline7-img-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683,