': '\xa0', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The 7.7.13 Issue', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The 7.7.13 Issue', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-07-19T14:31:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9d1b6618-1f5c-5aa1-a409-baf303d2dcfb', 'word_count': 773, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9d1b6618-1f5c-5aa1-a409-baf303d2dcfb'}, {'abstract': 'The 20th-century tool for 20th-century dos.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/21/magazine/who-made-that-hair-dryer.html', 'snippet': 'The 20th-century tool for 20th-century dos.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1888, Alexandre-Ferdinand Godefroy, a French coiffeur inventeur  — that’s hairstylist inventor — patented the hair dryer’s earliest ancestor. The contraption was to be hooked up “to any suitable form of heater,” which would send hot air through a pipe to a dome surrounding the woman’s head. The device was impressively multipurpose: to use it for shampooing, Godefroy suggested removing all pieces except one resembling the brim of a sombrero, which fastened around a woman’s head to redirect suds into the sink. (“Still another use to which the device may be applied,” Godefroy wrote, “is that of shaping wig-holders.”)', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/magazine/21wmt/mag-21WMT-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/21/magazine/21wmt/mag-21WMT-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/magazine/21wmt/mag-21WMT-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/magazine/21wmt/mag-21WMT-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/magazine/21wmt/mag-21WMT-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/21/magazine/21wmt/mag-21WMT-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/magazine/21wmt/mag-21WMT-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Made That Hair Dryer?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Who Made That? (Hair Dryer)', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Inventions and Patents', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gross, Jessica', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hair', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Godefroy, Alexandre-Ferdinand', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-19T14:31:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jessica Gross', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jessica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gross', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Series', '_id': 'nyt://article/547f8fab-95a8-54a0-b64e-366a740cd52c', 'word_count': 558, 'uri': 'nyt://article/547f8fab-95a8-54a0-b64e-366a740cd52c'}, {'abstract': 'A haunting summer afternoon in Central Park.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/21/magazine/i-jumped-in-to-save-the-child-but-who-would-rescue-me.html', 'snippet': 'A haunting summer afternoon in Central Park.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the distance, a small, black triangle rose from the surface of the pond, then shifted slightly. “Look at the turtle,” I said to my son, who was 9 at the time. On that late-summer afternoon, we had ridden our bicycles to one of our favorite spots in Central Park. Just west of Cleopatra’s Needle, and across from the statue of a mounted Polish warrior (arms raised, swords crossed), lies a sylvan vista called Turtle Pond — a small body of water, its rocky shore ringed with greenery. It was perpetually alluring to my urban child, even in that season when the water was covered with a thick carpet of bilious algae.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '54', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/magazine/21lives/mag-21lives-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 255, 'width': 439, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/magazine/21lives/mag-21lives-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 255, 'width': 439, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/magazine/21lives/mag-21lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/21/magazine/21lives/mag-21lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/magazine/21lives/mag-21lives-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'I Jumped In to Save the Child. But Who Would Rescue Me?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Plunge', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Camhi, Leslie', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Central Park (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rescues', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-19T14:31:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Leslie Camhi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Leslie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Camhi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6345e1ed-fcc5-5c40-89c7-808e2ddcfe7d', 'word_count': 778, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6345e1ed-fcc5-5c40-89c7-808e2ddcfe7d'}, {'abstract': 'How competitive is too competitive?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/21/magazine/im-good-im-really-good-damn-im-good.html', 'snippet': 'How competitive is too competitive?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Five days before my father died, I beat him at Ping-Pong. The table was in the lower level of Toronto’s Art Gallery of Ontario, part of a children’s art center where we could deposit my son for a convenient half-hour. Some administrator at the gallery is obviously a genius: it’s clear to me now that every art gallery in the world should have some means available for playing table tennis. Hitting little white balls with miniature paddles over a waist-high table turns out to be the perfect conclusion to a day spent looking at paintings with rambunctious children in tow.',