 chairs and a hotel-like concierge desk in the airy, glass-walled lobby. “I love the gym. I love the swimming pool on the roof!”', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/realestate/21WHAT1_SPAN/WHAT-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 407, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/21/realestate/21WHAT1_SPAN/WHAT-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 407}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/realestate/21WHAT1_SPAN/WHAT-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 694, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/realestate/21WHAT1_SPAN/WHAT-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1388, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/realestate/21WHAT1_SPAN/WHAT-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/21/realestate/21WHAT1_SPAN/WHAT-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/realestate/21WHAT1_SPAN/WHAT-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Amy Arbus’s One-Woman Show With a View', 'kicker': 'What I Love', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A One-Woman Show With a View', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Arbus, Amy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Greenwich Village (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-19T15:10:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dan Shaw', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shaw', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/27931576-ba7b-5e61-99c9-5586b01a2119', 'word_count': 1116, 'uri': 'nyt://article/27931576-ba7b-5e61-99c9-5586b01a2119'}, {'abstract': 'The decision comes after a dispute between museums that hold paintings that were in the Museum of New Western Art.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/19/russian-government-decides-against-recreating-museum-shut-by-stalin/', 'snippet': 'The decision comes after a dispute between museums that hold paintings that were in the Museum of New Western Art.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MOSCOW — The Russian government has decided against reuniting two prerevolutionary art collections that include works by Picasso and Matisse. The decision sets to rest, for now at least, a dispute that was addressed directly to President Vladimir V. Putin on live national television and prompted a monthslong war of words between the State Pushkin Museum of Fine Arts in Moscow and the State Hermitage Museum in St. Petersburg. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Russian Government Decides Against Recreating Museum Shut by Stalin', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hermitage Museum (Saint Petersburg, Russia)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pushkin Museum of Fine Arts', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-19T15:23:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sophia Kishkovsky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sophia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kishkovsky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2dd071c8-0ae2-5da4-aaf9-8ea6c71084d7', 'word_count': 503, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2dd071c8-0ae2-5da4-aaf9-8ea6c71084d7'}, {'abstract': 'William Boyd, the author of a forthcoming novel starring James Bond, says the literary character is “a far more complex, nuanced and intriguing figure than his celluloid counterpart.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/21/books/review/007-goes-rogue.html', 'snippet': 'William Boyd, the author of a forthcoming novel starring James Bond, says the literary character is “a far more complex, nuanced and intriguing figure than his celluloid counterpart.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'William Boyd, who reviews Henning Mankell’s latest novel in this issue, was a teenager when he first read Ian Fleming’s James Bond novels. “The thrill of reading them was almost illicit — they seemed so glamorous and daring,” he told me in an e-mail interview. “The feeling I experienced was that you were being given access to a world known and enjoyed by a very, very few. The ‘members only’ sign had been removed.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/21openbook-sub/21openbook-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 213, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/21openbook-sub/21openbook-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 213}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/21openbook-sub/21openbook-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 307, 'width': 864, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/21openbook-sub/21openbook-sub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 307, 'width': 864, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/21openbook-sub/21openbook-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/21openbook-sub/21openbook-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/21openbook-sub/21openbook-sub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '007 Goes Rogue', 'kicker': 'Open Book', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '007 Goes Rogue', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-19T15:25:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/55d97faf-8727-5bfa-9aa1-4c4f04b7c165', 'word_count': 383, 'uri': 'nyt://article/55d97faf-8727-5bfa-9aa1-4c4f04b7c165'}, {'abstract': 'A writer relies on skills he learned as a hunter.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/21/books/review/kill-your-darlings.html', 'snippet': 'A writer relies on skills he learned as a hunter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As an avid big-game hunter and a professional writer, I’m often asked how I reconcile these seemingly contradictory passions. It’s easy to imagine what people have in mind: the contemplative author in a lamp-lit study juxtaposed with the wilderness brute, arms sunk to the elbows in animal viscera. While I recognize the gulf that separates these two images, the skills required for hunting and writing are remarkably similar, even complementary.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/10/books/review/0721-bks-RINELLA/0721-bks-RINELLA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 868, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/10/books/review/0721-bks-RINELLA/0721-bks-RINELLA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 868}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/10/books/review/0721-bks-RINELLA/0721-bks-RINELLA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 709, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/10/books/review/0721-bks-RINELLA/0721-bks-RINELLA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1417, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/10/books/review/0721-bks-RINELLA/0721-bks-RINELLA-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/10/books/review/0721-bks-RINELLA/0721-bks-RINELLA-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/10/books/review/0721-bks-RINELLA/0721-bks-RINELLA-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Kill Your Darlings', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Kill Your Darlings', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rinella, Steven', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hunting and Trapping', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-19T15:25:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Rinella', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rinella', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab377c6a-e918-5436-a974-b37755dc05e3', 'word_count': 1097, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab377c6a-e918-5436-a974-b37755dc05e3'}, {'abstract': 'Secretary of State John Kerry said Israel and the Palestinian Authority had agreed to join him in Washington in hopes of resuming formal talks for the first time since 2010.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/20/world/middleeast/kerry-extends-stay-in-mideast-to-push-for-talks.html', 'snippet': 'Secretary of State John Kerry said Israel and the Palestinian Authority had agreed to join him in Washington in hopes of resuming formal talks for the first time since 2010.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AMMAN, Jordan — Israeli and Palestinian leaders have “established a basis” to resume direct peace negotiations for the first time in three years, Secretary of State John Kerry announced Friday, after an intense round of shuttle diplomacy aimed at reviving the dormant Middle East peace process.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/20/world/diplo/diplo-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/20/world/diplo/diplo-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/20/world/diplo/diplo-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/20/world/diplo/diplo-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/20/world/diplo/diplo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/20/world/diplo/diplo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/20/world/diplo/diplo-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Kerry Achieves Deal to Revive Mideast Talks', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Kerry Achieves Deal to Revive Mideast Talks', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kerry, John', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gordon, Michael R', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rudoren, Jodi', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Palestinians', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ABBAS, MAHMOUD', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Netanyahu, Benjamin', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-19T15:26:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael R. Gordon and Jodi Rudoren', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Gordon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Jodi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rudoren', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a815e59f-634e-5a64-ac62-9617e22d2c75', 'word_count': 1334, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a815e59f-634e-5a64-ac62-9617e22d2c75'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/21/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MY LUNCHES WITH ORSON: Conversations Between Henry Jaglom and Orson Welles,  edited by Peter Biskind. (Metropolitan/Holt, $28.) These chummy, rambling chats, recorded in Welles’s twilight, roam from chicken salad to chic romantic capers.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-19T15:27:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/246ac990-cb59-506d-9091-f0679068c4a4', 'word_count': 275, 'uri': 'nyt://article/246ac990-cb59-506d-9091-f0679068c4a4'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/21/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SHINE SHINE SHINE,  by Lydia Netzer. (St. Martin’s Griffin, $14.99.) For the picture-\xadperfect couple in Netzer’s finely textured first novel, “normal” is an illusion. Maxon Mann, a Nobel Prize-winning scientist, is on a mission to populate the Moon with robots, but his safe return is far from certain. Meanwhile, back home, Maxon’s conflicted (and heavily pregnant) wife, Sunny, has been left to care for their autistic 4-year-old son and her dying mother.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-19T15:27:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/9a61fd6a-67ee-5ad3-b4de-a3e3d3124dc0', 'word_count': 489, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9a61fd6a-67ee-5ad3-b4de-a3e3d3124dc0'}, {'abstract': 'Ivy Pochoda’s “Visitation Street” is a mystery about a disappearance, set against the changing neighborhood of Red Hook, Brooklyn.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/21/books/review/ivy-pochodas-visitation-street-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'Ivy Pochoda’s “Visitation Street” is a mystery about a disappearance, set against the changing neighborhood of Red Hook, Brooklyn.', 'lead_paragraph': 'People can never tell the exact moment their old neighborhood disappears. One day, everybody knows where you came from and where you’re going; the next, you don’t recognize a soul. VISITATION STREET (Dennis Lehane/Ecco/HarperCollins, $25.99), a powerfully beautiful novel by Ivy Pochoda, lingers on the moment the \xadworking-class neighborhood of Red Hook, Brooklyn, changed forever: the night 15-year-old June Giatto went out into the Upper Bay in a pink raft with her best friend, Val Marino, and never returned. Nothing was the same after that: developers discovered the historic waterfront, initiating a gentrification that was swift and unstop-pable. Even the longshoremen’s bars had their history “buffed and polished away.” But on the night June disappeared, families still lived in the shabby row houses and worked at blue-collar jobs while their children dreamed of sailing away on a pink raft.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/0721-bks-crime/0721-bks-crime-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 722, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/0721-bks-crime/0721-bks-crime-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 722}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/0721-bks-crime/0721-bks-crime-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 852, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/0721-bks-crime/0721-bks-crime-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1906, 'width': 1584, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/0721-bks-crime/0721-bks-crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/0721-bks-crime/0721-bks-crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/0721-bks-crime/0721-bks-crime-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Watery Graves', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Watery Graves', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pochoda, Ivy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Burke, James Lee', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'James, Bill', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Walker, Martin', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-19T15:28:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marilyn Stasio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marilyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stasio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/14d396b5-e0be-5f71-8421-25b7257ce92a', 'word_count': 909, 'uri': 'nyt://article/14d396b5-e0be-5f71-8421-25b7257ce92a'}, {'abstract': 'The Bank for International Settlements, a central bank for bankers, operates without disclosure.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/21/books/review/tower-of-basel-by-adam-lebor.html', 'snippet': 'The Bank for International Settlements, a central bank for bankers, operates without disclosure.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Adam LeBor’s history of the Bank for International Settlements, “Tower of Basel,” reads a little like a financial version of “Rosencrantz and Guildenstern Are Dead,” the Tom Stoppard play that places two minor characters in “Hamlet” in the forefront of the action while the drama’s major events unfold incomprehensibly in the background. In LeBor’s telling, the B.I.S., an obscure “bank for central banks” set up in Basel, Switzerland, in 1930 to facilitate World War I reparations payments from Germany, has been a critical, if secretive, actor in the global economy for more than 80 years. Today, he writes, it is “the most important bank in the world,” an institution with virtually no accountability, and yet “for decades it has stood at the center of a global network of money, power and covert global influence.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/0721-bks-HIRSH/0721-bks-HIRSH-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 377, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/0721-bks-HIRSH/0721-bks-HIRSH-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 377}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/0721-bks-HIRSH/0721-bks-HIRSH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 643, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/0721-bks-HIRSH/0721-bks-HIRSH-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1287, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/0721-bks-HIRSH/0721-bks-HIRSH-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/0721-bks-HIRSH/0721-bks-HIRSH-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/0721-bks-HIRSH/0721-bks-HIRSH-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'They’ve Got a Secret', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'They’ve Got a Secret', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'LeBor, Adam', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bank for International Settlements', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-19T15:28:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Hirsh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hirsh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d3fb714f-b389-5d55-b165-d256d084a1be', 'word_count': 1148, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d3fb714f-b389-5d55-b165-d256d084a1be'}, {'abstract': 'The formidable doyenne of 19th-century expatriate Florence.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/21/books/review/queen-bee-of-tuscany-by-ben-downing.html', 'snippet': 'The formidable doyenne of 19th-century expatriate Florence.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Janet Ross was one of those Lady Bracknell-like dragons for whom the word “formidable” was practically invented. Born in London in 1842, she spent the last six decades of her life — from 1867 until her death in 1927 — as an increasingly commanding personage in the Anglo-Tuscan colony around Florence, where she was known (and feared) as Aunt Janet. Ben Downing freely acknowledges near the beginning of “Queen Bee of Tuscany” that Ross “had her limitations. Though intelligent and learned, especially for an autodidact, she was by no means brilliant. She had little imagination or inner life, and she made no towering contribution to humanity.” Thus the biog\xadrapher throws down his own gauntlet: Why, then, are we reading about her?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/21SEIGLEMAN/21SEIGLEMAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 710, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/21SEIGLEMAN/21SEIGLEMAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 710}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/21SEIGLEMAN/21SEIGLEMAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 866, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/21SEIGLEMAN/21SEIGLEMAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1838, 'width': 1553, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/21SEIGLEMAN/21SEIGLEMAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/21SEIGLEMAN/21SEIGLEMAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/21SEIGLEMAN/21SEIGLEMAN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Who She Knew', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Who She Knew', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ross, Janet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Downing, Ben', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-19T15:29:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Craig Seligman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Craig', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Seligman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7876dc89-72c6-5208-9084-88690c2222ac', 'word_count': 770, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7876dc89-72c6-5208-9084-88690c2222ac'}, {'abstract': 'The life of the oddly obscure economist Albert O. Hirschman.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/21/books/review/worldly-philosopher-by-jeremy-adelman.html', 'snippet': 'The life of the oddly obscure economist Albert O. Hirschman.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The life that the economist Albert O. Hirschman lived in the 1980s and early ’90s was the stuff of an intellectual’s most self-aggrandizing daydreams. There were honorary degrees (at least a dozen), prestigious prizes (wouldn’t you want a Thomas Jefferson Medal from the American Philosophical Society?) and conference after conference held in his honor. It’s customary for a distinguished professor to get a “Festschrift” of writings by students and disciples; Hirschman got four. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/21FOX/21FOX-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 715, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/21FOX/21FOX-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 715}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/21FOX/21FOX-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 860, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/21FOX/21FOX-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1719, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/21FOX/21FOX-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/21FOX/21FOX-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/21FOX/21FOX-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Exit, Voice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Exit, Voice', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hirschman, Albert O', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Adelman, Jeremy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-19T15:30:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Justin Fox', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Justin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fox', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d7bf632c-71cf-5567-bdb8-82e50908a27e', 'word_count': 1177, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d7bf632c-71cf-5567-bdb8-82e50908a27e'}, {'abstract': 'Rebecca Lee’s characters are driven, and confounded, by their desires.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/21/books/review/rebecca-lees-bobcat.html', 'snippet': 'Rebecca Lee’s characters are driven, and confounded, by their desires.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I could spend this entire review pulling beautiful lines out of Rebecca Lee’s textured and nuanced new story collection, “Bobcat.” There are the piquant ones, echoing Flannery O’Connor: “In my experience, you would no more expect to find peace within a family than you would expect to find it in yourself.” Or the perceptive descriptions, as pert as Lorrie Moore’s: “She went twice a week to a tanning salon and bleached her hair frequently, so that it looked like radioactive foliage growing out of dark, moody sands.” The collection has so many good passages — whole paragraphs that move into pages with never a misstep — that any linguaphile could spend a great afternoon in a little spasm of dazzle.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/21romm-SUB/21romm-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 603, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/21romm-SUB/21romm-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 603}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/21romm-SUB/21romm-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 797, 'width': 793, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/21romm-SUB/21romm-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 797, 'width': 793, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/21romm-SUB/21romm-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/21romm-SUB/21romm-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/21romm-SUB/21romm-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Baser Instincts', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Baser Instincts', 'name': None, 'seo': None