, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ff8f3528-e2cf-5a6f-b023-0d0d208633a3'}, {'abstract': 'A new study has found that low vitamin D levels in people over 55 are associated with an inability to perform ordinary tasks of daily life.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/19/low-vitamin-d-tied-to-aging-problems/', 'snippet': 'A new study has found that low vitamin D levels in people over 55 are associated with an inability to perform ordinary tasks of daily life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A new study has found that low vitamin D levels in people over 55 are associated with an inability to perform ordinary tasks of daily life.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/04/health/well_aging/well_aging-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 215, 'width': 211, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/04/health/well_aging/well_aging-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 215, 'width': 211, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/04/health/well_aging/well_aging-thumbStandard-v4.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/04/health/well_aging/well_aging-thumbStandard-v4.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/04/health/well_aging/well_aging-thumbLarge-v4.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Low Vitamin D Tied to Aging Problems', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elderly', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vitamin D', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-19T15:32:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Bakalar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bakalar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/28b071d8-a9e1-5b1c-ad71-8b2f9394499c', 'word_count': 207, 'uri': 'nyt://article/28b071d8-a9e1-5b1c-ad71-8b2f9394499c'}, {'abstract': 'A graduate student is obsessed with her professor’s wife in Susan Choi’s novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/21/books/review/my-education-by-susan-choi.html', 'snippet': 'A graduate student is obsessed with her professor’s wife in Susan Choi’s novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Susan Choi’s new novel starts out on familiar territory: the attraction between a precocious young woman and an intellectually charismatic older man. Regina is a 21-year-old graduate student taking literature classes at an unnamed East Coast university; Nicholas Brodeur is her celebrated professor, graced with “exceptional, even sinister, attractiveness.” Yet “My Education,” Choi’s fourth book, only feints toward the conventional novel, with the standard affair between student and professor. Just when Regina and Nicholas seem about to sleep together, he is superseded in Regina’s affections by his 33-year-old wife, Martha, on maternity leave from a teaching position at the same university. One night at a dinner party, Regina trails the older woman out the back door and kisses her, and we find ourselves on a less familiar campus than we’d thought.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/21COOKE/21COOKE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 594, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/21COOKE/21COOKE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 594}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/21COOKE/21COOKE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1013, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/21COOKE/21COOKE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1998, 'width': 2019, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/21COOKE/21COOKE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/21COOKE/21COOKE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/21COOKE/21COOKE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Academic Affairs', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Academic Affairs', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Choi, Susan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-19T15:32:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emily Cooke', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cooke', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d6ac6e95-1f1c-5f12-9007-8b7fa515ab84', 'word_count': 1233, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d6ac6e95-1f1c-5f12-9007-8b7fa515ab84'}, {'abstract': 'Edward Hoagland’s adventure-seeking protagonist is swept up in the chaos of southern Sudan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/21/books/review/children-are-diamonds-by-edward-hoagland.html', 'snippet': 'Edward Hoagland’s adventure-seeking protagonist is swept up in the chaos of southern Sudan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the late 1970s, the novelist and travel writer Edward Hoagland spent several months in Sudan, which was then Africa’s largest country and one of its most fragile. There he observed the deepening divide between the Arab north and the black Christian south, exacerbated by “demoralizing poverty” and the influence of militant Islam. His account of his journey, “African Calliope,” wove encounters with government ministers and Dinka cattle herders, expatriate businessmen and Khartoum taxi drivers into a portrait of a country struggling for unity yet tipping toward civil war.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/21hammer-sub/21hammer-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 298, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/21hammer-sub/21hammer-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 298}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/books/review/21hammer-sub/21hammer-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 496, 'width': 1000, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'