ument_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Waldstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Waldstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0e0e413f-9326-5ba5-9718-877371777a96', 'word_count': 847, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0e0e413f-9326-5ba5-9718-877371777a96'}, {'abstract': 'The stories about the crazy things parents do for their camper kids are a little disillusioning for this noncamp veteran.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/19/are-over-the-top-parents-really-the-rule-at-summer-camp/', 'snippet': 'The stories about the crazy things parents do for their camper kids are a little disillusioning for this noncamp veteran.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last year, when my oldest spent five nights at hockey camp, I sent him a care package. I did so partly because I love him and missed him, but mostly because I thought it would just be fun for him to get mail. When he came home, he brought most of the small care package with him, unused or uneaten. It was nice to get mail, he said, but he didn’t have time for anything else. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/18/style/motherlode-camp-wyatt2/motherlode-camp-wyatt2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 640, 'width': 480, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/18/style/motherlode-camp-wyatt2/motherlode-camp-wyatt2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 640, 'width': 480, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/18/style/m