19/chevrolets-bow-tie-turns-100/', 'snippet': 'Chevrolet was formed in 1911 and got its famous bow tie logo in 1913. Since then, speculation about where the symbol came from has run from wallpaper to obscure Southern newspaper ads.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Chevrolet first used its bow tie logo in 1913 (only two years after the marque’s formation), for the 1914 model year lineup. Essentially a cross with the horizontal bar italicized and longer than the vertical one, with the word CHEVROLET written in all caps inside, the basic shape hasn’t changed over the last 100 years.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/19/automobiles/wheels/chevrolet-1/chevrolet-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/19/automobiles/wheels/chevrolet-1/chevrolet-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/19/automobiles/wheels/chevrolet-1/chevrolet-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 577, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/19/automobiles/wheels/chevrolet-1/chevrolet-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 648, 'width': 1151, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/19/automobiles/wheels/chevrolet-1/chevrolet-1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/19/automobiles/wheels/chevrolet-1/chevrolet-1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/19/automobiles/wheels/chevrolet-1/chevrolet-1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Chevrolet’s Bow Tie Turns 100', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Logos', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Trademarks and Trade Names', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chevrolet Division of General Motors Corp', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'General Motors', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-19T16:02:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Preston', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Preston', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6c7d4ecd-2524-5bc7-a242-d59a7c5eedd1', 'word_count': 525, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6c7d4ecd-2524-5bc7-a242-d59a7c5eedd1'}, {'abstract': 'A day in the life of armed America.', 'web_url': 'https://nocera.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/19/the-gun-report-july-19-2013/', 'snippet': 'A day in the life of armed America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Saturday marks the one-year anniversary of the massacre at a movie theater in Aurora, Colo. That day, 58 people were sent to University of Colorado Hospital with gunshot wounds. Only one died. CBS Denver interviewed the staff that treated the victims, who suffered horrific and bloody injuries, and doctors said they’re still traumatized by the shooting.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Gun Report: July 19, 2013', 'kicker': 'Joe Nocera', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-07-19T16:10:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Mascia', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mascia', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/938c86a5-84ec-5b45-b1f2-1867dbfa0495', 'word_count': 1082, 'uri': 'nyt://article/938c86a5-84ec-5b45-b1f2-1867dbfa0495'}, {'abstract': 'New Yorkers with deep roots in the city are a rare but often genealogically obsessed breed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/21/realestate/family-tree-new-york.html', 'snippet': 'New Yorkers with deep roots in the city are a rare but often genealogically obsessed breed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Most New Yorkers are comparative newcomers, and it’s easy to assume that nearly everyone who lives in the city started out somewhere else. Even a native-born New Yorker is a rare bird. But a relative handful of New Yorkers can claim bragging rights as fourth-, fifth- or even sixth-generation city dwellers whose families settled here many decades ago and have mostly stayed put, sinking ever deeper roots.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/realestate/21JPCOV1_SPAN/JP-COV-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/21/realestate/21JPCOV1_SPAN/JP-COV-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image',