’s most famous soldiers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A fast-moving column of Union troops marched along a narrow stretch of beach in coastal South Carolina in the twilight of July 18, 1863. In the vanguard of the federal force was the 600-strong 54th Massachusetts Infantry, the first black regiment raised in the North. Their objective, Confederate-held Fort Wagner, composed part of the defenses of Charleston. Victory here could open the way to the capture of the city and its harbor.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbStandard.gif', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbStandard.gif', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Old Flag Never Touched the Ground’', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shaw, Robert G', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Charleston (SC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Bedford (Mass)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-20T02:05:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opin