 'By William Bryant Logan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': 'Bryant', 'lastname': 'Logan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/d1967770-c425-54de-9556-355552e5ca3a', 'word_count': 462, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d1967770-c425-54de-9556-355552e5ca3a'}, {'abstract': 'Better job opportunities for the children of the top 1 percent deepen our cynicism about how people get ahead.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/20/whos-your-daddy/', 'snippet': 'Better job opportunities for the children of the top 1 percent deepen our cynicism about how people get ahead.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gus Wenner runs Rollingstone.com; his father gave him the job. But Jann Wenner, the magazine’s co-founder and publisher, was quick to assure critics of the appointment process that his son is terribly talented and had to prove himself before being given the reins. Apparently Gus worked his way up from more junior positions with the company, and demonstrated, according to his father, the “drive and discipline and charm, and all the things that show leadership.” Gus Wenner is 22 years old.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Who’s Your Daddy?', 'kicker': 'The Great Divide', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Who’s Your Daddy?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Inequality', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nepotism', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wenner, Jann', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-20T18:30:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Op