rney General Greg Abbott, who’s running for governor of Texas with the big-money Republicans locked up, doesn’t have to answer the kinds of questions voters want answered.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/21/us/rolling-in-cash-but-what-does-abbott-think.html', 'snippet': 'With no real opposition, a candidate like Attorney General Greg Abbott, who’s running for governor of Texas with the big-money Republicans locked up, doesn’t have to answer the kinds of questions voters want answered.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ross Ramsey, the executive editor of The Texas Tribune, writes a column for The Tribune.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '25A', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rolling in Cash, but What Does Abbott Think?', 'kicker': 'The Texas Tribune', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rolling in Cash, But What Does Abbott Think?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections, Governors', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Abbott, Gregory W (1957- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-21T03:02:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Ramsey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ramsey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8666efaa-9c4e-5057-9bce-498937439361', 'word_count': 744, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8666efaa-9c4e-5057-9bce-498937439361'}, {'abstract': 'Howard Rains, 43, is trying to bring back an old-time style of Texas fiddling that was popular between the Civil War and World War II.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/21/us/down-home-fiddling-the-way-it-really-used-to-be.html', 'snippet': 'Howard Rains, 43, is trying to bring back an old-time style of Texas fiddling that was popular between the Civil War and World War II.', 'lead_paragraph': 'During the mid-19th and early 20th centuries, a distinctive form of fiddle music emerged in Texas. Known for idiosyncratic timing and phrasing, this style was commonly played with banjos or guitars on front porches or in living rooms, less for show than for social interaction. It reflected the cross-pollination in Texas after the Civil War, with touches of African-American, Appalachian, Cajun, Czech, German, Irish, Mexican, Polish and Scottish musical forms.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '25B', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/us/21ttCulture/21ttCulture-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/21/us/21ttCulture/21ttCulture-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/us/21ttCulture/21ttCulture-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/us/21ttCulture/21ttCulture-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 844, 'width': 1266, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/us/21ttCulture/21ttCulture-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/21/us/21ttCulture/21ttCulture-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': '