ce': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pesticide in Lunch That Killed 23 in Bihar, Forensic Report Says', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Forensic Science', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pesticides', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-21T15:56:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hari Kumar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hari', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kumar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fa1c0736-d154-50c6-bd7f-118d12822f4b', 'word_count': 394, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fa1c0736-d154-50c6-bd7f-118d12822f4b'}, {'abstract': 'The candidates’ daily campaign schedule at a glance.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/21/july-21-where-the-candidates-are-today/', 'snippet': 'The candidates’ daily campaign schedule at a glance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Planned events for the mayoral candidates, according to the campaigns and organizations they are affiliated with. Times are listed as scheduled but frequently change.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'July 21: Where the Candidates Are Today', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections, Mayors', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-21T16:16:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alison Leigh Cowan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alison', 'middlename': 'Leigh', 'lastname': 'Cowan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/19ae37d2-3467-5819-9602-dfe9e4e4455f', 'word_count': 4259, 'uri': 'nyt://article/19ae37d2-3467-5819-9602-dfe9e4e4455f'}, {'abstract': 'Why has Pittsburgh done so much better?', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/21/a-tale-of-two-rust-belt-cities/', 'snippet': 'Why has Pittsburgh done so much better?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Here’s a question: is the crisis in Detroit simply a function of the industrial decline of the U.S. heartland, or is it about internal developments within the metro area that have produced a uniquely bad outcome? I think a useful comparison can be made with Pittsburgh, another city that once had an iconic monoculture economy — based on steel, not autos — that also took a terrible hit, but seems to be in a much better position now.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Tale of Two Rust-Belt Cities', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-07-21T16:23:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/db800b9a-09c4-5f55-9f52-8453ce8f87c9', 'word_count': 317, 'uri': 'nyt://article/db800b9a-09c4-5f55-9f52-8453ce8f87c9'}, {'abstract': 'Developing allergies to animals and substances can be an occupational hazard of science.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/23/science/allergies-in-the-time-of-research.html', 'snippet': 'Developing allergies to animals and substances can be an occupational hazard of science.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For his master’s thesis research, Andrew Martin, an evolutionary biologist, studied the periodical cicada, an insect that lives underground and emerges once every 13 or 17 years. Traveling the United States from Georgia to Illinois to New York, Dr. Martin, now a professor at the University of Colorado, spent a summer up close with the cicadas. At each site, he would handle anywhere from 30 to 50 of them.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/23/science/23ALLERGY/ALLERGY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/23/science/23ALLERGY/ALLERGY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/23/science/23ALLERGY/ALLERGY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/23/science/23ALLERGY/ALLERGY-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/23/science/23ALLERGY/ALLERGY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/23/science/23ALLERGY/ALLERGY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/23/science/23ALLERGY/ALLERGY-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Allergies in the Time of Research', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Allergies in the Time of Research', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ALLERGIES', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rosner, Hillary', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-21T17:54:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hillary Rosner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hillary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f9c0f470-1365-5eda-ab74-2cc4e8015865', 'word_count': 748, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f9c0f470-1365-5eda-ab74-2cc4e8015865'}, {'abstract': 'Black boxes in cars can be used to identify safety problems and as evidence in traffic accidents and criminal cases, but also raise privacy concerns.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/22/business/black-boxes-in-cars-a-question-of-privacy.html', 'snippet': 'Black boxes in cars can be used to identify safety problems and as evidence in traffic accidents and criminal cases, but also raise privacy concerns.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Timothy P. Murray crashed his government-issued Ford Crown Victoria in 2011, he was fortunate, as car accidents go. Mr. Murray, then the lieutenant governor of Massachusetts, was not seriously hurt, and he told the police he was wearing a seat belt and was not speeding.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/20/