 Sunday, has quickly established itself in New York as a reliably protean source of infectiously danceable melodies and rhythmic delights. But this year’s edition of the music festival, the third, also seems likely to feature a heightened quotient of pointedly political lyrics.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/22/arts/BRAZIL/BRAZIL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/22/arts/BRAZIL/BRAZIL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/22/arts/BRAZIL/BRAZIL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/22/arts/BRAZIL/BRAZIL-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/22/arts/BRAZIL/BRAZIL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/22/arts/BRAZIL/BRAZIL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/22/arts/BRAZIL/BRAZIL-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Soundtrack to a Party, and Unrest in Brazil', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Soundtrack To a Party, And Unrest In Brazil', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brazil', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-21T21:53:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Larry Rohter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Larry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/36d0f4b6-7d70-56d1-bdd0-214f79323939', 'word_count': 1065, 'uri': 'nyt://article/36d0f4b6-7d70-56d1-bdd0-214f79323939'}, {'abstract': 'Taking a trip with Ian Livengood. Unless you are already there. In which case, stay where you are.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/21/hurry-or-youll-miss-out/', 'snippet': 'Taking a trip with Ian Livengood. Unless you are already there. In which case, stay where you are.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MONDAY’S PUZZLE — Thought you’d take a break after the Sunday puzzle, did you? Sorry, back in the saddle please, because we’re taking a trip to BOSTON with Ian Livengood. Unless you already live there, of course. In that case, stay where you are.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hurry or You’ll Miss Out', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-21T22:00:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7a7dccb7-f50b-5c5d-8e9f-c19cb98b420d', 'word_count': 255, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7a7dccb7-f50b-5c5d-8e9f-c19cb98b420d'}, {'abstract': 'The music festival at Caramoor presented the 1884 version of “Don Carlos” in French, and it glowed with the ideals of the French Revolution.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/22/arts/music/don-carlos-in-its-original-tongue-reveals-intricacies.html', 'snippet': 'The music festival at Caramoor presented the 1884 version of “Don Carlos” in French, and it glowed with the ideals of the French Revolution.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KATONAH, N.Y. — In this bicentenary year, much will be made of Verdi’s status in Italian history. The music festival at Caramoor, with its current focus on Verdi in Paris examines the French connection. On Saturday it presented the 1884 version of “Don Carlos” sung in French, the language in which it was originally written. Blessed with a stellar cast of singers and an impassioned Orchestra of St. Luke’s under the direction of Will Crutchfield, this pivotal masterpiece of Verdi’s glowed with the ideals of the French revolution.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/22/arts/CARLOS/CARLOS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 411, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/22/arts/CARLOS/CARLOS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 411}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/22/arts/CARLOS/CARLOS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 701, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/22/arts/CARLOS/CARLOS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1403, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/22/arts/CARLOS/CARLOS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/22/arts/CARLOS/CARLOS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/22/arts/CARLOS/CARLOS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ear for French, and Renewed Reverence for Verdi', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ear for French, and Renewed Reverence for Verdi', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'da Fonseca-Wollheim, Corinna', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Caramoor International Music Festival', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Crutchfield, Will', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-21T22:00:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Corinna da Fonseca-Wollheim', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f092aa2f-ce28-5e05-8c7d-5e87fecbeb2d', 'word_count': 630, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f092aa2f-ce28-5e05-8c7d-5e87fecbeb2d'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Goldblum will join Laurie Metcalf in Bruce Norris’s new play this fall at Lincoln Center Theater.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/21/jeff-goldblum-joins-cast-of-domesticated/', 'snippet': 'Mr. Goldblum will join Laurie Metcalf in Bruce Norris’s new play this fall at Lincoln Center Theater.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jeff Goldblum will join Laurie Metcalf this fall in the Lincoln Center Theater production of “Domesticated,” a new work by the Pulitzer Prize-winning playwright Bruce Norris (“Clybourne Park”), the company announced on Sunday. Commissioned by Lincoln Center Theater, “Domesticated” is about a politician and his wife whose marriage is put in jeopardy by a scandal. The play is to begin preview performances Off Broadway at the Mitzi E. Newhouse Theater on Oct. 10, with opening night set for Nov. 4. The director will be Anna D. Shapiro (“August: Osage County”), who has directed several of Mr. Norris’s plays, including “The Unmentionables” and “The Pain and the Itch.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/theater/21goldblum/21goldblum-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 696, 'width': 454, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/theater/21goldblum/21goldblum-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 696, 'width': 454, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/theater/21goldblum/21goldblum-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/21/theater/21goldblum/21goldblum-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/theater/21goldblum/21goldblum-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Jeff Goldblum Joins Cast of ‘Domesticated’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Jeff Goldblum Joins Cast of ‘Domesticated’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goldblum, Jeff', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Norris, Bruce', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lincoln Center Theater', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-21T22:02:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Erik Piepenburg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erik', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Piepenburg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4884ffc9-bfe6-5088-89fe-97b2aa7d5748', 'word_count': 184, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4884ffc9-bfe6-5088-89fe-97b2aa7d5748'}, {'abstract': 'The band Om played its rhythmic drones at the Temple of Dendur at the Metropolitan Museum of Art.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/22/arts/music/om-plays-its-rhythmic-drones-at-the-temple-of-dendur.html', 'snippet': 'The band Om played its rhythmic drones at the Temple of Dendur at the Metropolitan Museum of Art.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The music of the band Om boils down song to rhythmic drones and chants and ancient scales, played on electric bass and drums with touches of electric guitar and analog modular synthesizer. For a decade, it has been both post-everything and pre-everything — quiet, methodically repetitive, possibly fed by Alice Coltrane and doom metal and Gregorian chant, but directly attached to none of those. It’s not for everyone. It’s either dull or the oneness. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'i