eeper and Breakout Candidates at Quarterback', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fantasy Sports', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Manning, Eli', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bradford, Sam', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weeden, Brandon', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vick, Michael', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Palmer, Carson', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Foles, Nick', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-22T15:44:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jason Sablich and Justin Sablich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jason', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sablich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Justin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sablich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4c5fa0c4-8c56-5513-8c55-8cefe95501f8', 'word_count': 816, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4c5fa0c4-8c56-5513-8c55-8cefe95501f8'}, {'abstract': 'What’s the price of cheap housing?', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/22/atl-v-bos/', 'snippet': 'What’s the price of cheap housing?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Still playing around with sprawl and all that. As many people have pointed out over the years, the most obvious comparison among major US metro areas is between Boston, still relatively traditional in urban structure, and Atlanta, the Sultan of Sprawl; not long ago the cities were about the same population, although Atlanta is growing much faster. A comparison looks like this:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'ATL v. BOS', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-07-22T16:00:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9aed9a38-39e2-5761-a02d-81ef1c5ae1ad', 'word_count': 225, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9aed9a38-39e2-5761-a02d-81ef1c5ae1ad'}, {'abstract': 'Can you use Zoltar the Fortune-Weighing Robot to help find a real coin among counterfeits?', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/22/zoltar/', 'snippet': 'Can you use Zoltar the Fortune-Weighing Robot to help find a real coin among counterfeits?', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week’s challenge originally appeared in Round 1 of the 2011-2012 U.S.A. Mathematical Talent Search, a free annual math competition open to all United States middle and high school students. (You’ll find additional information about the U.S.A.M.T.S. at the end of this post.) The challenge is a coin-weighing problem, but not your ordinary coin-weighing problem. Allow me to introduce —', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/crosswords/Zoltar/Zoltar-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 441, 'width': 480, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/crosswords/Zoltar/Zoltar-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 441, 'width': 480, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/crosswords/Zoltar/Zoltar-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/21/crosswords/Zoltar/Zoltar-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/21/crosswords/Zoltar/Zoltar-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Zoltar the Fortune-Weighing Robot', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mathematics', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Puzzles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'San Diego (Calif)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-22T16:00:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gary Antonick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Antonick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a1fa549f-10e9-5433-b3b9-a87abed18e33', 'word_count': 1550, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a1fa549f-10e9-5433-b3b9-a87abed18e33'}, {'abstract': 'A weekend in the life of armed America.', 'web_url': 'https://nocera.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/22/weekend-gun-report-july-20-22-2013/', 'snippet': 'A weekend in the life of armed America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This weekend—another gruesome summer weekend replete with drive-by shootings, kids shot by “mistake,” gang shootings, you name it—there were two instances, one on Chicago on Friday and one in Syracuse on Sunday, in which people were shot while visiting memorials for recent gunshot victims. Where else does that happen? War zones.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Weekend Gun Report: July 19-21, 2013', 'kicker': 'Joe Nocera', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-07-22T16:08:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Mascia', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mascia', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f0d6a1e6-96f7-5fd3-bc46-2f25326ce1ce', 'word_count': 2115, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f0d6a1e6-96f7-5fd3-bc46-2f25326ce1ce'}, {'abstract': 'General Robert E. Lee wasn’t the only Confederate leader to invade the Union during the summer of 1863.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/22/mr-morgans-daring-raid/', 'snippet': 'General Robert E. Lee wasn’t the only Confederate leader to invade the Union during the summer of 1863.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When, in July 1863, the Confederate guerrilla Henry Lane Stone stepped off the captured steamboat that had ferried him from Kentucky across the Ohio River, he “experienced some peculiar sensations” as he once again “set foot on Indiana soil.” Just nine months earlier, Stone had gone south to join the Confederate cavalry in occupied Kentucky under John Hunt Morgan. Now he was not only returning, but also “engaged in a hostile invasion of my adopted State.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mr. Morgan’s Daring Raid', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Indiana', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kentucky', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ohio', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-22T16:09:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicole Etcheson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicole', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Etcheson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8242d9b6-055b-5626-8fc7-6633e782ae73', 'word_count': 1759, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8242d9b6-055b-5626-8fc7-6633e782ae73'}, {'abstract': 'Cycling’s loss of credibility has made it impossible for riders to satisfactorily answer questions about performance enhancing drugs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/23/sports/cycling/tour-winner-cannot-break-away-from-doping-questions.html', 'snippet': 'Cycling’s loss of credibility has made it impossible for riders to satisfactorily answer questions about performance enhancing drugs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — Chris Froome, crowned the Tour de France winner on Sunday, was being peppered at a recent news conference with questions that furtively nibbled at a bigger issue — have you doped? — when his team director leapt into the fray, bubbling with frustration.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/23/sports/cycling/23cycling2_cnd/23cycling2_cnd-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 414, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/23/sports/cycling/23cycling2_cnd/23cycling2_cnd-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 414}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/23/sports/cycling/23cycling2_cn