may be suppressing the immune system and worsening the AIDS epidemic in Africa.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Aflatoxins — poisons produced by fungi that grow on moldy peanuts and corn — may be worsening Africa’s AIDS epidemic by helping suppress the immune systems of newly infected people, a new study has found.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mold Toxins Tied to AIDS Epidemic', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mold Toxins Tied To AIDS Epidemic', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McNeil, Donald G Jr', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Acquired Immune Deficiency Syndrome', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kenya', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mold', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFRICA', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'World Mycotoxin Journal', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-22T18:49:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Donald G. McNeil Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Donald', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'McNeil', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/997dce58-55c9-5312-a06c-b211b0900be1', 'word_count': 248, 'uri': 'nyt://article/997dce58-55c9-5312-a06c-b211b0900be1'}, {'abstract': 'Recent developments in health and science news. Also this week: A clue to the origin of heavy metals like gold and the discovery of a new moon circling Neptune.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/23/science/a-big-nosed-dinosaur-and-ancient-time-keeping.html', 'snippet': 'Recent developments in health and science news. Also this week: A clue to the origin of heavy metals like gold and the discovery of a new moon circling Neptune.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Everything new this week was old. Paleontologists in Utah discovered a cowlike species of dinosaur (about 65 million years old), and archaeologists in Scotland found what may be the world’s first lunar calendar (10,000 years old). Meanwhile, astronomers tell us that all of Earth’s gold was formed by ancient stars colliding (more than 4.5 billion years ago) and that there is a moon orbiting Neptune we all somehow failed to notice (also quite old).', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/23/science/23WEEK/WEEK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 379, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/23/science/23WEEK/WEEK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 379}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/23/science/23WEEK/WEEK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 566, 'width': 895, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/23/science/23WEEK/WEEK-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 566, 'width': 895, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/23/science/23WEEK/WEEK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/23/science/23WEEK/WEEK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/23/science/23WEEK/WEEK-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'cr