ion_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By J. D. Biersdorfer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'J.', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/9f45f06e-7998-5cf5-8c78-82ba3b8175cf', 'word_count': 574, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9f45f06e-7998-5cf5-8c78-82ba3b8175cf'}, {'abstract': 'Australian physicists — lifelong cricket fans — have come up with equations that calculate the effect of wind on balls that are spinning in arbitrary directions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/23/science/tracking-a-spinning-ball-with-math.html', 'snippet': 'Australian physicists — lifelong cricket fans — have come up with equations that calculate the effect of wind on balls that are spinning in arbitrary directions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From tennis pros to cricket bowlers, athletes have long known that wind will influence the trajectory of a spinning ball. But until now, research on the topic has tended to focus on topspin or underspin — movement perpendicular to the direction in which the ball is moving.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/23/science/23spin/23spin-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 408, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/23/science/23spin/23spin-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 408}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/23/science/23spin/23spin-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 696, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/23/science/23sp