e_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a90608ca-5e2c-5895-9463-81b7f54c4624', 'word_count': 658, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a90608ca-5e2c-5895-9463-81b7f54c4624'}, {'abstract': 'On a dangerous journey from war-torn villages to a refugee camp,\xa0Congolese families are caught in the cross-fire.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/23/opinion/congo-the-road-to-ruin.html', 'snippet': 'On a dangerous journey from war-torn villages to a refugee camp,\xa0Congolese families are caught in the cross-fire.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This Op-Doc video shows just one scene from a violent saga that has consumed the Democratic Republic of Congo since 1998. Over that time the conflict has left more than five million dead and churned up hundreds of thousands of refugees, a result of the constant battle for control over the country’s abundant supply of precious minerals. The epicenter of the fighting is Goma, one of the largest cities in this central African nation and where this Op-Doc segment was filmed. For more than a decade, Rwandan-backed rebel groups based in Goma have been trying to overthrow Congo’s national government, which is plagued by corruption. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/23/opinion/video-opdoc-congo/video-opdoc-congo-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/23/opinion/video-opdoc-congo/video-opdoc-congo-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/23/opinion/video-opdoc-congo/video-opdoc-congo-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 507, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/23/opinion/video-opdoc-congo/video-opdoc-congo-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 507, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/23/opinion/video-opdoc-congo/video-opdoc-congo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/23/opinion/video-opdoc-congo/video-opdoc-congo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/23/opinion/video-opdoc-congo/video-opdoc-congo-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Congo: The Road to Ruin’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Congo: The Road to Ruin’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Congo, Democratic Republic of (Congo-Kinshasa)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Goma (Congo, Democratic Republic of)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Refugees and Displaced Persons', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'March 23 Movement', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-23T00:59:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel McCabe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McCabe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/f202d0f3-dbdb-5334-822d-c3d921b510a2', 'word_count': 480, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f202d0f3-dbdb-5334-822d-c3d921b510a2'}, {'abstract': 'Now that Prime Minister Shinzo Abe’s party and governing coalition has full control of Parliament, economic reforms should be the No. 1 priority.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/23/opinion/opportunity-and-risk-in-japan.html', 'snippet': 'Now that Prime Minister Shinzo Abe’s party and governing coalition has full control of Parliament, economic reforms should be the No. 1 priority.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sunday’s election for the upper house of Parliament in Japan was a strong vote of confidence for Prime Minister Shinzo Abe and the pro-growth fiscal and monetary policies he has pursued since entering office in December. His conservative coalition now seems assured a working parliamentary majority through 2016, which would end the trend of weak revolving-door governments, which saw six successive prime ministers over the past six years.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Opportunity and Risk in Japan', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Opportunity and Risk in Japan', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ABE, SHINZO', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-23T01:00:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/73bf60da-ba39-54e0-b6c8-216770a3d9fe', 'word_count': 469, 'uri': 'nyt://article/73bf60da-ba39-54e0-b6c8-216770a3d9fe'}, {'abstract': 'These laws’ implicit affirmation of a code of manliness links them to novelistic and filmic representations of the old west.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/22/stand-your-ground-be-a-man/', 'snippet': 'These laws’ implicit affirmation of a code of manliness links them to novelistic and filmic representations of the old west.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Florida Stand Your Ground law is often characterized as an expansion of the venerable “castle doctrine” — because a man’s home is his castle, he is justified in repelling intruders with force if necessary — but, as many have observed, it may be more accurate to see it as the return to the contemporary American landscape of the “shoot first” ethic of the old west, at least as it has been portrayed in dozens of movies. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Stand Your Ground, Be a Man', 'kicker': 'Stanley Fish', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Self-Defense', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-23T01:00:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stanley Fish', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stanley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fish', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fdf4b3c6-b2bb-54f0-ad8f-8fd47f2fb26e', 'word_count': 1000, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fdf4b3c6-b2bb-54f0-ad8f-8fd47f2fb26e'}, {'abstract': 'Times readers reflect on their own experiences with anxiety to mark the end of the series.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/22/the-end-of-anxiety/', 'snippet': 'Times readers reflect on their own experiences with anxiety to mark the end of the series.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last week brought the final installment in the Opinionator’s Anxiety series. In more than 70 essays, the series explored everything from obsessive compulsive disorder to being overscheduled to getting groped on the subway. There were even some suggestions for anxiety-related tattoos.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The End of Anxiety', 'kicker': 'Anxiety', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Anxiety and Stress', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fear (Emotion)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-23T01:00:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a796d56a-d6b5-55fe-9d10-66f26c065850', 'word_count': 1467, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a796d56a-d6b5-55fe-9d10-66f26c065850'}, {'abstract': 'President Obama is making the case that prosperity comes from the “middle out,” and doesn’t trickle down from the top.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/23/us/politics/president-adopts-catchphrase-to-describe-proposed-recipe-for-economic-revival.html', 'snippet': 'President Obama is making the case that prosperity comes from the “middle out,” and doesn’t trickle down from the top.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — President Obama says prosperity does not trickle down, and a rising tide does not necessarily lift all boats. The conservative policies predicated on those ideas, he maintains, amount to a you’re-on-your-own economics, when the country really needs a we’re-in-this-together approach: in short, prosperity needs to come from the “middle out” rather than the top down. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'President Adopts Catchphrase to Describe Proposed Recipe for Economic Revival', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Obama Adopts Catchphrase to Describe Proposed Recipe for Economic Revival', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lowrey, Annie', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-23T01:03:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Annie Lowrey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Annie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lowrey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/108ac482-a7b4-5c7a-bb29-be47849856fb', 'word_count': 875, 'uri': 'nyt://article/108ac482-a7b4-5c7a-bb29-be47849856fb'}, {'abstract': 'An egregious federal ruling harms reporting and government accountability.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/23/opinion/a-terrible-precedent-for-press-freedom.html', 'snippet': 'An egregious federal ruling harms reporting and government accountability.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An egregious appeals court ruling on Friday has dealt a major setback to press freedoms by requiring the author of a 2006 book to testify in the criminal trial of a former Central Intelligence Agency official charged with leaking classified information. The ruling and the Justice Department’s misplaced zeal in subpoenaing James Risen, the book’s author and a reporter for The Times, carry costs for robust journalism and government accountability that should alarm all Americans. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Terrible Precedent for Press Freedom', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Terrible Precedent for Press Freedom', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Risen, James', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News Sources, Confidential Status of', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Justice Department', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of the Press', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Central Intelligence Agency', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gregory, Roger L', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brinkema, Leonie M', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sterling, Jeffrey Alexander', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-23T01:05:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf55aec2-75b2-5c87-9ace-a0118f4b52c5', 'word_count': 484, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf55aec2-75b2-5c87-9ace-a0118f4b52c5'}, {'abstract': 'Having “pro bono publico” lawyers challenge the government’s requests for surveillance will result in better judicial outcomes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/23/opinion/a-better-secret-court.html', 'snippet': 'Having “pro bono publico” lawyers challenge the government’s requests for surveillance will result in better judicial outcomes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TOLEDO, Ohio — CONGRESS created the Foreign Intelligence Surveillance Court in 1978 as a check on executive authority. Recent disclosures about vast data-gathering by the government have raised concerns about the legitimacy of the court’s actions. Congress can take a simple step to restore confidence in the court’s impartiality and integrity: authorizing its judges to appoint lawyers to serve the public interest when novel legal issues come before it. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Better Secret Court', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Better Secret Court', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carr, James G', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Foreign Intelligence Surveillance Court', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Surveillance of Citizens by Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Foreign Intelligence Surveillance Act (FISA)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-23T01:05:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By James G. Carr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'Carr', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/779fd03c-690b-571a-bb89-aeb8ab3dcb3e', 'word_count': 924, 'uri': 'nyt://article/779fd03c-690b-571a-bb89-aeb8ab3dcb3e'}, {'abstract': 'The Catholic Church isn’t the only place where child sexual abuse cases have exposed religious communities as havens for wrongdoing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/23/opinion/bruni-the-faithfuls-failing.html', 'snippet': 'The Catholic Church isn’t the only place where child sexual abuse cases have exposed religious communities as havens for wrongdoing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The men were spiritual leaders, held up before the children around them as wise and righteous and right. So they had special access to those kids. Special sway.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/frank-bruni/frank-bruni-articleLarge.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/frank-bru