iddlename': None, 'lastname': 'Protess', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lattman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9d2e47b5-8434-5380-b80f-3b3b240b2f7d', 'word_count': 1413, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9d2e47b5-8434-5380-b80f-3b3b240b2f7d'}, {'abstract': 'Bankruptcy proceedings will be very painful, but city and state officials should resist the temptation to gut modest public pensions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/23/opinion/getting-detroit-back-on-its-feet.html', 'snippet': 'Bankruptcy proceedings will be very painful, but city and state officials should resist the temptation to gut modest public pensions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After suffering through decades of economic decline and official mismanagement, the city of Detroit sought bankruptcy protection last week, becoming the largest American municipality to take that extreme step. The city’s debts total a staggering $18 billion, far more than it could ever afford to pay back given its shrinking population and tax base.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Getting Detroit Back on Its Feet', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Getting Detroit Back on Its Feet', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Detroit (Mich)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bankruptcies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pensions and Retirement Plans', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-23T01:10:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The 