elationship between the police and the news media.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/23/nyregion/shades-of-the-truth-from-the-police.html', 'snippet': 'Paul J. Browne, the chief spokesman for the police commissioner, helped create something of a tense relationship between the police and the news media.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Years ago, Leland Jones, the press secretary for Mayor David N. Dinkins, and I sat on a bench off the marble rotunda in City Hall and stared at each other glumly. We’d just had another sonic disagreement. He was convinced that I had misunderstood and misrepresented his boss. I was convinced he was an argumentative factotum.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/23/nyregion/GOTHAM/GOTHAM-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 538, 'width': 381, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/23/nyregion/GOTHAM/GOTHAM-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 538, 'width': 381, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/23/nyregion/GOTHAM/GOTHAM-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/23/nyregion/GOTHAM/GOTHAM-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/23/nyregion/GOTHAM/GOTHAM-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Shades of the Truth From the Police', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Shades Of the Truth From the Police', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Police Department (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Powell, Michael K', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Browne, Paul J', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kelly, Raymond W', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-23T01:19:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Powell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Powell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/58386e06-3887-5cce-96e2-fbde3d126bd4', 'word_count': 776, 'uri': 'nyt://article/58386e06-3887-5cce-96e2-fbde3d126bd4'}, {'abstract': 'The command is not perfect, implying, as it does, that one’s written output should be limited to one’s passions.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/22/should-we-write-what-we-know/', 'snippet': 'The command is not perfect, implying, as it does, that one’s written output should be limited to one’s passions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the three most famous writing mottoes is “Kill your darlings.” Commonly attributed to William Faulkner and others, the sentiment seems originally to have been expressed by Sir Arthur Quiller-Couch in the early 20th century. He said, “Whenever you feel an impulse to perpetrate a piece of exceptionally fine writing, obey it — whole-heartedly — and delete it before sending your manuscript to press. Murder your darlings.” No matter the verb, the sentiment is that sometimes a passage can be improved by the removal of a metaphor, turn of phrase or quip to which one has become partial. True enough, but, in my experience, the problem of a well-turned phrase hurting the larger piece doesn’t come up all that often. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Should We Write What We Know?', 'kicker': 'Draft', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Slogans and Mottoes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-23T01:20:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Yagoda', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yagoda', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/617019ca-0b83-59d4-b701-fa9eb97908c9', 'word_count': 1109, 'uri': 'nyt://article/617019ca-0b83-59d4-b701-fa9eb97908c9'}, {'abstract': 'Gov. Andrew M. Cuomo, 55, and Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg, 71, had a whitewater-rafting showdown. Guess who won?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/23/nyregion/cuomo-promotes-adirondack-tourism-by-taking-bloomberg-on-a-wet-ride.html', 'snippet': 'Gov. Andrew M. Cuomo, 55, and Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg, 71, had a whitewater-rafting showdown. Guess who won?', 'lead_paragraph': 'INDIAN LAKE, N.Y. — When Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg travels by boat, it is probably more likely to be by yacht than by dinghy. But on Monday, the mayor boarded an inflatable raft in the middle of the Adirondack backwoods to do a surefire publicity-friendly favor for a man, Gov. Andrew M. Cuomo, with whom he has had at times a choppy relationship.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/23/nyregion/RAFTING1/R