2013-07-23T13:02:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/80288498-cf8b-5e0b-889b-cc79666bc648', 'word_count': 97, 'uri': 'nyt://article/80288498-cf8b-5e0b-889b-cc79666bc648'}, {'abstract': 'When Ryan Braun finally accepted a drug-related suspension, he made it harder for the next Biogenesis suspects.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/24/sports/bad-day-for-braun-good-day-for-baseball.html', 'snippet': 'When Ryan Braun finally accepted a drug-related suspension, he made it harder for the next Biogenesis suspects.', 'lead_paragraph': 'That sound you hear emanating from Manhattan today is the grand pooh-bahs of baseball celebrating as if it were Mardi Gras. Yes, today is Fat Tuesday, now that Ryan Braun chose the path of least resistance to his latest doping suspension. The only stunning part of Baseball versus Braun, the sequel, is that Braun dropped all the self-righteous howling about his absolute innocence and decided to meekly accept a summer of playing golf instead of baseball. No challenging baseball’s reliance on evidence outside of a positive drug test. No bashing the credibility of the dubious character that is the Biogenesis clinic guru Anthony Bosch. Nope, just a shrug and something that might resemble a shred of an apology if you squint really hard.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bad Day for Braun, Good Day for Baseball', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doping (Sports)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Braun, Ryan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Miller, Von', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Biogenesis of America LLC', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Major League Baseball', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-23T13:06:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lynn Zinser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zinser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2847416c-a921-50ef-99d0-058b4b947b50', 'word_count': 679, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2847416c-a921-50ef-99d0-058b4b947b50'}, {'abstract': 'Metropolitan Diary: In the early morning hours, listening to the mysterious, perfect rhythm of a cooing bird.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/23/a-mourning-dove-ring-tone/', 'snippet': 'Metropolitan Diary: In the early morning hours, listening to the mysterious, perfect rhythm of a cooing bird.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dear Diary:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Mourning Dove Ring Tone', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Birds', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-23T13:09:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Krause', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krause', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Ne