 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tanzina Vega', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tanzina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vega', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/85c57193-4c24-5c6c-b06e-5dcc95a4726d', 'word_count': 484, 'uri': 'nyt://article/85c57193-4c24-5c6c-b06e-5dcc95a4726d'}, {'abstract': 'Proposals to link the interest rates for federal student loans to the rate at which banks borrow from the Federal Reserve are misguided but provide a useful analogy, an economist writes.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/23/support-for-college-students-and-banks-not-so-different/', 'snippet': 'Proposals to link the interest rates for federal student loans to the rate at which banks borrow from the Federal Reserve are misguided but provide a useful analogy, an economist writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Phillip Swagel is a professor at the School of Public Policy at the University of Maryland and was assistant secretary for economic policy at the Treasury Department from 2006 to 2009.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Support for College Students and Banks: Not So Different', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interest Rates', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Student Loans', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Warren, Elizabeth', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-23T15:00:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Phillip Swagel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phillip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Swagel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/18bda3a8-de92-58bf-8643-fa9dd99e4571', 'word_count': 1130, 'uri': 'nyt://article/18bda3a8-de92-58bf-8643-fa9dd99e4571'}, {'abstract': 'On the anniversary of Anders Behring Breivik’s killing spree, Norway revisits a tamer kind of evil.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/23/what-a-difference-a-massacre-makes/', 'snippet': 'On the anniversary of Anders Behring Breivik’s killing spree, Norway revisits a tamer kind of evil.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BERGEN, NORWAY — Last week, the 40-year-old death-metal artist, arsonist and murderer Kristian “Varg” Vikernes was arrested in central France on suspicion of planning mass murder. Vikernes was once known in Norway as “the most evil man in the kingdom since Quisling” for burning to the ground several historic Norwegian churches and fatally stabbing exactly one person, the fellow death-metal guitarist Euronymous, in the early 1990s. In a country priding itself on wholesomeness, those crimes seemed especially gruesome.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What a Difference a Massacre Makes', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANTI-SEMITISM', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Breivik, Anders Behring', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Norway', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_