': 'Planned events for the mayoral candidates, according to the campaigns and organizations they are affiliated with. Times are listed as scheduled but frequently change.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'July 23: Where the Candidates Are Today', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections, Mayors', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-23T15:30:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alison Leigh Cowan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alison', 'middlename': 'Leigh', 'lastname': 'Cowan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/56742c7f-047c-5a10-9611-5f22aa966e81', 'word_count': 2154, 'uri': 'nyt://article/56742c7f-047c-5a10-9611-5f22aa966e81'}, {'abstract': 'After kayakers persuaded the Coast Guard to enforce a rule requiring ferries to blast their horns before departing docks, residents of Battery Park City say they are losing sleep.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/23/ferry-horns-a-safety-regulation-cause-disturbance-in-battery-park-city/', 'snippet': 'After kayakers persuaded the Coast Guard to enforce a rule requiring ferries to blast their horns before departing docks, residents of Battery Park City say they are losing sleep.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Three years ago, residents of Battery Park City in Lower Manhattan complained about noisy engines of ferryboats. Now, the ferries’ horns have some of them squawking and blaming letter-of-the law kayakers for their lost sleep.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/24/nyregion/23cityroom-kayaks/23cityroom-kayaks-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 384, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/24/nyregion/23cityroom-kayaks/23cityroom-kayaks-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 384}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/24/nyregion/23cityroom-kayaks/23cityroom-kayaks-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 656, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/24/nyregion/23cityroom-kayaks/23cityroom-kayaks-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1311, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/24/nyregion/23cityroom-kayaks/23cityroom-kayaks-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/24/nyregion/23cityroom-kayaks/23cityroom-kayaks-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/24/nyregion/23cityroom-kayaks/23cityroom-kayaks-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ferry Horns, a Safety Regulation, Cause Disturbance in Battery Park City', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ferries', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Kayaks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Noise', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Coast Guard', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Battery Park City (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hudson River', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-23T15:57:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick McGeehan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGeehan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2c613763-357d-5ffd-a147-a9300577dde0', 'word_count': 722, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2c613763-357d-5ffd-a147-a9300577dde0'}, {'abstract': 'A day in the life of armed America.', 'web_url': 'https://nocera.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/23/the-gun-report-july-23-2013/', 'snippet': 'A day in the life of armed America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When last we left Heidi Yewman, she had written the first part of a series for Ms. Magazine about buying a gun and carrying it around for 30 days, just so she could recount the experience. But Ms., inundated by nasty emails and comments from gun advocates, concluded that it lacked the resources to handle the onslaught—and it didn’t like the tenor of the conversation that Yewman’s article had generated. So it spiked the rest of her series. In the Gun Report a few weeks ago, I characterized this as a craven act of self-censorship, allowing gun proponents to effectively shut down a piece of reporting they didn’t like.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Gun Report: July 23, 2013', 'kicker': 'Joe Nocera', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-07-23T15:59:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joe Nocera', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nocera', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c4a46764-e5c1-5077-846d-36fffdc6696c', 'word_count': 1188, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c4a46764-e5c1-5077-846d-36fffdc6696c'}, {'abstract': 'At the 11the annual Tales of the Cocktail convention in New Orleans, revelers embraced large-batch, high-proof drinks served in giant bowls.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/23/all-the-rage-the-next-great-cocktail-craze-boozy-punches/', 'snippet': 'At the 11the annual Tales of the Cocktail convention in New Orleans, revelers embraced large-batch, high-proof drinks served in giant bowls.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When 22,000 bartenders, distillers, industry reps and cocktail enthusiasts gathered in New Orleans last week for the 11th annual Tales of the Cocktail convention, one truth emerged: punches are back. Gin punches complemented sour flavors. Whiskey punches swirled with citrus. Nearly every event featured a bowl at the bar that was regularly drained and refilled. At the Absolut Welcome Party, 16 rooms of the French Quarter’s historic Arnaud’s Restaurant were transformed into a maze of bespoke bars and boîtes, featuring punch-bowl fountains of such classic concoctions as the sea breeze, the corpse reviver and the honey pot. And at the closing day’s annual Pig & Punch fund-raiser, herbaceous Jagermeister and Barenjager Honey Liqueur punctuated flowing bowls of tequila and plum-wine punch.The colonial-era precursor to the cocktail has been enjoying a renaissance ever since the release of the cocktail historian David Wondrich’s 2010 book, “Punch: The Delights (and Dangers) of the Flowing Bowl.” Typically comprised of five ingredients (spirit, citrus, sugar, spice, water), punches have grown in popularity in part because of their low-maintenance preparation — all that’s required before serving is floating a giant ice cube in a massive bowl.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'All the Rage | The Next Great Cocktail Craze? Boozy Punches', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'All The Range: Bowl of Plenty', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ALCOHOLIC BEVERAGES', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cocktails', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Orleans (La)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-23T16:00:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lauren Viera', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lauren', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Viera', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c273659c-8162-58a3-bb21-52eaef04ac7c', 'word_count': 481, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c273659c-8162-58a3-bb21-52eaef04ac7c'}, {'abstract': 'Entering one of the hottest categories in food today, Starbucks will sell a yogurt jointly branded with Danone of France.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/24/business/starbucks-and-danone-joining-forces-to-sell-yogurt.html', 'snippet': 'Entering one of the hottest categories in food today, Starbucks will sell a yogurt jointly branded with Danone of France.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Starbucks is taking on the thriving market for yogurt, teaming up with French dairy powerhouse Danone to create a line of yogurts that will be sold in the coffee company’s stores and in grocery stores.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/24/business/24yogurt/24yogurt-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 398, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/24/business/24yogurt/24yogurt-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 398}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/24/business/24yogurt/24yogurt-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 680, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/24/business/24yogurt/24yogurt-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1359, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/24/business/24yogurt/24yogurt-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/24/business/24yogurt/24yogurt-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/24/business/24yogurt/24yogurt-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Want a Yogurt With That Venti Latte? Starbucks and Danone to Join Forces ', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How About a Yogurt With That Venti Latte?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Strom, Stephanie', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Danone SA', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Starbucks Corporation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Yogurt', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Riboud, Franck', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schultz, Howard D', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-23T16:01:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephanie Strom', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephanie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Strom', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/353adf44-394c-5306-a486-cc5978a83d76', 'word_count': 943, 'uri': 'nyt://article/353adf44-394c-5306-a486-cc5978a83d76'}, {'abstract': 'The tragic glory of one of the Union’s most bloodied units.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/23/the-iron-brigade/', 'snippet': 'The tragic glory of one of the Union’s most bloodied units.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Though destined for fame and glory, the Western Brigade inspired neither awe nor praise at its May 1861 debut in Washington. The only all-Western infantry brigade serving in the Union’s Eastern Theater armies, it included five colorfully named regiments: the Calico (6th Wisconsin), the Huckleberries (7th Wisconsin), the Babies (19th Indiana) and the Ragged Asstetical (2nd Wisconsin), which won its not-so-fierce nom de guerre, said the veteran Aubrey Cullen, “from the fact that the government contractors had run short of good material when they made the pantaloons … allowing their flag of truce always to be kept in their rear.” The 24th Michigan, last to join the Brigade in October 1862, was called the Feather-beds. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Iron Brigade', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Gettysburg (Pa)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Wisconsin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-23T16:07:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jean Huets', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jean', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Huets', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8155c0fc-d419-5603-9ed0-5537b789f455', 'word_count': 2250, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8155c0fc-d419-5603-9ed0-5537b789f455'}, {'abstract': 'Tomatoes and summer squash make for delicious taco fillings.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/24/health/soft-tacos-with-roasted-or-grilled-tomatoes-and-summer-squash.html', 'snippet': 'Tomatoes and summer squash make for delicious taco fillings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you’ve got the grill fired up you can cook the vegetables in a grill pan. Otherwise roast the tomatoes under the broiler and cook the filling on top of the stove.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/24/science/24recipehealth/24recipehealth-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 428, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/24/science/24recipehealth/24recipehealth-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 428}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/24/science/24recipehealth/24recipehealth-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 730, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/24/science/24recipehealth/24recipehealth-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1461, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/24/science/24recipehealth/24recipehealth-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 