l': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/23/feeling-for-wireless-portable-speakers-that-will-dress-up-any-room/', 'snippet': 'The new line Grain Audio encases its audio products in solid walnut, infusing technology with an appealing dose of design.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In my dream house, I can hide the entertainment system with the click of a remote control that slides a panel closed or lowers a screen. But a new Kickstarter-financed company called Grain Audio is making audio products, including the above Packable Wireless System (PWS), that look as great on a Saarinen table as they sound — and because they’re portable they don’t need to be worked into the design of a room. Eschewing the glossy black boxes that encase so many tech products, Mitch Wenger, the president of Grain Audio, and his team tested dozens of species of wood before settling on solid walnut. “Because every piece of wood is so different in the grain structure, each one of these products is unique to the owner,” said Chris Weir, the company’s vice president of design, whose background is in furniture design and architecture. A gorgeous, one-of-a-kind BlueTooth speaker for $249? Don’t mind if I do.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Feeling For | Wireless Portable Speakers That Will Dress Up Any Room', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-07-23T18:00:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alainna Lexie Beddie', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alainna', 'middlename': 'Lexie', 'lastname': 'Beddie', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2184b85c-f2ad-56b8-b6df-dbd9c13ec1c3', 'word_count': 166, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2184b85c-f2ad-56b8-b6df-dbd9c13ec1c3'}, {'abstract': 'Men work longer hours than women, even when both are working full-time. And high school grads put in the longest workdays when you sort by education.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/23/who-works-the-longest-days/', 'snippet': 'Men work longer hours than women, even when both are working full-time. And high school grads put in the longest workdays when you sort by education.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As I’ve written before, the pay gap between men and women narrows when you control for occupation. Another factor that may play a role: the hours that men work compared with women, even when they’re both technically employed full time (which means usually working at least 35 hours a week).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Works the Longest Days', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Men and Boys', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Working Hours', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-23T18:00:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7eed573c-685f-56a3-bbfc-5af503c4ab30', 'word_count': 190, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7eed573c-685f-56a3-bbfc-5af503c4ab30'}, {'abstract': 'Who is who in the race for mayor.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2013/07/28/magazine/28mayor_ss.html', 'snippet': 'Who is who in the race for mayor.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Who is who