icle', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeannette Catsoulis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeannette', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Catsoulis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/aad7704a-2e57-531d-b860-268ad88a48e0', 'word_count': 301, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aad7704a-2e57-531d-b860-268ad88a48e0'}, {'abstract': 'In the Italian film “Terraferma,” a flood of African refugees complicate the lives of a struggling fisherman and his family.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/24/movies/emanuele-crialese-tackles-migration-in-terraferma.html', 'snippet': 'In the Italian film “Terraferma,” a flood of African refugees complicate the lives of a struggling fisherman and his family.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Somebody else almost always has it worse, “Terraferma” reminds us, but that’s not a comfort in this latest film from Emanuele Crialese. Far from it. For a fisherman and his family struggling to make a living in the 21st century on a Sicilian island, a flood of illegal African immigrants brings moral questions and other complications to their doorstep. Playing out the consequences of that encounter on the family yields dramatic moments of decision, but what comes afterward feels more like a show of resolve than compelling drama.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/24/arts/filmroundup-4/filmroundup-4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 404, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/24/arts/filmroundup-4/filmroundup-4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 404}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/24/arts/filmrou