senberg” across the border from New to Old Mexico and foreshadowed his clashes with the cartel competition. “This homie is already dead; he just doesn’t know it yet,” the lyrics warned, and of course the joke was that, at the time, Walter’s unlikely career as a drug kingpin was made possible by exactly such knowledge. As far as he or anyone else could tell — apart, perhaps, from Vince Gilligan, the creator of the series and the ultimate master of Walter’s fate — cancer would finish Heisenberg long before any cartel hit man got to him.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/arts/28BREAKING6/28BREAKING-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 710, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/28/arts/28BREAKING6/28BREAKING-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 710}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/arts/28BREAKING6/28BREAKING-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 866, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/arts/28BREAKING6/28BREAKING-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1732, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/arts/28BREAKING6/28BREAKING-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/28/arts/28BREAKING6/28BREAKING-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/arts/28BREAKING6/28BREAKING-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bad in the Bones', 'kicker': 'Cross Cuts', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bad in the Bones', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Breaking Bad (TV Program)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cranston, Bryan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-24T14:48:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fc934dd1-7fb7-5740-a8ee-0b8cfa6298ff', 'word_count': 2090, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fc934dd1-7fb7-5740-a8ee-0b8cfa6298ff'}, {'abstract': 'The soft, floppy headwear, beloved by fishermen and ’80s rappers alike, is making a welcome comeback.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/24/feeling-for-the-timeless-warm-weather-style-of-the-bucket-hat/', 'snippet': 'The soft, floppy headwear, beloved by fishermen and ’80s rappers alike, is making a welcome comeback.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The bucket hat — that staple of ’80s hip-hop culture that was popularized by stars like Run-D.M.C. and LL Cool J, then trotted out again by Johnny Depp in 1998’s “Fear and Loathing in Las Vegas” — is making a comeback. First, the floppy-brimmed headpiece, a distant descendant of the hats worn by fishermen to ward off the elements, was spotted on the heads of performers like Usher, Kid Cudi and Schoolboy Q at South by Southwest, the annual music jamboree in Austin, Tex. Then it surfaced in a number of men’s runway shows in Europe last month, including Margaret Howell, Shaun Samson and Carven. This past week, Laurent Bourven, a co-founder of the French brand Arpenteur, showed up at the men’s trade show Capsule New York sporting one from his own line. You don’t have to wait till next spring when the most recently shown men’s collections hit stores to get yours. The Swedish brand Our Legacy is offering this smart, sleek version made from nylon.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Feeling For | The Timeless Warm-Weather Style of the Bucket Hat', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hats and Caps', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-24T14:55:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alex Tudela', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alex', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tudela', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e6cb5fb5-600d-590d-946a-520a1b8dabf9', 'word_count': 173, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e6cb5fb5-600d-590d-946a-520a1b8dabf9'}, {'abstract': 'The lawyer helping Edward J. Snowden said more work was needed on his asylum request.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/25/world/europe/snowden.html', 'snippet': 'The lawyer helping Edward J. Snowden said more work was needed on his asylum request.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MOSCOW — After a month holed up in the transit zone of Moscow’s Sheremetyevo airport, Edward J. Snowden, the former intelligence contractor sought by the United States on espionage charges, received a change of clothes and a copy of “Crime and Punishment” during a meeting with his lawyer on Wednesday, but still no clearance to enter Russia.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/25/world/europe/25snowden/25snowden-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/25/world/europe/25snowden/25snowden-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/25/world/europe/25snowden/25snowden-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/25/world/europe/25snowden/25snowden-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1152, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/25/world/europe/25snowden/25snowden-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/25/world/europe/25snowden/25snowden-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/25/world/europe/25snowden/25snowden-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Snowden Gets Novel and Change of Clothes, but No Clearance to Exit Airport', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Snowden Gets Novel and Change of Clothes, but No Clearance to Exit Airport', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Snowden, Edward J', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Moscow (Russia)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Surveillance of Citizens by Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-24T14:59:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By David M. Herszenhorn and Steven Lee Myers', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/66dea0b2-572d-587d-b81e-3b7163523a53', 'word_count': 721, 'uri': 'nyt://article/66dea0b2-572d-587d-b81e-3b7163523a53'}, {'abstract': 'A large new study suggests that Gary Solon, a Michigan State economist, was correct when he argued years ago that upward mobility is less common in the United States than many people imagine.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/24/a-mobility-prophet/', 'snippet': 'A large new study suggests that Gary Solon, a Michigan State economist, was correct when he argued years ago that upward mobility is less common in the United States than many people imagine.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Only 26 years ago, one of the country’s most respected economists argued that social mobility was a problem largely solved. Birth rarely dictated a child’s destiny, suggested Gary Becker, in a 1987 speech to fellow economists. Children born rich often ended up poor, and children born poor often ended up rich. “Low earnings as well as high earnings,” said Mr. Becker, a University of Chicago professor who won a Nobel five years later, “are not strongly transmitted from fathers to sons.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Mobility Prophet', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Inequality', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 