], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bf2bf7cc-814d-58de-b178-82a4c7729a56', 'word_count': 531, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bf2bf7cc-814d-58de-b178-82a4c7729a56'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Kizzia’s new book tells the story of a violent patriarch and his large family living in remote Alaska.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/24/a-fathers-wrath-tom-kizzia-talks-about-pilgrims-wilderness/', 'snippet': 'Mr. Kizzia’s new book tells the story of a violent patriarch and his large family living in remote Alaska.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Not since “The Shining” has family life off the grid seemed as terrifying as it does in “Pilgrim’s Wilderness,” by Tom Kizzia, but this time the chills come from nonfiction. Mr. Kizzia tells the story of Robert Hale, a Texan who went on to call himself Papa Pilgrim as the patriarch of 15 children. Pilgrim eventually landed in Alaska on protected land, where several local allies adopted him as a heroic pioneer-antagonist of the National Park Service. Others bristled at Pilgrim’s self-styled messianism, and all the while his children suffered greatly under his rule. In a recent e-mail interview, Mr. Kizzia discussed whether he ever felt in danger while reporting the book, the keys that helped him unlock the full story and more. Below are edited excerpts from the conversation:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/25/books/25kizzia/25kizzia-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 500, 'width': 500, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/25/books/25kizzia/25kizzia-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 500, 'width': 500, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/0