pace with the news cycle.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We’ve all heard of fast-breaking stories but something relatively new in the newspaper world is the fast-breaking editorial.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Real Time, Calling for Anthony Weiner to Step Out of the Mayor’s Race', 'kicker': 'The Public Editor&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-24T21:09:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Margaret Sullivan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Margaret', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sullivan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/72901aed-e134-5014-96f9-21043ef82f46', 'word_count': 599, 'uri': 'nyt://article/72901aed-e134-5014-96f9-21043ef82f46'}, {'abstract': 'Dell was presented with a twist on the practice of corporations moving overseas to take advantage of lower taxes but thought it would create image problems.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/07/24/dell-considered-novel-tax-strategy-in-buyout/', 'snippet': 'Dell was presented with a twist on the practice of corporations moving overseas to take advantage of lower taxes but thought it would create image problems.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the proposed buyout of Dell by its founder, the company considered but rejected as too risky a novel strategy that tweaks the now-curbed practice of corporations moving overseas to take advantage of lower taxes.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dell Considered Novel Tax Strategy in Buyout', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tax Strategy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corporate Taxes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tax Evasion', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dell Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-24T21:10:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lynnley Browning', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynnley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Browning', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a8e552fc-0e77-52ef-9801-3dca034f409e', 'word_count': 909, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a8e552fc-0e77-52ef-9801-3dca034f409e'}, {'abstract': 'Ray LaHood, the former transportation secretary, said that he wanted to see the tech and car industries consistently warn consumers about the risks, just as beer companies have done with drunken driving.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/24/lahood-says-companies-must-wake-up-to-distracted-driving/', 'snippet': 'Ray LaHood, the former transportation secretary, said that he wanted to see the tech and car industries consistently warn consumers about the risks, just as beer companies have done with drunken driving.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Ray LaHood was transportation secretary, he wasn’t one to mince words about the risks of driving with a cellphone. But in an interview this week, just a few weeks after leaving the cabinet, he put a particularly fine point on his concerns, saying that car companies and technology companies must wake up to the deadly dangers their products can pose. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/25/business/24bits-lahood/24bits-lahood-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/25/business/24bits-lahood/24bits-lahood-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/25/business/24bits-lahood/24bits-lahood-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/25/business/24bits-lahood/24bits-lahood-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/25/business/24bits-lahood/24bits-lahood-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/25/business/24bits-lahood/24bits-lahood-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/25/business/24bits-lahood/24bits-lahood-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'LaHood Says Companies Must Wake Up to Distracted Driving', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Driver Distraction and Fatigue', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Text Messaging', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'LaHood, Ray', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Transportation Department (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-24T21:10:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt Richtel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Richtel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/49d0313e-c70c-57df-a2e3-d948628a20de', 'word_count': 666, 'uri': 'nyt://article/49d0313e-c70c-57df-a2e3-d948628a20de'}, {'abstract': 'Gaming a heat wave is no walk in the park. Designers share their strategies.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/25/fashion/manning-up-in-the-heat.html', 'snippet': 'Gaming a heat wave is no walk in the park. Designers share their strategies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Unlike showy thunderstorms that grab you by the lapels and shout “Wear your galoshes!” heat waves are like wolves in wool clothing, causing hellish discomfort in the disguise of a sunny summer day. Some of us are fooled: we put on a brave face, our usual clothes, and go to work, where we arrive in a damp, disheveled heap. But gaming a heat wave is no walk in the park. The usual codes of summer style go right out the window (if you’re daft enough to have left it open). At a certain point (say, 90 degrees), a new set of objectives takes over. Questions like “What would look sexy?” or “What would look proper?” are brushed aside, leaving one desperate quest: how to get through the next 12 hours with the least amount of suffering.', 'print_section': 'E', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/25/fashion/25MENSUMMERSPAN/25MENSUMMERSPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/25/fashion/25MENSUMMERSPAN/25MENSUMMERSPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 576}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None,