https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/25/garden/the-storm-erases-the-drawing-board-replaces.html', 'snippet': 'Two landscape designers give guidance on how to repair a landscape after destruction from a storm.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s hard to be optimistic when you’re restoring a landscape after a major storm. Trucks tear up your lawn, workers drag fallen logs across your shrubs, the ground cover you carefully nurtured is buried in wood chips, and contractors empty your bank account. It’s enough to keep a gardener indoors with the shades drawn. What to do?', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/25/garden/25GARDEN_SPAN/25GARDEN1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 365, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/25/garden/25GARDEN_SPAN/25GARDEN1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 365}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/25/garden/25GARDEN_SPAN/25GARDEN1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 623, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/25/garden/25GARDEN_SPAN/25GARDEN1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1245, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/25/garden/25GARDEN_SPAN/25GARDEN1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/25/garden/25GARDEN_SPAN/25GARDEN1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/25/garden/25GARDEN_SPAN/25GARDEN1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Storm Erases, the Drawing Board Replaces', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Storm Erases, the Drawing Board Replaces', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gardens and Gardening', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Trees and Shrubs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Westchester County (NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricane Sandy (2012)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricane Irene (2011)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Landscaping', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-24T22:13:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sara Barrett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barrett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f2ce3aca-306b-565c-a6b5-a5a92ba75d32', 'word_count': 583, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f2ce3aca-306b-565c-a6b5-a5a92ba75d32'}, {'abstract': 'Furniture made in Nicaragua from local tempest-tossed wood.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/25/garden/new-uses-for-rescued-timber.html', 'snippet': 'Furniture made in Nicaragua from local tempest-tossed wood.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last year, Aaron Poritz, 28, an architect and Massachusetts native, was visiting a friend in Nicaragua when he stumbled upon a source of guilt-free exotic wood. “I found that people were legally pulling lumber out of the region affected by Hurricane Felix,” he said. “The trees are hundreds of years old, they fell naturally, and were just rotting.” Until then, Mr. Poritz had designed furniture as a hobby, but now he was emboldened to start a business. This month, he introduced a 20-piece wood furniture collection made in Nicaragua by local workers. The line, which he said is meant to express “a balance between beauty and form and functionality and comfort,” includes chairs, stools, tables, desks, beds and a sofa. Prices range from $190 for a toolbox to $3,095 for a dining table. Information: (917) 755-2948 or aaronporitz.com.\xa0', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/25/garden/25FURNITURE/25CURR4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/25/garden/25FURNITURE/25CURR4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/25/garden/25FURNITURE/25CURR4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/25/garden/25FURNITURE/25CURR4-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/25/garden/25FURNITURE/25CURR4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/25/garden/25FURNITURE/25CURR4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/25/garden/25FURNITURE/25CURR4-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thu