'Waterfalls', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Swimming', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-24T22:16:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joshua Lyon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joshua', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lyon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0e9f69da-69a2-55ce-a7c5-586de97cba40', 'word_count': 1403, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0e9f69da-69a2-55ce-a7c5-586de97cba40'}, {'abstract': 'Tony Duquette’s Splashing Water chandeliers are put into production by Remains Lighting.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/25/garden/bathed-in-the-glow-of-splashing-water.html', 'snippet': 'Tony Duquette’s Splashing Water chandeliers are put into production by Remains Lighting.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As much theatrical designer as interior designer, Tony Duquette occupied a studio on Robertson Boulevard in Los Angeles that featured a “tray ceiling” made of gold plastic trays. In 1956, five years before he won the Tony Award for his costumes for “Camelot” on Broadway, he created the exuberant Splashing Water chandelier for the space. “That was an interesting challenge for us, to find a way to retain the lightness of the original fixtures made by hand, but make them consistent,” said Alix Calligeros, an owner of Remains Lighting, in Brooklyn, which licensed the chandeliers from Mr. Duquette’s estate. Splashing Water is $7,125 to $10,495, depending on size (37 inches or 54 inches in diameter) and material (the example shown is powder-coated steel; other versions come in brass in a variety of finishes). Available at Remains showrooms. Information: remains.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image