ject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Warren, Elizabeth', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sanders, Bernard', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-24T23:25:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeremy W. Peters', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeremy', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Peters', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8f25a54b-cfe4-5d9a-a2e9-ab911fc1609c', 'word_count': 673, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8f25a54b-cfe4-5d9a-a2e9-ab911fc1609c'}, {'abstract': 'The announcement signals a shift for the industry, which in the past has resisted calls to systematically share its data.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/25/business/drug-companies-promise-more-data-transparency.html', 'snippet': 'The announcement signals a shift for the industry, which in the past has resisted calls to systematically share its data.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Representatives of the world’s biggest pharmaceutical companies pledged on Wednesday to release detailed data about their drugs to outside researchers, a move that was applauded by some but also seen as an effort to head off more extensive disclosure requirements that are under review in Europe.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Drug Companies Promise More Data Transparency', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Drug Companies Promise More Data Transparency', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drugs (Pharmaceuticals)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thomas, Katie', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pharmaceutical Research and Manufacturers of America', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Data-Mining and Database Marketing', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Medicines Agency', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Clinical Trials', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-24T23:25:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katie Thomas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Thomas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/59118038-8b93-5678-8613-665fe11ee437', 'word_count': 556, 'uri': 'nyt://article/59118038-8b93-5678-8613-665fe11ee437'}, {'abstract': 'The 217-205 vote was far closer than expected and displayed the shifting allegiances and fierce lobbying on both sides of the issue.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/25/us/politics/house-defeats-effort-to-rein-in-nsa-data-gathering.html', 'snippet': 'The 217-205 vote was far closer than expected and displayed the shifting allegiances and fierce lobbying on both sides of the issue.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — A deeply divided House defeated legislation Wednesday that would have blocked the National Security Agency from collecting vast amounts of phone records, handing the Obama administration a hard-fought victory in the first Congressional showdown over the N.S.A.’s surveillance activities since Edward J. Snowden’s security breaches last month.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'House Defeats Effort to Rein In N.S.A. Data Gathering', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'House’s Attempt  to Rein In N.S.A. Narrowly Fails', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classified Information and State Secrets', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Surveillance of Citizens by Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Security Agency', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Snowden, Edward J', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Privacy', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-24T23:38:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Weisman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weisman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/67baf399-c21a-516b-84ec-473054ae780b', 'word_count': 1041, 'uri': 'nyt://article/67baf399-c21a-516b-84ec-473054ae780b'}, {'abstract': 'Ten million dollars will go to victims of a eugenics program that for decades forced people who social workers said were developmentally disabled to undergo sterilization.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/25/us/north-carolina-budget-pays-eugenics-victims.html', 'snippet': 'Ten million dollars will go to victims of a eugenics program that for decades forced people who social workers said were developmentally disabled to undergo sterilization.', 'lead_paragraph': 'State lawmakers have agreed to compensate victims of a eugenics program that for decades forced people who social workers said were developmentally disabled to undergo sterilization. Although 32 states practiced eugenics, thought at the time to strengthen the gene pool and reduce poverty, North Carolina is the first to pay victims. Lawmakers on Wednesday passed a budget that includes $10 million for victims. A state board ran the program from 1933 to 1977, sterilizing about 7,600 people. Only about 200 have been identified, though others are likely to come forward. “It’s the most egregious taking that government could possibly be guilty of,” said the House speaker, Thom Tillis, a Republican. “It was critical to close this chapter in our history.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'North Carolina: Budget Pays Eugenics Victims', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | South', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'North Carolina: Budget Pays Eugenics Victims', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sterilization (Biological)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Carolina', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Compensation for Damages (Law)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-24T23:42:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kim Severson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Severson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/79e52b9e-e6c4-53e8-a06d-4e9fe57cac76', 'word_count': 120, 'uri': 'nyt://article/79e52b9e-e6c4-53e8-a06d-4e9fe57cac76'}, {'abstract': 'Coming to understand a life’s peculiar loneliness.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/24/my-other-self/', 'snippet': 'Coming to understand a life’s peculiar loneliness.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I was 6 years old when I first heard I had a twin brother. My family — my father, my mother and I — had recently crossed the border from Mexico to settle in Brownsville, Tex. My maternal grandmother, Mamá Concha, lived there, too, in a little house of her own. I was always left in her care when my parents went out for a late night. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'My Other Self', 'kicker': 'Private Lives', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-25T00:00:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Carlos Blake', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'Carlos', 'lastname': 'Blake', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/09813b29-9ad9-55fb-8cd1-ed6d8e460342', 'word_count': 1216, 'uri': 'nyt://article/09813b29-9ad9-55fb-8cd1-ed6d8e460342'}, {'abstract': 'Less ice along the Northeast Passage means Russian energy companies have more places to drill and a clear shipping lane to reach customers in China.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/25/business/energy-environment/polar-thaw-opens-shortcut-for-russian-natural-gas.html', 'snippet': 'Less ice along the Northeast Passage means Russian energy companies have more places to drill and a clear shipping lane to reach customers in China.', 'lead_paragraph': 'YURKHAROVSKOYE GAS FIELD, Russia — The polar ice cap is melting, and if executives at the Russian energy company Novatek feel guilty about profiting from that, they do not let it be known in public.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/25/business/25arctic-span/25arctic-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'a