y The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/2af3dc78-62cf-577c-8d06-36aa8d1e70a5', 'word_count': 275, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2af3dc78-62cf-577c-8d06-36aa8d1e70a5'}, {'abstract': 'Loan servicers and banks have kept the money they received, even though more than a third of homeowners who received loan modifications have defaulted again, a report has found.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/25/business/new-defaults-trouble-a-mortgage-program.html', 'snippet': 'Loan servicers and banks have kept the money they received, even though more than a third of homeowners who received loan modifications have defaulted again, a report has found.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Banks and other mortgage servicers have accepted $815 million in taxpayer-funded incentives for helping homeowners who have since redefaulted on their home loans, a watchdog for the Treasury Department’s Troubled Asset Relief Program, or TARP, reported on Wednesday. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Defaults Trouble a Mortgage Program', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New Defaults Trouble a Mortgage Program', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dewan, Shaila', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homeowner Affordability and Stability Plan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mortgages', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Foreclosures', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Treasury Department', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-25T01:22:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shaila Dewan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shaila', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dewan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/853b3f5b-20a9-57d5-a2d1-79ff7b65bea0', 'word_count': 663, 'uri': 'nyt://article/853b3f5b-20a9-57d5-a2d1-79ff7b65bea0'}, {'abstract': 'After years of trying to pin criminal charges on the billionaire Steven A. Cohen, the authorities came together on a more unusual plan: indict Mr. Cohen’s hedge fund itself.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/07/24/indictment-of-fund-is-a-rare-and-bold-move/', 'snippet': 'After years of trying to pin criminal charges on the billionaire Steven A. Cohen, the authorities came together on a more unusual plan: indict Mr. Cohen’s hedge fund itself.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Steven A. Cohen’s lawyers arrived for a meeting this spring at the United States attorney’s offices in Lower Manhattan, in a Brutalist-style building tucked behind a pair of courthouses, the conference room was so packed with federal investigators that one official had to venture down the hall for additional chairs. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Case Against SAC Capital, a Show of Force', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Case Against Hedge Fund, a Show of Force', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-07-25T01:22:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Protess and Peter Lattman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Protess', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lattman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3f79ba14-f7f6-5ef7-973a-2bb5f3945564', 'word_count': 1512, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3f79ba14-f7f6-5ef7-973a-2bb5f3945564'}, {'abstract': 'Will America ever have effective ground-based missile defense?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/25/opinion/a-failure-to-intercept.html', 'snippet': 'Will America ever have effective ground-based missile defense?', 'lead_paragraph': 'After 30 years of research and an estimated $250 billion investment, the Pentagon’s defense program against intercontinental ballistic missiles from adversaries like Iran and North Korea had another failed test this month. The advanced missile interceptor launched on July 5 from Vandenberg Air Force Base in California failed to hit its target over the Pacific Ocean, the third consecutive dud. The military has tested the ground-based midcourse defense system 16 times; only eight were successful, the last in 2008. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Failure to Intercept', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Failure to Intercept', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Missiles and Missile Defense Systems', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Defense Department', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-25T01:24:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/2e9d8b21-a404-5281-b2af-288b940b9ff5', 'word_count': 426, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2e9d8b21-a404-5281-b2af-288b940b9ff5'}, {'abstract': 'A consent decree will bring New York into full compliance with federal disability law and improve the lives of some of the most vulnerable.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/25/opinion/justice-for-the-mentally-disabled.html', 'snippet': 'A consent decree will bring New York into full compliance with federal disability law and improve the lives of some of the most vulnerable.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gov. Andrew Cuomo closed out a shameful period in New York’s history earlier this week when he agreed to give about 4,000 mentally ill people held in highly restrictive institutional settings the option of moving into supported housing, where they can live independently with the help of social service organizations. The agreement, outlined in a consent decree filed in federal court in New York City, ends a long legal battle and could bring a new day for people isolated in inadequate, for-profit residences that make their disabilities that much harder to bear. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Justice for the Mentally Disabled', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Justice for the Mentally Disabled', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuomo, Andrew M', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Group Homes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Disabilities', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mental Health and Disorders', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-25T01:25:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editor