of', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-25T06:48:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt Siegel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Siegel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/243a5608-751d-5ad0-888c-9572ba358486', 'word_count': 631, 'uri': 'nyt://article/243a5608-751d-5ad0-888c-9572ba358486'}, {'abstract': 'Credit Suisse is joining Goldman Sachs and other Wall Street rivals in reporting improved results for the second quarter, helped by an increase in client activity in sales and trading and investment banking.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/07/25/credit-suisse-reports-2nd-quarter-profit-rise/', 'snippet': 'Credit Suisse is joining Goldman Sachs and other Wall Street rivals in reporting improved results for the second quarter, helped by an increase in client activity in sales and trading and investment banking.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON – Credit Suisse, one of Switzerland’s largest banks, said on Thursday that profit in the second quarter rose by a third, thanks to higher earnings at its investment banking operation.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Credit Suisse Posts $1.1 Billion Profit', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Company Reports', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dougan, Brady W', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Credit Suisse Group AG', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Switzerland', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-25T07:23:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Werdigier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Werdigier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c3e0d024-8072-5d99-8aaa-9ff994761a99', 'word_count': 490, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c3e0d024-8072-5d99-8aaa-9ff994761a99'}, {'abstract': 'A man with a knife killed two government workers at a family planning bureau after the office refused to register his fourth child for residency.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/26/world/asia/killing-highlights-concerns-over-chinas-one-child-policy.html', 'snippet': 'A man with a knife killed two government workers at a family planning bureau after the office refused to register his fourth child for residency.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HONG KONG — A man with a knife killed two government officials at a family planning bureau and injured four other people, the Chinese state news media reported on Wednesday, in an episode that drew wide attention in China because of continuing controversy over the country’s one-child policy.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Killing Puts Focus on Concerns Over China’s One-Child Policy', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Killing Puts Focus on Concerns Over China’s One-Child Policy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bradsher, Keith', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Birth Control and Family Planning', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pregnancy and Childbirth', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-25T07:29:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Keith Bradsher', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Keith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bradsher', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d9961816-cd39-5ba9-9da1-16c670c95f32', 'word_count': 273, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d9961816-cd39-5ba9-9da1-16c670c95f32'}, {'abstract': 'The resilience of an Indian acid attack survivor makes the country’s Supreme Court amend India’s criminal law.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/25/an-acid-attack-survivor-makes-india-to-change-its-law/', 'snippet': 'The resilience of an Indian acid attack survivor makes the country’s Supreme Court amend India’s criminal law.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NEW DELHI – On the morning of April 23, 2005, a seventh-grade student, Laxmi (who uses only one name), was waiting for a bus outside the Khan Market shopping complex in central Delhi. She had been hassled for the past few months. A friend’s older brother, Naim Khan, 32, a tailor, had been stalking Laxmi and insisting that she marry him. Laxmi had repeatedly warded him off. As she waited for a bus, Mr. Khan appeared on a motorcycle with a woman. His female companion charged at Laxmi, pushed her onto the road, and poured a bottle of acid on her face. Moments later, the woman and Mr. Khan sped away on the motorcycle.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/25/world/asia/25-student-protest-IndiaInk/25-student-protest-IndiaInk-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 404, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/25/world/asia/25-student-protest-IndiaInk/25-student-protest-IndiaInk-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 404}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/25/world/asia/25-student-protest-IndiaInk/25-student-protest-IndiaInk-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 690, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/25/world/asia/25-student-protest-IndiaInk/25-student-protest-IndiaInk-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1380, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/25/world/asia/25-student-protest-IndiaInk/25-student-protest-IndiaInk-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/25/world/asia/25-student-protest-IndiaInk/25-student-protest-IndiaInk-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/25/world/asia/25-student-protest-IndiaInk/25-student-protest-IndiaInk-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Acid Attack Survivor Helps India Change Its Laws', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACIDS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court of India', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Delhi (India)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Delhi (India)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-25T07:32:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hari Kumar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hari', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kumar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6f8b1863-e03c-542b-95d7-440fa0c3d77d', 'word_count': 844, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6f8b1863-e03c-542b-95d7-440fa0c3d77d'}, {'abstract': 'When the Sunil Tripathi rumor went viral after the Boston Marathon bombing, it laid bare the dysfunctional codependence between new and old media.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/28/magazine/should-reddit-be-blamed-for-the-spreading-of-a-smear.html', 'snippet': 'When the Sunil Tripathi rumor went viral after the Boston Marathon bombing, it laid bare the dysfunctional codependence between new and old media.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On an overcast day in early May, I traveled to suburban Philadelphia to visit the family of Sunil Tripathi, the deceased 22-year-old Brown University student who, for about four hours on the morning of April 19, was mistakenly identified as Suspect No. 2 in the Boston Marathon bombings. The Tripathis had just arrived home after nearly two months spent in Providence, R.I., where they went to organize the search for Sunil, who disappeared on March 16. When I entered the house, Judy Tripathi, Sunil’s mother, asked me for a hug. In a shattered voice, she said, “I need hugs these days.” We sat at the kitchen table and talked, and at one point Judy handed me a photo of a young, smiling Sunil, caught in the motion of throwing a ball. “Look how happy he looks,” she said. For the next two hours, she and her husband, Akhil, and their daughter, Sangeeta, described what happened to them in the early-morning hours of April 19, and how the false identification of their son derailed their ongoing search for him and further traumatized their lives.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '36', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/magazine/28reddit_span/28reddit_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 375, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/28/magazine/28reddit_span/28reddit_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 375}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/magazine/28reddit_span/28reddit_span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 640, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/magazine/28reddit_span/28reddit_span-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 781, 'width': 1250, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/magazine/28reddit_span/28reddit_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/28/magazine/28reddit_span/28reddit_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/magazine/28reddit_span/28reddit_span-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Should Reddit Be Blamed for the Spreading of a Smear?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Crowd-Sourcing A Smear', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kang, Jay Caspian', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Boston Marathon Bombings (2013)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Media', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Reddit Inc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tripathi, Sunil', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Boston (Mass)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Philadelphia (Pa)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-25T09:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jay Caspian Kang', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jay', 'middlename': 'Caspian', 'lastname': 'Kang', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/902d2031-2b45-5b5e-a2fe-4b1d896ac249', 'word_count': 6333, 'uri': 'nyt://article/902d2031-2b45-5b5e-a2fe-4b1d896ac249'}, {'abstract': 'A young photographer figured out how to get in the center of the action at a surprise championship basketball game.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/25/diving-for-that-winning-shot/', 'snippet': 'A young photographer figured out how to get in the center of the action at a surprise championship basketball game.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jabin Botsford, 23, is a freelance photojournalist whose work has been published in The New York Times, The Washington Post, The Wall Street Journal and Getty Images. Originally from West Palm Beach, Fla., Mr. Botsford is studying photography and sociology at Western Kentucky University and is a staff photographer at the student newspaper, The College Heights Herald. He completed an internship with The Washington Post in 2012 and is now a photo intern at The New York Times.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Diving for the Winning Shot', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-25T09:00:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Whitney Richardson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Whitney', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Richardson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a46d4282-0205-5c9f-8c80-6b36f40833f9', 'word_count': 916, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a46d4282-0205-5c9f-8c80-6b36f40833f9'}, {'abstract': 'The prosecution argues that Pfc. Bradley Manning is a traitor, but his lawyers say he is a principled protester who wanted to help society.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/26/us/politics/closing-arguments-due-in-manning-leaks-case.html', 'snippet': 'The prosecution argues that Pfc. Bradley Manning is a traitor, but his lawyers say he is a principled protester who wanted to help society.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FORT MEADE, Md. — A military prosecutor portrayed Pfc. Bradley Manning on Thursday as an “anarchist” who, seeking to “make a splash,” betrayed the United States’ trust when he leaked vast archives of secret documents to WikiLeaks, lifting a veil on American diplomatic and military activities.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url