dy-shows.html', 'snippet': 'Scientists say they have created a false memory in a mouse, providing detailed clues to how such memories may form in human brains.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The vagaries of human memory are notorious. A friend insists you were at your 15th class reunion when you know it was your 10th. You distinctly remember that another friend was at your wedding, until she reminds you that you didn’t invite her. Or, more seriously, an eyewitness misidentifies the perpetrator of a terrible crime.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Scientists Trace Memories of Things That Never Happened', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Scientists Trace Memories of Things That Never Happened', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Memory', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gorman, James', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Brain', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mice', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Massachusetts Institute of Technology', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-25T18:04:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Gorman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gorman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e879b517-199b-52be-bc2d-73532a65dd69', 'word_count': 767, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e879b517-199b-52be-bc2d-73532a65dd69'}, {'abstract': 'Part 2 of the Frugal Traveler’s summer road trip takes him to Memphis, where he searches for the tacky and the tasty while (generally) trying to avoid the touristy.', 'web_url': 'h