fier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/de436db6-c34c-5f00-a314-b7eb374b0fed', 'word_count': 1251, 'uri': 'nyt://article/de436db6-c34c-5f00-a314-b7eb374b0fed'}, {'abstract': 'Mark Leibovich eviscerates the status-obsessed denizens of our nation’s capital.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/28/books/review/this-town-by-mark-leibovich.html', 'snippet': 'Mark Leibovich eviscerates the status-obsessed denizens of our nation’s capital.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Not to ruin it for you, but: if you already hate Washington, you’re going to hate it a whole lot more after reading Mark Leibovich’s takedown of the creatures who infest our nation’s capital and rule our destinies. And in case you are deluded enough as to think they care, you’ll learn that they already hate you. He quotes his former Washington Post colleague Henry \xadAllen: \xad“Washington feels like a conspiracy we’re all in together, and nobody else in America quite understands, even though they pay for it.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/0728-bks-Buckley-SUB1/0728-bks-Buckley-SUB1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 507, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/0728-bks-Buckley-SUB1/0728-bks-Buckley-SUB1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 507}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/0728-bks-Buckley-SUB1/0728-bks-Buckley-SUB1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 866, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/0728-bks-Buckley-SUB1/0728-bks-Buckley-SUB1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1732, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/0728-bks-Buckley-SUB1/0728-bks-Buckley-SUB1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/0728-bks-Buckley-SUB1/0728-bks-Buckley-SUB1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/0728-bks-Buckley-SUB1/0728-bks-Buckley-SUB1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Confederacy of Lunches', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Confederacy of Lunches', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Buckley, Christopher', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Leibovich, Mark', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-25T19:24:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Buckley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Buckley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/87621697-7012-54b9-adbb-a2116f3b8b1e', 'word_count': 1622, 'uri': 'nyt://article/87621697-7012-54b9-adbb-a2116f3b8b1e'}, {'abstract': '“Those Angry Days,” by Lynne Olson, and “1940,” by Susan Dunn, revisit the ferocity of the disputes over America’s entrance into World War II.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/28/books/review/those-angry-days-and-1940.html', 'snippet': '“Those Angry Days,” by Lynne Olson, and “1940,” by Susan Dunn, revisit the ferocity of the disputes over America’s entrance into World War II.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In July 1939, Franklin D. Roosevelt met with senators from both political parties at the White House in a final effort to persuade them to amend the Neutrality Act preventing America from aiding other countries. After drinks were poured, Roosevelt and his secretary of state, Cordell Hull, argued that the world was approaching a catastrophic war. The 74-year-old Republican senator William Borah, who had led the fight against Woodrow Wilson and American entry into the League of Nations in 1919, shook his head in disgust. “There is not going to be any war in Europe this year,” he said. “All this hysteria is manufactured and artificial.” Two months later Hitler invaded Poland, and England and France declared war on Germany.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/0728-bks-cover-sub/0728-bks-cover-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 370, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/0728-bks-cover-sub/0728-bks-cover-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 370}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/0728-bks-cover-sub/0728-bks-cover-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 631, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/0728-bks-cover-sub/0728-bks-cover-sub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1261, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/0728-bks-cover-sub/0728-bks-cover-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/0728-bks-cover-sub/0728-bks-cover-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/0728-bks-cover-sub/0728-bks-cover-sub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'War Torn', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'War Torn', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Heilbrunn, Jacob', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "Those Angry Days: Roosevelt, Lindbergh, and America's Fight Over World War II, 1939-1941 (Book)", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Olson, Lynne', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dunn, Susan', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-25T19:25:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jacob Heilbrunn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jacob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Heilbrunn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organi