rara, Preet', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cohen, Steven A', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'SAC Capital Advisors', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-26T00:24:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Creswell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Creswell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ee34c36f-39f8-599a-b858-1af7e270fa8f', 'word_count': 1028, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ee34c36f-39f8-599a-b858-1af7e270fa8f'}, {'abstract': 'In Jamie Babbit’s “Breaking the Girls,” two friends fantasize about offing their enemies, but one turns out to be serious.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/26/movies/breaking-the-girls-a-thriller-stuffed-full-of-twists.html', 'snippet': 'In Jamie Babbit’s “Breaking the Girls,” two friends fantasize about offing their enemies, but one turns out to be serious.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jamie Babbit’s golden-hued noir, “Breaking the Girls,” takes inspiration from Hitchcock’s psychological thriller “Strangers on a Train” (itself based on Patricia Highsmith’s novel) and updates it for the texting, it’s-all-about-me generation. But this film goes further, turning the tale into an erotic thriller with too many twists and back stories to count.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/26/arts/26RDP_BREAKING_SPAN/26RDP_BREAKING_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/26/arts/26RDP_BREAKING_SPAN/26RDP_BREAKING_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'sub