one, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/2bcbd872-dabd-52b7-8d8b-00e2a31829d1', 'word_count': 851, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2bcbd872-dabd-52b7-8d8b-00e2a31829d1'}, {'abstract': 'The Penobscot River is expected to begin yielding river herring, Atlantic salmon and other fish in numbers not seen in centuries.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/26/us/hopes-for-a-fish-revival-as-a-dam-is-demolished.html', 'snippet': 'The Penobscot River is expected to begin yielding river herring, Atlantic salmon and other fish in numbers not seen in centuries.', 'lead_paragraph': 'EDDINGTON, Me. — There is a bend in the Penobscot River here, embanked by an Indian burial ground, through which millions of fish used to make a strenuous journey upstream to spawn before returning to the sea. There were elegant Atlantic salmon, prehistoric-looking sturgeon and, most numerous of all, lowly river herring, a nutrient-rich forage fish prized by ground fish, bears and birds.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/26/us/ALEWIVES-2/ALEWIVES-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 468, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/26/us/ALEWIVES-2/ALEWIVES-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 468}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/26/us/ALEWIVES-2/ALEWIVES-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 799, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/26/us/ALEWIVES-2/ALEWIVES-2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1013, 'width': 1298, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy'