e': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Corrections: July 26, 2013', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Corrections', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-07-26T02:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/eb1d609b-b738-5978-ba4a-a6b2c048021c', 'word_count': 412, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eb1d609b-b738-5978-ba4a-a6b2c048021c'}, {'abstract': '“Murder for Two,” a new two-performer musical produced by Second Stage Theater’s Uptown series, uses its actor-doubling to daffy effects.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/26/theater/reviews/in-the-musical-murder-for-two-one-man-is-many-suspects.html', 'snippet': '“Murder for Two,” a new two-performer musical produced by Second Stage Theater’s Uptown series, uses its actor-doubling to daffy effects.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In one sense there’s little mystery surrounding the question of who pulled the fatal trigger in the new musical “Murder for Two,” which opened on Thursday night at the McGinn/Cazale Theater as part of Second Stage Theater’s Uptown series. Only two actors are in the show, and one plays the police officer investigating the crime. So it doesn’t take a Hercule Poirot, a Miss Marple or a Jackson Brodie (my current fave, from Kate Atkinson’s wonderful books) to conclude that the culprit is definitely the lanky, bald fellow with popping eyes and pinwheeling limbs.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/26/arts/26MURDER_SPAN/26MURDER_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/26/arts/26MURDER_SP