rantland and the author of “Should Reddit Be Blamed for the Spreading of a Smear?” in this weekend’s magazine, will participate in an A.M.A. — “Ask Me Anything” — on Reddit.\xa0Kang will answer your questions about his article, which examines how, in the hours after the Boston Marathon bombings, a rumor initially posted on Reddit about the identity of Suspect No. 2 gained traction in social media and the news. (It turned out to not be true.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/26/magazine/26jaykang/26jaykang-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/26/magazine/26jaykang/26jaykang-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/26/magazine/26jaykang/26jaykang-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/26/magazine/26jaykang/26jaykang-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1200, 'width': 1600, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/26/magazine/26jaykang/26jaykang-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/26/magazine/26jaykang/26jaykang-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/26/magazine/26jaykang/26jaykang-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ask Jay Caspian Kang Anything … Today on Reddit', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Boston Marathon', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Reddit Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-26T15:34:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Staff', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Staff'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/72416e82-4fa6-5cb5-bc68-afcb064dc6e9', 'word_count': 115, 'uri': 'nyt://article/72416e82-4fa6-5cb5-bc68-afcb064dc6e9'}, {'abstract': 'In a letter to the Russian minister of justice, Attorney General Eric H. Holder Jr. said the United States was also willing to issue a passport to Edward J. Snowden so he could return to the country.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/27/world/europe/edward-snowden.html', 'snippet': 'In a letter to the Russian minister of justice, Attorney General Eric H. Holder Jr. said the United States was also willing to issue a passport to Edward J. Snowden so he could return to the country.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Attorney General Eric H. Holder Jr. said in a letter sent to the Russian minister of justice this week that the United States would not seek the death penalty against Edward J. Snowden, and would issue him a passport immediately so he could travel back to the United States.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/27/world/snowden/snowden-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 397, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/27/world/snowden/snowden-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 397}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/27/world/snowden/snowden-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 677, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/27/world/snowden/snowden-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1354, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'U.S. Letter Says Leaker Won’t Face Death Penalty', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'U.S. Letter Says Leaker Won’t Face Death Penalty', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schmidt, Michael S', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Snowden, Edward J', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fugitives', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Holder, Eric H Jr', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Surveillance of Citizens by Government', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Security Agency', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Asylum, Right of', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Capital Punishment', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Extradition', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classified Information and State Secrets', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-26T15:36:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael S. Schmidt and Steven Lee Myers', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Schmidt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': 'Lee', 'lastname': 'Myers', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bac70816-6e4d-518d-bcce-053afb882cfb', 'word_count': 469, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bac70816-6e4d-518d-bcce-053afb882cfb'}, {'abstract': 'Reza Aslan’s “Zealot,” No. 4 on the hardcover nonfiction list, investigates the historical Jesus and leaves the impression that Monty Python got a lot right in “Life of Brian.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/04/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Reza Aslan’s “Zealot,” No. 4 on the hardcover nonfiction list, investigates the historical Jesus and leaves the impression that Monty Python got a lot right in “Life of Brian.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'A.D.: Reading “Zealot,” Reza Aslan’s account of the historical Jesus and his political environment (new at No. 4 on the hardcover nonfiction list), it’s easy to conclude that Monty Python got a lot right in the 1979 movie “Life of Brian,” which likewise depicts an ancient Judea rife with messianic cult leaders and Jewish liberation groups intent on overthrowing their Roman occupiers.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28insidelist/28insidelist-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 454, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28insidelist/28insidelist-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 454, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28insidelist/28insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28insidelist/28insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28insidelist/28insidelist-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'TBR: Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-26T15:36:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/418ca646-4877-5505-99d4-3568bfe666ba', 'word_count': 633, 'uri': 'nyt://article/418ca646-4877-5505-99d4-3568bfe666ba'}, {'abstract': 'In “Rendezvous With Destiny,” Michael Fullilove tells the story of how America joined World War II by focusing on the missions of five men he identifies as Franklin Roosevelt’s “personal envoys.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/28/books/review/rendezvous-with-destiny-by-michael-fullilove.html', 'snippet': 'In “Rendezvous With Destiny,” Michael Fullilove tells the story of how America joined World War II by focusing on the missions of five men he identifies as Franklin Roosevelt’s “personal envoys.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Each time an American president sends soldiers, mercenaries, jets or drones to fight abroad, those of us whose historical knowledge or memories reach back to the last century recall when this nation entered into armed conflict the old-\xadfashioned way, by declaring war on an enemy. Americans appear to have an endless appetite for stories about World War II, perhaps because it was the last war “declared” by Congress and the only war of the 20th century that continues to be viewed sympathetically by a majority of every segment of the population.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28NASAW/28NASAW-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 702, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28NASAW/28NASAW-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 702}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28NASAW/28NASAW-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 876, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28NASAW/28NASAW-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1751, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28NASAW/28NASAW-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28NASAW/28NASAW-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28NASAW/28NASAW-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The President’s Men', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'War Torn', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fullilove, Michael', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roosevelt, Franklin Delano', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-26T15:38:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Nasaw', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nasaw', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3027d444-366d-5897-9c64-a1870450121a', 'word_count': 1185, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3027d444-366d-5897-9c64-a1870450121a'}, {'abstract': 'Sarah Butler’s novel alternates between a rootless woman and the father she’s never known.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/28/books/review/sarah-butlers-ten-things-ive-learnt-about-love.html', 'snippet': 'Sarah Butler’s novel alternates between a rootless woman and the father she’s never known.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Sarah Butler’s first novel, “Ten Things I’ve Learnt About Love,” lists of 10 are laid down like landing pads between chapters: “Ten things I will say to my father,” “Ten things in my father’s shed,” “Ten things I thought I’d do with my life,” “Ten places I’ve had sex.” It may sound like a gimmicky approach — after all, in the world BuzzFeed and Business Insider have wrought, the list has become inescapable, manhandling written communication, making style and linguistic grace seem somehow outdated and effete. Do we really need lists in novels, too? ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28RUSSO/28RUSSO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 555, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28RUSSO/28RUSSO-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 555}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28RUSSO/28RUSSO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 948, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28RUSSO/28RUSSO-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1895, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28RUSSO/28RUSSO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28RUSSO/28RUSSO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28RUSSO/28RUSSO-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Count Your Blessings', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Count Your Blessings', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Butler, Sarah', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-26T15:38:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maria Russo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maria', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Russo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2eab6ae1-cc2c-5d25-98d3-adbfc0f8ca5f', 'word_count': 829, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2eab6ae1-cc2c-5d25-98d3-adbfc0f8ca5f'}, {'abstract': 'An impenetrable author gathers his three sons for a tense reunion in David Gilbert’s novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/28/books/review/david-gilberts-sons.html', 'snippet': 'An impenetrable author gathers his three sons for a tense reunion in David Gilbert’s novel.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I’ve always been interested in how we travel through time,” David Gilbert says in a “conversation” included with the publicity materials for his new novel, “& Sons.” Given that his own authoritative father had once been painfully shy, Gilbert wondered what it would be like to meet one’s father in the midst of his awkward adolescence, and therein lay a promising if problematic premise for a novel. But then Gilbert gave this screw a rather broad turn: what if one’s father were a “really mythical” writer along the lines of Salinger, “vivid on the page yet impenetrable in the flesh?” If one assumes that resembling Salinger entails not only reclusiveness but also rotten parenting skills (in his case, it depends on which child you ask), then the result would be a personage like A. N. Dyer, the impenetrable writer-father at the center of Gilbert’s novel, who leaves at least one of his sons wondering how a man could be so “funny and smart” in his work, and yet such a creep in person.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28bailey/28bailey-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 638, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 