uson-and-eric-mccormack.html', 'snippet': 'The actors Eric McCormack and Jesse Tyler Ferguson are known for their groundbreaking TV roles, but both agree that a subtle approach to getting out a message works best.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jesse Tyler Ferguson arrived 10 minutes early for the 1 p.m. reservation at Il Cantinori in Greenwich Village, wearing sunglasses and a porkpie hat, which he quickly removed as he expressed thanks for being invited to lunch. Then, Eric McCormack texted from his car to apologize for running late. Except that he wasn’t: he arrived right on time.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/fashion/28TABLE_SPAN/28TABLE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/28/fashion/28TABLE_SPAN/28TABLE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/fashion/28TABLE_SPAN/28TABLE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/fashion/28TABLE_SPAN/28TABLE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/fashion/28TABLE_SPAN/28TABLE_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/28/fashion/28TABLE_SPAN/28TABLE_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/fashion/28TABLE_SPAN/28TABLE_SPAN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'At Lunch With Jesse Tyler Ferguson and Eric McCormack', 'kicker': 'Table for Three', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'At Lunch With Jesse Tyler Ferguson and Eric McCormack', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ferguson, Jesse Tyler', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCormack, Eric', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Modern Family (TV Program)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-26T15:41:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Philip Galanes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Philip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Galanes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/df34be76-95c2-517a-afc3-2a6186d7d50a', 'word_count': 3017, 'uri': 'nyt://article/df34be76-95c2-517a-afc3-2a6186d7d50a'}, {'abstract': 'Eduardo Galeano’s daybook of political and social struggle.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/28/books/review/children-of-the-days-by-eduardo-galeano.html', 'snippet': 'Eduardo Galeano’s daybook of political and social struggle.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One would think that Latin Americans, after all they’ve suffered, from the tortures and terrors of the Spanish Inquisition to the death squads and disappearances of the cold war, would have given up on the idea that history is redeemable. But it seems that centuries of repression and struggle have had the opposite effect, searing into their political culture a kind of irrepressible Hegelianism, an ability both to recognize the dialectic lurking behind the brutality and to answer every bloody body with ever more adamant affirmations of humanity.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28-GRANDIN/28-GRANDIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 720, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28-GRANDIN/28-GRANDIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 720}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28-GRANDIN/28-GRANDIN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 862, 'width': 718, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28-GRANDIN/28-GRANDIN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 862, 'width': 718, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28-GRANDIN/28-GRANDIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28-GRANDIN/28-GRANDIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28-GRANDIN/28-GRANDIN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'La Lucha Continúa', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'La Lucha Continúa', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grandin, Greg', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Galeano, Eduardo', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-26T15:42:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Greg Grandin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Greg', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grandin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/840a07d9-ed64-5cc7-92ad-dff69a3938cb', 'word_count': 619, 'uri': 'nyt://article/840a07d9-ed64-5cc7-92ad-dff69a3938cb'}, {'abstract': 'Howard Norman’s memoir explores anxiety and displacement.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/28/books/review/i-hate-to-leave-this-beautiful-place-by-howard-norman.html', 'snippet': 'Howard Norman’s memoir explores anxiety and displacement.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In his new memoir, “I Hate to Leave This Beautiful Place,” Howard Norman writes like a man serenely unconcerned with small matters like whether or not his readers actually keep reading. This is unexpectedly arresting. We’re accustomed to memoirists working double time to keep our attention, giving jumped-up accounts of personal disasters while at the same time relentlessly trying to burrow their way into our hearts with lovable, self-deprecating personas. Memoir is a crowded genre, and memoirists too often behave accordingly. Norman — author of the National Book Award finalists “The Northern Lights” and “The Bird Artist,” as well as several other novels — writes with zero desperation. He simply plows his field, and if you want to come watch, fine by him.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28-DEDERER/28-DEDERER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28-DEDERER/28-DEDERER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28-DEDERER/28-DEDERER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28-DEDERER/28-DEDERER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28-DEDERER/28-DEDERER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28-DEDERER/28-DEDERER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28-DEDERER/28-DEDERER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Threatening Weather', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Threatening Weather', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Norman, Howard', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-26T15:42:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Claire Dederer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Claire', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dederer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/12a2eb8b-e2fc-5bd6-bcd3-3be9e94e6284', 'word_count': 647, 'uri': 'nyt://article/12a2eb8b-e2fc-5bd6-bcd3-3be9e94e6284'}, {'abstract': 'Roxana Robinson’s novel examines war’s psychological wounds.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/28/books/review/sparta-by-roxana-robinson.html', 'snippet': 'Roxana Robinson’s novel examines war’s psychological wounds.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The returning military veteran is a firmly established character in American literature. Like most of the fiction that has preceded it, Roxana Robinson’s new novel, “Sparta,” places a young man in this role: Conrad, a Williams graduate who flies back home to the United States after four years in Iraq to face the weird vagaries of his homeland. At only 26, he sees American life with new eyes — or are they old eyes, those of a damaged warrior?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28carlson/0728-bks-RABB-1374070803078-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 266, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28carlson/0728-bks-RABB-1374070803078-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 266}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28carlson/0728-bks-RABB-1374070803078-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 455, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28carlson/0728-bks-RABB-1374070803078-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 1351, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28carlson/0728-bks-RABB-1374070803078-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28carlson/0728-bks-RABB-1374070803078-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28carlson/0728-bks-RABB-1374070803078-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Damaged Warrior', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Damaged Warrior', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Robinson, Roxana', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-26T15:42:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ron Carlson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ron', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carlson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b7b1fae5-53c1-5cc0-a4fc-b99c927f8913', 'word_count': 625, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b7b1fae5-53c1-5cc0-a4fc-b99c927f8913'}, {'abstract': 'A family’s secrets are unearthed during the British heat wave of 1976 in Maggie O’Farrell’s psychological drama.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/28/books/review/maggie-ofarrells-instructions-for-a-heatwave.html', 'snippet': 'A family’s secrets are unearthed during the British heat wave of 1976 in Maggie O’Farrell’s psychological drama.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In her previous novels, Maggie O’Farrell has often measured the distance between intimates, and the unexpected intimacy of distance — geographic, temporal, cultural. In “The Hand That First Held Mine” and “The Distance Between Us,” characters separated by many miles or many years turn out to be joined in ways they never anticipated. In other books, like “The Vanishing Act of Esme Lennox” and “After You’d Gone,” women confined to hospital wards slowly reveal secrets that both bind and unbind their families. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28-SUB-DERASMO/28-SUB-DERASMO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 536, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28-SUB-DERASMO/28-SUB-DERASMO-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 536}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28-SUB-DERASMO/28-SUB-DERASMO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 915, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28-SUB-DERASMO/28-SUB-DERASMO-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 993, 'width': 1111, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28-SUB-DERASMO/28-SUB-DERASMO-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28-SUB-DERASMO/28-SUB-DERASMO-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28-SUB-DERASMO/28-SUB-DERASMO-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bubbling to the Surface', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bubbling to the Surface', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': "O'Farrell, Maggie", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-26T15:43:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': "By Stacey D'Erasmo", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stacey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "D'Erasmo", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/03b1ceec-6676-586d-af7d-4caa14584c80', 'word_count': 1299, 'uri': 'nyt://article/03b1ceec-6676-586d-af7d-4caa14584c80'}, {'abstract': 'Joan Silber’s characters come into hard-won wisdom.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/28/books/review/fools-by-joan-silber.html', 'snippet': 'Joan Silber’s characters come into hard-won wisdom.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A few years ago, in a craft book called “The Art of Time in Fiction,” Joan Silber noted: “I’m interested in how fates roll out over many years and am drawn to write fiction that takes on the task of compressing whole lifetimes into short stories or chapters.” I’ve always thought of time as creating a natural tension in fiction, but Silber posits that it actually creates content. “Time draws the shapes of stories,” she argues. “A story is entirely determined by what portion of time it chooses to narrate.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28BACKOPOULOS/28BACKOPOULOS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1200, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28BACKOPOULOS/28BACKOPOULOS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1200}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28BACKOPOULOS/28BACKOPOULOS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 512, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28BACKOPOULOS/28BACKOPOULOS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28BACKOPOULOS/28BACKOPOULOS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28BACKOPOULOS/28BACKOPOULOS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28BACKOPOULOS/28BACKOPOULOS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Suffer Gladly', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Suffer Gladly', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Silber, Joan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-26T15:44:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Natalie Bakopoulos', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Natalie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bakopoulos', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1922feb0-7af2-52cf-8faa-8379746aa792', 'word_count': 578, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1922feb0-7af2-52cf-8faa-8379746aa792'}, {'abstract': 'A novel of Chinese workers in 19th-century Massachusetts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/28/books/review/karen-shepards-celestials.html', 'snippet': 'A novel of Chinese workers in 19th-century Massachusetts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On a June morning in 1870, 75 \xadCelestials came to earth in North Adams, Mass. They were Chinese men — 19th-century Americans knew China as “the Celestial Empire” — mostly teenagers, and they had just traveled for two weeks by train to New England from San Francisco to work as strikebreakers in Calvin T. Sampson’s shoe factory. The infuriated strikers, members of the Order of the Knights of St. Crispin, massed at the railroad station armed with clubs and rifles. Reporters from Boston looked on warily. Hundreds of agitated townspeople spilled over into the adjoining streets, a dangerous crowd.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28BYRD/28BYRD-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 960, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/28/books/review/28BYRD/28BYRD-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 960}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/books/revi