on': [{'firstname': 'J.', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/a76fd9a3-dd19-5b87-bebb-401636576d67', 'word_count': 457, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a76fd9a3-dd19-5b87-bebb-401636576d67'}, {'abstract': 'A weekly capsule of events around the globe curated by our writers and editors.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/26/calendar-coming-events-in-new-orleans-wisconsin-goteborg-sweden/', 'snippet': 'A weekly capsule of events around the globe curated by our writers and editors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Satchmo SummerFest, New Orleans A three-day jazz festival in New Orleans, dedicated to the life, legacy and music of the city’s native son, Louis Satchmo Armstrong, is scheduled to take place Aug. 2 to 4 in the French Quarter at the Louisiana State Museum’s Old Mint. Satchmo SummerFest will feature performances by traditional and contemporary jazz and brass band musicians, with a lineup that includes Allen Toussaint, Ellis Marsalis, Wycliffe Gordon and the Preservation Hall Brass Band. Festivalgoers can also attend movies, exhibits, family activities, seminars, dance lessons and special events like the Jazz Mass at the St. Augustine Church in Treme, a traditional second line parade and the annual Trumpet Tribute, which closes the festival Sunday night. Events are free and open to the public, with the exception of the Aug. 1 opening reception.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Calendar: Coming Events in New Orleans; Wisconsin; Goteborg, Sweden', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Gothenburg (Sweden)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Orleans (La)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Wisconsin', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-26T17:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d4f863cd-4bc3-5b82-901a-796a3a9ba70d', 'word_count': 285, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d4f863cd-4bc3-5b82-901a-796a3a9ba70d'}, {'abstract': 'A new show by Daniel Knorr at Kayne Griffin Corcoran gallery in Los Angeles elevates the banal byproducts of human existence into beautiful objects.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/26/on-view-potholes-as-art-garbage-as-great-books/', 'snippet': 'A new show by Daniel Knorr at Kayne Griffin Corcoran gallery in Los Angeles elevates the banal byproducts of human existence into beautiful objects.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When most people think of Los Angeles, they visualize the Hollywood sign, palm trees, the beach. The conceptual artist Daniel Knorr sees potholes. For his first Los Angeles exhibit, “Depression Elevations,” at Kayne Griffin Corcoran, the Bucharest-born, Berlin-based artist took to the streets, scouring the L.A. landscape in a van for aesthetically appealing divots and puddles to cast with polyurethane. “We gave him a van, vests and flares, and he went all over town,” the gallerist Maggie Kayne said. The resulting wall pieces — Knorr describes them as “materializations” rather than sculptures, to denote the shift from idea to object — are hung smooth side out, creating the impression of fluorescent relief maps, as translucent as gummy bears.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Potholes as Art, Garbage as Great Books', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'On View: Potholes as Art', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-07-26T17:00:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maxwell Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maxwell', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e7472702-497d-5ad7-a315-168d2159ae5c', 'word_count': 294, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e7472702-497d-5ad7-a315-168d2159ae5c'}, {'abstract': 'The personality trait that distinguishes smokers from nonsmokers: poor self-control.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/28/opinion/sunday/why-smokers-still-smoke.html', 'snippet': 'The personality trait that distinguishes smokers from nonsmokers: poor self-control.', 'lead_paragraph': 'GIVEN how hazardous it is to their health, why do smokers continue to smoke?', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/sunday-review/28GRAYMATTER/28GRAYMATTER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 686, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/28/sunday-review/28GRAYMATTER/28GRAYMATTER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 686}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/sunday-review/28GRAYMATTER/28GRAYMATTER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 896, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/sunday-review/28GRAYMATTER/28GRAYMATTER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1792, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type':