pe_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1da46e2a-03af-5a0d-88f0-c8f576d54747', 'word_count': 2384, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1da46e2a-03af-5a0d-88f0-c8f576d54747'}, {'abstract': 'How two American businessmen came to undertake a secret mission against Confederate ships under construction in Britain.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/26/forbes-and-aspinwall-go-to-war/', 'snippet': 'How two American businessmen came to undertake a secret mission against Confederate ships under construction in Britain.', 'lead_paragraph': 'John Murray Forbes was feeling “half ill” in his Milton, Mass., home on Saturday March 14, 1863, when he received a brief telegram from Treasury Secretary Salmon P. Chase. The secretary wanted to meet with him the next day at the Fifth Avenue Hotel in Manhattan. Forbes, a man who once served tea to John Brown, “could not refuse.” Shaking off his sickness, Forbes hurried overnight to New York. The next day, he sat down with Chase, like him an ardent abolitionist. This was no ordinary meeting: also attending were Navy Secretary Gideon Welles and the New York businessman William H. Aspinwall, the owner of the lucrative gold-hauling Pacific Mail Steamship Company. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Forbes and Aspinwall Go to War', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Espionage and Intelligence Services', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ships and Shipping', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ADAMS, CHARLES FRANCIS', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': '