al': 'By Sam Roberts', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Roberts', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/752fe1f5-6c4f-56e2-af5b-350448ac2e79', 'word_count': 526, 'uri': 'nyt://article/752fe1f5-6c4f-56e2-af5b-350448ac2e79'}, {'abstract': '“Intolerance,” D. W. Griffith’s 1916 follow-up to “The Birth of a Nation,” is having a weeklong run at Film Forum in a newly restored version.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/28/movies/film-forum-shows-d-w-griffiths-vast-intolerance.html', 'snippet': '“Intolerance,” D. W. Griffith’s 1916 follow-up to “The Birth of a Nation,” is having a weeklong run at Film Forum in a newly restored version.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Both admired and reviled, D. W. Griffith’s 1915 feature “The Birth of a Nation” still occupies an unusual position in American cinema. When it was released, its unnervingly compelling account of star-crossed Civil War families and the Ku Klux Klan was the subject of protests and censorship battles. After all that, you might expect a filmmaker to be taken down a notch. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/arts/28INTOLERANCE1_SPAN/28INTOLERANCE1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/28/arts/28INTOLERANCE1_SPAN/28INTOLERANCE1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/28/arts/28INTOLERANCE1_SPAN/28INTOLERANCE1_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit':