'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Langree, Louis', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-26T20:05:31+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Arts', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/3f0edde4-4c65-5372-a7a5-f7e88a2f5869', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/3f0edde4-4c65-5372-a7a5-f7e88a2f5869'}, {'abstract': 'Beyoncé troupes on with a tour she calls the “Mrs. Carter Show” in New Jersey, New York and Connecticut.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/28/arts/music/mrs-carter-on-her-tour.html', 'snippet': 'Beyoncé troupes on with a tour she calls the “Mrs. Carter Show” in New Jersey, New York and Connecticut.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Who wants that perfect love story, anyway?” Beyoncé sings on “Part II (On the Run),” a track on her husband’s new album. He, of course, is Shawn Carter, a k a Jay Z, and the album is “Magna Carta ... Holy Grail.” In case her reservations about storybook romance weren’t clear, she adds a disdainfully silky refrain: “Cliché, cliché, cliché, cliché.”', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mrs. Carter on Her Tour', 'kicker': 'Pop', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mrs. Carter On Her Tour', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Knowles, Beyonce', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-26T20:07:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Chinen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chinen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3e22acc4-8412-53b5-83dc-1289513535cf', 'word_count': 154, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3e22acc4-8412-53b5-83dc-1289513535cf'}, {'abstract': 'Although it has generated less hoopla than the Verdi and Wagner bicentennials, the English composer Benjamin Britten’s 100th birthday is also cause for celebration.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/28/arts/music/britten-serenade-in-the-park.html', 'snippet': 'Although it has generated less hoopla than the Verdi and Wagner bicentennials, the English composer Benjamin Britten’s 100th birthday is also cause for celebration.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Although it has generated less hoopla than the Verdi and Wagner bicentennials this season, the English composer Benjamin Britten’s 100th birthday is also cause for celebration in 2013. Major events loom as Britten’s actual birth date (Nov. 22) draws near. But on Tuesday, the Knights, a terrific independent orchestra, get a jump on the festivities with their second appearance in the free Naumburg Orchestral Concerts series in Central Park. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Britten Serenade in the Park', 'kicker': 'Classical', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Britten Serenade In the Park', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Britten, Benjamin', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-26T20:08:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/76d0f300-1cbb-5d6d-9a7d-2f6945ef5c4e', 'word_count': 170, 'uri': 'nyt://article/76d0f300-1cbb-5d6d-9a7d-2f6945ef5c4e'}, {'abstract': 'The enterprising Drilling Company ends its free series Shakespeare in the Parking Lot with “Richard III.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/28/theater/the-summer-of-their-lucky-lot.html', 'snippet': 'The enterprising Drilling Company ends its free series Shakespeare in the Parking Lot with “Richard III.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'William Shakespeare, whose characters ignore signs and portents at their peril, would surely have appreciated the mystical convergences involved in performing his “Richard III” in a parking lot. Bones believed to belong to the real Richard III were exhumed last fall from beneath a municipal parking lot in Leicester, England. Now that discovery is being most happily capitalized upon by the enterprising Drilling Company in its free series Shakespeare in the Parking Lot, which this summer ends with the story of Old Crookback. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Summer of Their Lucky Lot', 'kicker': 'Theater', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Summer Of Their Lucky Lot', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Drilling Co', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-26T20:09:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Brantley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brantley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d433eec-f227-5dbe-a730-522104265aef', 'word_count': 185, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d433eec-f227-5dbe-a730-522104265aef'}, {'abstract': 'A lawyer for Pfc. Bradley Manning tried to counter the prosecution’s portrayal in his court-martial over leaking government documents, calling him a “humanist” and a “whistle-blower.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/27/us/defense-calls-manning-naive-but-good-intentioned.html', 'snippet': 'A lawyer for Pfc. Bradley Manning tried to counter the prosecution’s portrayal in his court-martial over leaking government documents, calling him a “humanist” and a “whistle-blower.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'FORT MEADE, Md. — A defense lawyer for Pfc. Bradley Manning on Friday portrayed his client as “young, naïve, but good-intentioned” when he sent databases of secret documents about American military and diplomatic activities to WikiLeaks, and he urged the judge in his court-martial to be lenient when she decides his fate.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/27/us/MANNING/MANNING-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 482, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/27/us/MANNING/MANNING-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 482}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/27/us/MANNING/MANNING-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 822, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/27/us/MANNING/MANNING-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1580, 'width': 1968, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/27/us/MANNING/MANNING-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/27/us/MANNING/MANNING-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight'