+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jacob Bernstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jacob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bernstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f9d99361-9604-5c60-9f2b-d7ee7040b76d', 'word_count': 2095, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f9d99361-9604-5c60-9f2b-d7ee7040b76d'}, {'abstract': 'Rescued from cancellation, “The Killing,” in its third season, overflows with suspects, bloodshed and dead ends.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/28/arts/television/the-deadline-of-the-killing.html', 'snippet': 'Rescued from cancellation, “The Killing,” in its third season, overflows with suspects, bloodshed and dead ends.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When “The Killing” set off on its somber course in April 2011, viewers didn’t expect to wade through two seasons of Seattle murk to answer “Who killed Rosie Larsen?” Soon critical acclaim and a loyal fan base dissolved into outrage and dwindling viewership, as the first season ended with a cliffhanger not resolved until the final moments of the second. That’s when “The Killing” was pronounced dead. But the patient still had a pulse.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Deadline of ‘The Killing’', 'kicker': 'Television', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Deadline Of ‘The Killing’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-26T20:11:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/29b5784b-1dc8-596f-981b-6ba5353c548c', 'word_count': 182, 'uri': 'nyt://article/29b5784b-1dc8-596f-981b-6ba5353c548c'}, {'abstract': 'This week, however, you can simply go to the Berkshires to see the Paris Opera Ballet.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/28/arts/dance/a-rare-sighting-of-paris-talent.html', 'snippet': 'This week, however, you can simply go to the Berkshires to see the Paris Opera Ballet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Paris Opera Ballet doesn’t travel much, and its dancers tend not to be a presence on the international guest-star circuit. If you want to see how the company is dancing, and who might be up and coming, you generally have to go to Paris. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Rare Sighting of Paris Talent', 'kicker': 'Dance', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Rare Sighting Of Paris Talent', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-26T20:12:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roslyn Sulcas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roslyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sulcas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/30f06995-3bc9-5c34-9920-b961bf8778ef', 'word_count': 211, 'uri': 'nyt://article/30f06995-3bc9-5c34-9920-b961bf8778ef'}, {'abstract': 'Like the Beatles, the Dave Clark Five experimented with filmmaking, including the 1965 John Boorman film “Catch Us if You Can.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/28/movies/five-reasons-to-go-mod.html', 'snippet': 'Like the Beatles, the Dave Clark Five experimented with filmmaking, including the 1965 John Boorman film “Catch Us if You Can.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The British band the Dave Clark Five was among the most successful pop groups of the 1960s, combining catchy tunes with a propulsive beat. Its hits included “Glad All Over,” “Do You Love Me” and “I Like It Like That,” and the quintet turned up on “The Ed Sullivan Show” so often it seemed like the house band. “Din Overpowers Dave Clark Five,” a May 1964 headline in The New York Times ran atop an article about the band’s appearance at Carnegie Hall. “Rivals of the Beatles Hailed by Screaming Girls.” ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Five Reasons to Go Mod', 'kicker': 'Film', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Five Reasons To Go Mod', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dave Clark Five, The', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-26T20:13:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/367127ae-960c-5121-87b1-7c20dfbafa55', 'word_count': 202, 'uri': 'nyt://article/367127ae-960c-5121-87b1-7c20dfbafa55'}, {'abstract': 'Fund indicted; called magnet for cheating. | Wall Street’s exposure to hacking laid bare. | Trader and S.E.C. lawyer spar over e-mail. | A legal bane of Wall Street switches sides.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/07/26/week-in-review-a-crippling-blow-follows-outsize-profits/', 'snippet': 'Fund indicted; called magnet for cheating. | Wall Street’s exposure to hacking laid bare. | Trader and S.E.C. 