ad_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Three times in the last 35 years, a president has quietly picked a new chairman for the Federal Reserve and then quickly obtained the Senate’s confirmation. The choice of the nation’s most powerful technocrat has far-reaching consequences, but it has rarely generated much public anticipation or political debate. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/27/business/27FED_395/27FED_395-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 263, 'width': 395, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/27/business/27FED_395/27FED_395-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 263, 'width': 395, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Quiet Rivalry Over the Next Fed Leader Comes Out of the Shadows', 'kicker': 'News Analysis', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Quiet Rivalry Over the Next Fed Leader Comes Out of the Shadows', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Appelbaum, Binyamin', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Reserve System', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Yellen, Janet L', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Summers, Lawrence H', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bernanke, Ben S', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government',