: '2013-07-29T18:36:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Preston', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Preston', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e79bbf13-4ec0-5b95-9c21-1cffceaa18a5', 'word_count': 342, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e79bbf13-4ec0-5b95-9c21-1cffceaa18a5'}, {'abstract': 'The government’s failure to cleanly deal with Fannie and Freddie’s stock and bondholders during the financial crisis is coming back to haunt it, the authors write.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/07/29/hedge-funds-suit-on-fannie-and-freddie-may-spell-trouble-for-u-s/', 'snippet': 'The government’s failure to cleanly deal with Fannie and Freddie’s stock and bondholders during the financial crisis is coming back to haunt it, the authors write.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The lawsuit brought by the hedge fund Perry Capital against the federal government over the Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac bailout may be the case that finally subjects the government’s bailout practices to closer outside scrutiny. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hedge Fund’s Suit on Fannie and Freddie May Spell Trouble for U.S.', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Subprime Mortgage Crisis', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation (Civil)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Home Loan Mortgage Corp (Freddie Mac)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal National Mortgage Assn (Fannie Mae)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Perry Capital LLC', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-29T18:46:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Davidoff Solomon and David Zaring', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': 'Davidoff', 'lastname': 'Solomon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zaring', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cae710cf-4f42-5eb9-97eb-691d080be0cf', 'word_count': 1282, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cae710cf-4f42-5eb9-97eb-691d080be0cf'}, {'abstract': 'Stories about young real estate investors may be persuasive, the author writes, but deciding if similar actions would be good for us is crucial.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/29/your-money/some-investing-stories-sound-good-until-you-analyze-them.html', 'snippet': 'Stories about young real estate investors may be persuasive, the author writes, but deciding if similar actions would be good for us is crucial.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Making smart money decisions is an incredibly difficult way to behave. So after my post last week about buying a house based on your situation, my jaw dropped when I saw an article in The Wall Street Journal about young home buyers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/29/business/072913bucks-carl-sketch/072913bucks-carl-sketch-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/29/business/072913bucks-carl-sketch/072913bucks-carl-sketch-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/29/business/072913bucks-carl-sketch/072913bucks-carl