07/30/science/when-clean-was-a-new-concept-in-the-operating-room.html', 'snippet': 'The words “surgery” and “sterile” were not always bedfellows. A short history of advances in the hospital.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Today, keeping things clean as a way to ward off germs and infections just makes sense. But before antibiotic-resistant superbugs became a hospital’s biggest concern, there were the bad old days when doctors would move from surgery to surgery without washing. And surprisingly it was only during the 20th century that sterilization evolved from a simple and very new concept into one of the most important life-saving practices in medicine.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When Clean Was a New Concept in the Operating Room', 'kicker': 'Science of Its Time', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hospitals', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hygiene and Cleanliness', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Surgery and Surgeons', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bacteria', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poliomyelitis', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANTIBIOTICS', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-29T19:49:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katie Hiler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hiler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/67350910-e622-52d2-9a8c-fba46b3a9170', 'word_count': 540, 'uri': 'nyt://article/67350910-e622-52d2-9a8c-fba46b3a9170'}, {'abstract': 'The Times’s Ian Fisher, a former Rome bureau chief, on the pope’s statement on gay priests and how it differentiates Fr