e/ffe723d3-0b31-5bc4-9a64-30f5747b47b7'}, {'abstract': 'Two teams ended up with opposing conclusions in trying to solve the puzzle of why some mammals choose a single mate.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/30/science/despite-two-new-studies-on-motives-for-monogamy-the-debate-continues.html', 'snippet': 'Two teams ended up with opposing conclusions in trying to solve the puzzle of why some mammals choose a single mate.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The golden lion tamarin, a one-pound primate that lives in Brazil, is a stunningly monogamous creature. A male will typically pair with a female and they will stay close for the rest of their lives, mating only with each other and then working together to care for their young.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Despite Two New Studies on Motives for Monogamy, the Debate Continues', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Despite Two New Studies on Motives for Monogamy, the Debate Continues', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Evolution (Biology)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mammals', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Science (Journal)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reproduction (Biological)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-29T23:47:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carl Zimmer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zimmer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea8ecbf2-f866-50a0-9c24-002e997e3190', 'word_count': 634, 'uri': 'nyt://ar