, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Duke University', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-30T04:11:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pam Belluck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belluck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5770a114-474d-5312-91f3-0523f0105612', 'word_count': 1221, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5770a114-474d-5312-91f3-0523f0105612'}, {'abstract': 'Two people who were on an elevated track in Hollis were struck by a westbound train, the Metropolitan Transportation Authority said.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/30/nyregion/lirr-train-hits-two-in-queens-killing-them.html', 'snippet': 'Two people who were on an elevated track in Hollis were struck by a westbound train, the Metropolitan Transportation Authority said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two people were stuck and killed by a Long Island Rail Road train in Queens on Monday evening, the authorities said. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'L.I.R.R. Train Hits Two in Queens, Killing Them', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'L.I.R.R. Train Hits Two in Queens, Killing Them', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Railroad Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Long Island Rail Road Co', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Transportation Authority', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Jamaica (Queens, NY)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-30T04:40:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Schwirtz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schwirtz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2d27be6c-b40e-59f2-a5cd-a35e2e615fd2', 'word_count': 118, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2d27be6c-b40e-59f2-a5cd-a35e2e615fd2'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/30/arts/television/whats-on-tuesday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '10 P.M. (13, 49) LIFE AND DEATH IN ASSISTED LIVING Nearly 750,000 people reside in assisted-living facilities across the country, with for-profit chains like Emeritus Senior Living — the nation’s largest, with 37,000 residents in 400 compounds — dominating the industry. But standards for care and training vary by state, and, unlike nursing homes, these institutions are not regulated by the federal government. “Frontline” and ProPublica investigate this multibillion-dollar industry, examining alarming incidents involving residents: a Texan who froze to death on Christmas morning when she wandered outdoors, and, in another place, the Hall of Fame football player George McAfee (above, in a photograph held by his daughter Cheryl Morgan), who mistakenly drank dishwashing liquid and died.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On Tuesday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Tuesday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-07-30T05:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/47796