 [{'firstname': 'Jared', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bernstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1a89fc3d-a0d6-5131-b389-d8a09af4eefc', 'word_count': 711, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1a89fc3d-a0d6-5131-b389-d8a09af4eefc'}, {'abstract': 'This wine should leave you refreshed while matching the sweetness of the dish’s fruit, spices and caramelized onions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/31/dining/try-a-chenin-blanc-with-the-chicken-from-jerusalem.html', 'snippet': 'This wine should leave you refreshed while matching the sweetness of the dish’s fruit, spices and caramelized onions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Chenin blanc wines are among the most glorious and the most underappreciated. I think serving a good demi-sec Vouvray with this chicken dish will offer immediate insight into their deliciousness. Demi-sec indicates that the wine has some residual sugar, but don’t fear a wine that’s not dry. Chenin blanc, like riesling, has such lively acidity that it will balance out the moderate sweetness, leaving you refreshed while matching the sweetness of the fruit, spices and caramelized onions. What’s more, chenin blanc has a beautifully rich texture that not only feels great in the mouth but will also enhance the amplitude of the basmati rice and chicken. A young demi-sec is fine, an older one even better.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For Chicken With Onions and Cardamom Rice, Try a Chenin Blanc', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'And to Drink...', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Asimov, Eric', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wines', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-30T18:10:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Asimov', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Asimov', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/172ee3f0-9d2b-5d6b-9112-cfb55b84c598', 'word_count': 157, 'uri': 'nyt://article/172ee3f0-9d2b-5d6b-9112-cfb55b84c598'}, {'abstract': 'What are the best ways to see the City of Light with children? Here’s a guide to everything from museums to parks to food.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/04/travel/travel-guide-paris-for-kids.html', 'snippet': 'What are the best ways to see the City of Light with children? Here’s a guide to everything from museums to parks to food.', 'lead_paragraph': 'First, the bad news: Since Paris is one of the world’s most visited cities, seeing its best-known monuments and museums can require wading through masses of people and waiting in long lines. If you’re traveling with children, you will probably be exhausted before you even get inside.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/travel/04KIDS/04KIDS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 493, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/04/travel/04KIDS/04KIDS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 493}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/travel/04KIDS/04KIDS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 842, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/travel/04KIDS/04KIDS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1683, 'wi