:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By David W. Chen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Chen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/493dd325-a17f-5d3a-8641-7ec2b455c22e', 'word_count': 378, 'uri': 'nyt://article/493dd325-a17f-5d3a-8641-7ec2b455c22e'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/31/arts/television/whats-on-wednesday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '8 P.M. (VH1) THE 2013 DO SOMETHING AWARDS Sophia Bush, below, hosts this ceremony honoring “do-gooders” 25 and under, including five who will compete for $100,000. The finalists, from whom viewers will choose the winner, are Sasha Fisher, a founder of Spark MicroGrants, which provides money to communities in Rwanda and Uganda to build schools, health centers and wells; Daniel Maree, whose Million Hoodies Movement for Justice helped bring the Trayvon Martin case to national attention; Jillian Mourning, whose organization, All We Want Is L.O.V.E. (Liberation of Victims Everywhere), was created to educate the public about sex trafficking in the United States; Lorella Praeli, who advocates for the fair treatment of immigrant children and their families through United We Dream; and Ben Simon, whose Food Recovery Network uses student volunteers at the University of Maryland to collect leftover food from dining halls and donate it to soup kitchens and shelters. The competitors who don’t win the top prize will receive $10,000 community grants. Patrick Dempsey, Jesse Tyler Ferguson, Jennifer Hudson, L L Cool J and Kelly Osbourne will be honored for their own philanthropic work.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On Wednesday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Wednesday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-07-31T05:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/da953aaa-936e-59e9-8d8a-de5f2df7b570', 'word_count': 737, 'uri': 'nyt://article/da953aaa-936e-59e9-8d8a-de5f2df7b570'}, {'abstract': 'See what you know about the news of the day.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/31/news-quiz-july-31-2013/', 'snippet': 'See what you know about the news of the day.', 'lead_paragraph': 'See what you know about the news below. To prepare, you might scan the articles or summaries on today’s paper. Good luck!', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'News Quiz | July 31, 2013', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-07-31T05:30:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anastasia Economides', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anastasia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Economides', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6a3d6395-f2c7-5ada-9dfe-143154cee58d', 'word_count': 22, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6a3d6395-f2c7-5ada-9dfe-143154cee58d'}, {'abstract': 'The 23 percent rise in civilian casualties signals the challenge Afghan troops face as they take over the combat role from American forces.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/