ne, 'lastname': 'Ewing', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d8c36c84-52fb-5e8a-aee4-9d7dab5a4da9', 'word_count': 988, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d8c36c84-52fb-5e8a-aee4-9d7dab5a4da9'}, {'abstract': 'My daughter is kind and compassionate, but she will not smile unless either she is genuinely glad to see you or you’re telling her a joke that has a scatological punch line.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/31/i-do-not-want-my-daughter-to-be-nice/', 'snippet': 'My daughter is kind and compassionate, but she will not smile unless either she is genuinely glad to see you or you’re telling her a joke that has a scatological punch line.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My 10-year-old daughter, Birdy, is not nice, not exactly. She is deeply kind, profoundly compassionate and, probably, the most ethical person I know — but she will not smile at you unless either she is genuinely glad to see you or you’re telling her a joke that has something scatological for a punch line. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'I Do Not Want My Daughter to Be ‘Nice’', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Customs, Etiquette and Manners', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gender', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Toys', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-31T13:31:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Newman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7cb48157-ba00-53ae-b6be-b96cec74dff7', 'word_count': 976, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7cb48157-ba00-53ae-b6be-b96cec74dff7'}, {'abstract': 'In what would be a key breakthrough for renewable fuels, INEOS Bio said it had produced commercial quantities of ethanol from wood waste and other nonfood vegetative matter.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/01/business/energy-environment/company-says-its-the-first-to-make-ethanol-from-waste.html', 'snippet': 'In what would be a key breakthrough for renewable fuels, INEOS Bio said it had produced commercial quantities of ethanol from wood waste and other nonfood vegetative matter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — After months of frustrating delays, a chemical company announced Wednesday that it had produced commercial quantities of ethanol from wood waste and other nonfood vegetative matter, a long-sought goal that, if it can be expanded economically, has major implications for providing vehicle fuel and limiting greenhouse gas emissions.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Milestone Claimed in Creating Fuel From Waste', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethanol', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Alternative and Renewable Energy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ineos AG', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bioenergy and Biofuels', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Methane', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-31T14:05:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Energy & Environment ', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew L. Wald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Wald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/85a7f1ec-d4a8-5f34-b87e-8119728a765c', 'word_count': 595, 'uri': 'nyt://article/85a7f1ec-d4a8-5f34-b87e-8119728a765c'}, {'abstract': 'Looking for a new way to connect people with the bells he sells, Jeffrey Crook, president of Chime Master, decided a tractor-trailered carillon was just the thing his business needed.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/31/bell-peddlers-mobile-carillon-could-be-one-of-the-heaviest-marketing-tools-ever/', 'snippet': 'Looking for a new way to connect people with the bells he sells, Jeffrey Crook, president of Chime Master, decided a tractor-trailered carillon was just the thing his business needed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'STAMFORD, Conn. — Don’t honk your horn at Jeffrey Crook or the unusual cargo stowed atop the 21-foot trailer he’s towing behind his Mack truck. The feeble sound your vehicle emits is no competition for what he has on board.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/31/automobiles/wheels/bells-1/bells-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/31/automobiles/wheels/bells-1/bells-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/31/automobiles/wheels/bells-1/bells-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 577, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/31/automobiles/wheels/bells-1/bells-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1153, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/31/automobiles/wheels/bells-1/bells-1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/31/automobiles/wheels/bells-1/bells-1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/31/automobiles/wheels/bells-1/bells-1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bell Peddler’s Mobile Carillon Could Be One of the Heaviest Marketing Tools Ever', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ohio', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Stamford (Conn)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-31T14:09:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christine Negroni', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Negroni', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7e8b363c-b3c7-5373-b575-a27d631ba9da', 'word_count': 517, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7e8b363c-b3c7-5373-b575-a27d631ba9da'}, {'abstract': 'Home is at the heart of two new picture books, one set in a city that could be New York and the other in a wintry rural landscape.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/31/books/this-is-our-house-and-once-upon-a-northern-night.html', 'snippet': 'Home is at the heart of two new picture books, one set in a city that could be New York and the other in a wintry rural landscape.', 'lead_paragraph': '“What would it be like to stay in one place — to have your own bed, to ride your own bicycle?” a little girl named Anna wonders in Maxine Trottier’s 2011 picture book, “Migrant.” “Now that would be something.” Anna’s parents, who are migrant workers, move from one temporary home to another, and Anna imagines herself as a rabbit, living in abandoned burrows, or a bee, flitting from flower to flower. She is effectively homeless, and longs to live a settled life, “like a tree with roots sunk deeply into the earth.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/31/books/31childrens-1/31childrens-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/31/books/31childrens-1/31childrens-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/31/books/31childrens-1/31childrens-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/31/books/31childrens-1/31childrens-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/31/books/31childrens-1/31childrens-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/31/books/31childrens-1/31childrens-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/31/books/31childrens-1/31childrens-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Sense of Place', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Yum, Hyewon', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Arsenault, Isabelle', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pendziwol, Jean E', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-31T14:15:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Harrison Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': 'Harrison', 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d05900ab-f008-528c-b1d8-ca2996659a01', 'word_count': 757, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d05900ab-f008-528c-b1d8-ca2996659a01'}, {'abstract': 'By clearly posting their prices, The Surgery Center of Oklahoma provides transparency for patients paying their own bills.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/31/a-new-health-care-approach-dont-hide-the-price/', 'snippet': 'By clearly posting their prices, The Surgery Center of Oklahoma provides transparency for patients paying their own bills.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Surgery Center of Oklahoma is an ambulatory surgical center in Oklahoma City owned by its roughly 40 surgeons and anesthesiologists. What makes it different from every other such facility in America is this: If you need an anterior cruciate ligament reconstruction, you will know beforehand — because it’s on their Web site — that it costs $6,990 if you self-pay in advance. If you need a tonsillectomy, that’s $3,600. Repair of a simple closed nasal fracture: $1,900. These prices are all-inclusive.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Revealing a Health Care Secret: The Price', 'kicker': 'Fixes', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hospitals', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Surgery and Surgeons', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Oklahoma City (Okla)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-31T14:20:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tina Rosenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/00d2f18d-aa37-5568-b86b-eb4a357559a0', 'word_count': 1702, 'uri': 'nyt://article/00d2f18d-aa37-5568-b86b-eb4a357559a0'}, {'abstract': 'In a 30-second spot, John A. Catsimatidis says he is the most serious candidate about New York’s security.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/31/the-ad-campaign-catsimatidis-takes-aim-at-quinn-on-issue-of-public-safety/', 'snippet': 'In a 30-second spot, John A. Catsimatidis says he is the most serious candidate about New York’s security.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Law and order has been a prevailing theme for John A. Catsimatidis, the billionaire Republican candidate who has repeatedly pledged to keep “hoodlums” off the streets. In a 30-second spot, “Safe,” Mr. Catsimatidis warns that one rival in particular, Christine C. Quinn, the City Council speaker, could fall short in this arena. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Ad Campaign: Catsimatidis Calls Quinn ‘Out of Touch’ on Public Safety', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections, Mayors', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Advertising', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-31T14:27:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael M. Grynbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dc2663d2-5573-58f4-93ec-ff9f5fb1eedb', 'word_count': 398, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dc2663d2-5573-58f4-93ec-ff9f5fb1eedb'}, {'abstract': 'Senators of both parties challenged the collection of records of domestic phone calls, as the latest leak provided new details on the way the agency monitors Web browsing around the world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/01/us/nsa-surveillance.html', 'snippet': 'Senators of both parties challenged the collection of records of domestic phone calls, as the latest leak provided new details on the way the agency monitors Web browsing around the world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Senators of both parties on Wednesday sharply challenged the National Security Agency’s collection of records of all domestic phone calls, even as the latest leaked N.S.A. document provided new details on the way the agency monitors Web browsing around the world.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/01/us/01nsa_inline/01nsa_inline-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 386, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'im