', 'lastname': 'Sanger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/448cb122-1c7e-5e8b-bb21-98c9adcee23b', 'word_count': 1279, 'uri': 'nyt://article/448cb122-1c7e-5e8b-bb21-98c9adcee23b'}, {'abstract': 'An exiled politician is still calling the shots in Pakistan — from a middle-class neighborhood of London.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/31/karachi-upon-thames/', 'snippet': 'An exiled politician is still calling the shots in Pakistan — from a middle-class neighborhood of London.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — For many years Pakistanis have joked that their capital, Islamabad, is run from Washington. These days, however, it looks more like Karachi, Pakistan’s financial and business hub, is being run from London — thanks to an exiled Pakistani politician settled in the middle-class neighborhood of Edgware.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Karachi-upon-Thames', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hussain, Altaf', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Muttahida Qaumi Movement', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Karachi (Pakistan)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-31T14:59:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Huma Yusuf', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Huma', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yusuf', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e69229df-138e-54e5-aaff-d2eb2ee24f14', 'word_count': 611, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e69229df-138e-54e5-aaff-d2eb2ee24f14'}, {'abstract': 'The publishing company’s archive, containing manuscripts and correspondence from writers like David Foster Wallace, Rick Moody and Zadie Smith, is headed to the Harry Ransom Center at the University of Texas at Austin.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/31/mcsweeneys-archive-acquired-by-ransom-center-in-austin/', 'snippet': 'The publishing company’s archive, containing manuscripts and correspondence from writers like David Foster Wallace, Rick Moody and Zadie Smith, is headed to the Harry Ransom Center at the University of Texas at Austin.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While informal archives of McSweeney’s, the eclectic literary magazine created by Dave Eggers, can be found in living rooms, bedrooms and bathrooms of apartments across New York, a more proper collection of the assets of this prestigious journal and its sibling publications is headed to the Harry Ransom Center at the University of Texas at Austin.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/31/arts/mcsweeney/mcsweeney-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 741, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/31/arts/mcsweeney/mcsweeney-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 741}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/31/arts/mcsweeney/mcsweeney-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 830, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/31/arts/mcsweeney/mcsweeney-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1706, 'width': 1382, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/31/arts/mcsweeney/mcsweeney-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_