, an economist writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jared Bernstein is a senior fellow at the Center on Budget and Policy Priorities in Washington and a former chief economist to Vice President Joseph R. Biden Jr.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Consumer Spending and Growth: A Rejoinder', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Consumer Behavior', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Inequality', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Congressional Budget Office', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-31T15:00:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jared Bernstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jared', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bernstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3bc22543-014d-5ded-8d36-9f1173a95408', 'word_count': 896, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3bc22543-014d-5ded-8d36-9f1173a95408'}, {'abstract': 'Almost invisibly in her own day, Natalie de Blois, of Skidmore, Owings & Merrill, helped guide the design of three of the most important corporate landmarks of the 1950s and ’60s.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/01/nyregion/an-architect-whose-work-stood-out-even-if-she-didnt.html', 'snippet': 'Almost invisibly in her own day, Natalie de Blois, of Skidmore, Owings & Merrill, helped guide the design of three of the most important corporate landmarks of the 1950s and ’60s.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In architecture’s “Mad Men” era, there was a woman.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': '