or her song “Forcelessness,” Sarah Neufeld — best known as the violinist for the Grammy-winning indie collective Arcade Fire — wanted to go outside the bounds of indie rock. The clip for the track from her debut album, “Hero Brother,” which has its premiere here, pairs Neufeld’s hypnotic string arrangement with an intimate routine performed by two dancers in a brick-walled studio, a simple conceit that proves remarkably mesmerizing. It’s the debut project by Pressur.es, a new Brooklyn-based multidisciplinary arts organization and film series dedicated to exploring artistic modern dance and progressive music.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Listen Up | Violin Meets Modern Dance in a New Video From Arcade Fire’s Sarah Neufeld', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ARCADE FIRE', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-31T16:09:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By MATT DIEHL', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'DIEHL', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/594b4c8a-88e1-5110-a98a-cd2756ccdde4', 'word_count': 411, 'uri': 'nyt://article/594b4c8a-88e1-5110-a98a-cd2756ccdde4'}, {'abstract': 'We are in no position to say that he did. But are we really in a position to say that he didn’t?', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/31/did-zeus-exist/', 'snippet': 'We are in no position to say that he did. But are we really in a position to say that he didn’t?', 'lead_paragraph': 'When my children were little, they liked to play “Mother, May I?” At one point, I combined the game with an early introduction to classical culture, changing the key question to “Zeus, May I?” with an imaginary thunderbolt throwing back anyone forgetting to ask permission.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Did Zeus Exist?', 'kicker': 'The Stone', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greek Civilization', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Religion and Belief', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Socrates', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Greece', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-07-31T16:15:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gary Gutting', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gutting', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f91b3c36-04ab-5d19-961f-fb109aaab7ab', 'word_count': 1219, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f91b3c36-04ab-5d19-961f-fb109aaab7ab'}, {'abstract': 'Members of the literary family — which includes five novelists — leave curious references to one other’s characters and ideas in their books.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/07/31/an-easter-egg-hunt-with-stephen-king-and-family/', 'snippet': 'Members of the literary family — which includes five novelists — leave curious references to one other’s characters and ideas in their books.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Tabitha King, a writer married to Stephen King, wrote a novel about a woman from warmer climes who opens a diner in Maine, she lavished almost two pages of description on the man who taught the novel’s heroine how to cook. “Dick used to work summers in a resort in Colorado,” that character, named Pearl, recalls, in the novel that carries her name. “Summers in Key West, nobody came there back then, except diehard fishermen and drunks like Hemingway, maybe there’s no difference, huh?” Fans of “The Shining” might be surprised to find Dick Halloran, a hero of “The Shining,” showing up in a novel that is essentially a liberated woman’s love story, a novel without so much of a hint of the supernatural (they might also be surprised to learn that the brave and gentle Dick “had a taste for fast women, strippers and dancers and scat singers,” as he is further described in “Pearl”).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/31/magazine/31-king-family-books/31-king-family-books-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/31/magazine/31-king-family-books/31-king-family-books-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/31/magazine/31-king-family-books/31-king-family-books-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/31/magazine/31-king-family-books/31-king-family-books-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/31/magazine/31-king-family-books/31-king-family-books-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/07/31/magazine/31-king-family-books/31-king-family-books-thu